    /* The stored ModernizePanelPosition preference is NO LONGER read here (Nils 2026-08-12).
       It used to set peBarPosition on every activation, defaulting to xtpBarTop when nothing was
       stored - which is exactly how a freshly migrated DF26 workspace, carrying no user settings,
       put this bar under the toolbar instead of above the output tabs. The position is now a
       design-time constant above, so it is right on the first creation and cannot drift.
       CONSEQUENCE: Program Setup's 'Modernize panel position' control still reads and writes the
       setting (ProgramSetupFT.dg) but nothing consumes it any more. Retire that control, or teach
       this class to honour the preference again by re-docking an existing bar rather than only
       setting a property - a bar that is already docked does not move when the property changes. */
    Procedure Activating
        Forward Send Activating
    End_Procedure

    /* THE ACTION STRIP - Compile / Analyze / Analyze Source / Run Plan, framework buttons (cFTWebButton,
       RDCFlexTron) on a grid host of 8 px columns since 2026-09-30 (Nils: "Library setter first"), then the
       loop's tail and the busy marquee - markup, a readout - in column 58, which takes the rest of the width.
       The program combo that shared this bar is gone (branch guided-flow-redesign, Nils 2026-07-20): program
       selection lives in the Check Workspace view's single picker, which writes CS_LastCompileKeyPrefix - the
       key StartCompile reads.

       THE GUIDED SEQUENCE (Nils 2026-07-10; numbers retired 2026-07-13): the strip is the loop's steps -
       Compile -> Analyze -> Run Fix Plan. The 1-2-3 bubbles were dropped because a fixed numbering misleads
       once Deep Scan is in play (that path enters at Run Fix Plan, not step 1); the you-are-here points at the
       ACTUAL next step - exactly one button in the main action's amber (UpdatePrimary, RDCFlexTron 0.9.8; the
       iNext ladder in RefreshActionButtons) - and the tail after the buttons names where the loop stands
       ("repeat until clean" / "plan applied - recompile to verify" / the teal all-clear). The busy MARQUEE at
       the tail's right edge is an HTML/CSS block: it animates on WebView2's compositor even while the DF
       thread runs un-pumped. The three grid-TRIAGE buttons (Toggle Explain / Ignore Row(s) / Clear Ignored)
       live in the lower row, beside the analyze counts they act on.

       PROGRESSIVE ENABLE (Nils 2026-07-05): each button is live only when its pipeline stage has something to
       act on, so the strip teaches the compile -> analyze -> plan order and a click can no longer land in a
       silent guard; a shut button's tooltip says what would enable it. The gates MIRROR the actions' own
       guards, and each action keeps its guard as the safety net. RefreshActionButtons sets every button's
       state - caption, glyph, enabled, tooltip, the amber and Analyze Source's violet (UpdateClasses
       "tc-scan", testcompile.css) - stored for a page that loads and pushed to a page that is up, so it serves
       the un-shown dock too. Compile doubles as Stop (DoCompileToggle checks the live process) and SHOWS it:
       the button reads "Stop Compile" while a compile runs (Nils 2026-07-04 - the stop existed, but nothing
       revealed it). Each button's work runs in OnButtonClick, after the web click has returned (the library's
       click relay): the actions activate other docks and pop modals, which is what oButtonDeferTimer did for
       the chips. Spans fit the captions (Stop Compile ~114 px, Analyze ~84, Analyze Source ~124, Run Plan ~90,
       measured 2026-09-30).

       TWO OF THE FOUR ARE HIDDEN FOR NOW (Nils 2026-10-12: "I don't think that we ever use the buttons 'Analyze
       Source' and 'Run Plan' + the '-> run the fix...'. we might want to use them later, but for now how about
       make them invisible?", then "Always hide the controls"). CB_TestCompilePlanButtons
       (RefactorConstants.h.pkg) is False: RefreshActionButtons does not render the two buttons, the tail no
       longer points at the guided dashboard for the plan, and the tail stands right after Analyze (oTail_htm).
       Nothing else is taken out - the objects, their gates and their clicks are all here, and True brings the
       strip back as it was. One thing follows from it: the scan view no longer shuts Compile, because Run
       Plan, the way out that lock counted on, is not on the bar. */
    Object oWebActionBar is a cFTGridHost
        Set Location to 0 0
        Set Size to 22 519
        Set peAnchors to anTopLeftRight
        Set piColumnCount to 59         // Compile's 16, Analyze's 12, Analyze Source's 17, Run Plan's 13, then column 58
        Set psColumnWidths to "58/1fr"  // column 58 - the tail - takes the extra width

        /* IS THE GUIDED TRAIL THE BOARD IN USE? Two buttons on this bar behave differently when it is
           (Nils 2026-08-07: "when using the (WIP) dashboard and the Check button, perhaps Deep scan
           and Run Plan always should stay disabled?").

           piLastBoardView, not pbGuidedCheckRun or pbGuidedSurfaceUsed, because only it answers THIS
           question: pbGuidedCheckRun is a one-shot consumed by the post-compile handback, and
           pbGuidedSurfaceUsed is sticky-once-guided, so it would keep claiming the trail long after
           the user moved to the Manual board. piLastBoardView is stamped by each board's own
           Activate and flips when they switch - and its eBoardGuided default is right, since the
           trail is where a session starts.

           NOT an unconditional disable: this dock is shared with the manual and cockpit flows,
           where both chips are correct and needed. Defined above its callers (function-style calls
           resolve at compile time). */
        Function GuidedTrailInUse Returns Boolean
            Function_Return (piLastBoardView(ghoApplication) = eBoardGuided)
        End_Function

        Object oCompileBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piColumnIndex to 0
            Set piColumnSpan to 16
            Set psCaption to "Compile"
            // In the main action's amber: nothing is compiled yet, so the loop starts here.
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftbtn-primary ftg-compile"
            Set psToolTip to "(F5) Compile the selected program. Click again while compiling to Stop."

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send DoCompileToggle of oTestCompile_dg
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        Object oAnalyzeBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piColumnIndex to 16
            Set piColumnSpan to 12
            Set psCaption to "Analyze"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-analyze"
            Set pbEnabled to False
            Set psToolTip to "Run a compile first - Analyze reads the compiler's warning and error output."

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send StartAnalyze of oTestCompile_dg
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        // Analyze Source (Nils 2026-07-11): the SCAN loop's own re-scan point - the parallel path's door, not a
        // compile step. Alpha-gated with the rest of Deep Scan (CB_DeepScanEnabled). The scan worker (RunDeepScan:
        // scope confirm + panel + grid load) lives on the DASHBOARD view - the broadcast resolves it by name at
        // runtime; only oDashboard_vw handles it.
        Object oAnalyzeSourceBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piColumnIndex to 28
            Set piColumnSpan to 17
            Set psCaption to "Analyze Source"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-scan"
            Set pbEnabled to False
            Set psToolTip to "Analyze Source is the alternative discovery path - this bar is in the Compile path. The button lights once the compiler has nothing left to flag; a guided Check analyzes the source in the same pass."

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Broadcast Send RunDeepScan of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        Object oRunPlanBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piColumnIndex to 45
            Set piColumnSpan to 13
            Set psCaption to "Run Plan"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-plan"
            Set pbEnabled to False
            Set psToolTip to "Run Analyze first - the fix plan is built from the analyzed warnings and errors."

            /* THE SAME PLAN, RUN THE TRAIL'S WAY when the trail is the surface (Nils 2026-08-07). RunGuidedPlan is
               not a different action - it stamps pbGuidedPlanRun, calls the very same StartAutoSelect, then reads
               piEngineRunSeq to repaint the trail, retire the spent action and handle the empty-plan case. Firing
               StartAutoSelect straight from here skipped all of that: a plan ran and the trail did not know. The
               button is SHUT on the trail since 2026-08-09 (one action, one front door - the dashboard's; see
               RefreshActionButtons), and this route STAYS as the safety net behind that gate: if the button is
               ever lit on the trail again, the plan still cannot run without the trail finding out. */
            Procedure OnButtonClick
                If (GuidedTrailInUse(Parent(Self)) = True) ;
                    Broadcast Send RunGuidedPlan of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
                Else ;
                    Send StartAutoSelect of oTestCompile_dg
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        // The loop's tail and the busy marquee - a readout, so markup. RefreshActionButtons composes it.
        Object oTail_htm is a cFTWebHtmlBox
            Set piColumnIndex to 58
            Set piColumnSpan  to 1
            // DESIGN-TIME PLACEHOLDER for the Studio's Windows Designer, which models the LAST literal
            // psHtml it can parse: the tail of a bar with nothing compiled yet. The runtime content is set by
            // RefreshActionButtons, first from the panel's End_Construct_Object.
            Set psHtml to """
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="CssStyle/testcompile.css">
            <div class="tctailbar"><span class="tctail">
                <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                    <path d="M4 12v-3a3 3 0 0 1 3 -3h13 M17 3l3 3l-3 3 M20 12v3a3 3 0 0 1 -3 3h-13 M7 21l-3 -3l3 -3"/></svg>repeat until clean</span></div>
            """

            /* WHERE THE TAIL STANDS while Analyze Source and Run Plan are hidden (CB_TestCompilePlanButtons):
               right after Analyze, over the two buttons' columns and on to the end of the bar. A button that
               is not rendered keeps its columns, so at column 58 the tail would stand 240 px away from the
               last button. Here rather than in the object body, so the Designer keeps the literals above. */
            Procedure End_Construct_Object
                Forward Send End_Construct_Object
                If (CB_TestCompilePlanButtons = False) Begin
                    Set piColumnIndex to 28
                    Set piColumnSpan to 31
                End
            End_Procedure
        End_Object
    End_Object

    // Flip the busy marquee (the HTML/CSS block at the action strip's right edge - see
    // RefreshActionButtons) on/off and re-render the strip. Replaced the native Win32 oProgress_pb
    // (PBS_MARQUEE) 2026-07-10: that one animated off WM_TIMER, so it FROZE whenever the DF thread
    // ran un-pumped (the recurring "is it hung?" complaint - e.g. a customer plan-build freeze), and it
    // needed a dark-theme colour exception. The CSS animation runs on WebView2's own compositor, so
    // it glides through compile, analyze and plan build regardless of pumping; the DF-timer-driven
    // elapsed-seconds text (the result line / status bar) remains the honest is-it-alive signal. De-duped:
    // a same-state call skips the re-render (many FinishCompile branches stop an already-stopped
    // marquee). Callers on this object use plain Send; nested hosts go via (oTestCompile_dg(Self)).
    Procedure SetBusyMarquee Boolean bOn
        Boolean bCur
        Get pbBusyMarquee to bCur
        If (bCur = bOn) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set pbBusyMarquee to bOn
        Send SyncWebButtonStates
    End_Procedure

    // The lower row's state. The counts band stands in place of the result line while a tab's band is shown
    // (ShowAnalyzeSummary), and the three triage buttons show with it when the last counts push asked for them.
    Property Boolean pbSummaryShown False
    Property Boolean pbTriageWanted False
    // The result line: its text, and the class that colours it (SetResultText, SetResultColor).
    Property String psResultText ""
    Property String psResultClass ""

    /* THE LOWER ROW, ONE HOST (Nils 2026-10-12: the three triage buttons "all moves left to right when the
       program is enlarged. Then there is a gap and two more buttons appear", and that gap, "of about 2 cm before
       the Clear and Close buttons", costs room "when the program is narrowed down"). Until then the row was
       three things side by side and on top of each other: a Windows form for the compile result text, a host
       with the counts and the triage buttons that covered the form on the Analyzer tab, and a second host with
       Clear and Close. Two hosts on one row have to agree on a boundary in dialog units while what must fit is
       measured in pixels, and nobody can say how many pixels a unit is on a given machine - so the second host
       was made wide enough to be safe, and its spare width showed as that gap. One host has no such boundary:
       every distance in it is pixels.

       ITS COLUMNS: column 0 takes the extra width (minmax(0,1fr)) and holds the READOUT; then Toggle Explain's
       17 columns, Ignore Row(s)' 16 and Clear Ignored's 20; one empty column, so the two groups read as two;
       then Clear's 11 and Close's 11. The five buttons stand together at the right end at any width, and it
       is the readout that gives way when the bar is narrow. Spans fit the captions (Toggle Explain ~119 px,
       Ignore Row(s) ~116, Clear Ignored (999) ~145, Clear ~70, Close ~72, measured 2026-09-30).

       THE READOUT IS ONE OF TWO BOXES, never both (ShowAnalyzeSummary sets which one is rendered):
       - the RESULT LINE (oResult_htm) - "Compiling ...", "Success: ...", "Failed: ..." - on the Output tab and
         while a compile runs. No triage button is rendered then, so the line runs over their columns as well,
         up to Clear. It was the form; as markup it is cut off with an ellipsis when it is too long, and the
         whole text is its tooltip.
       - the COUNTS (oAnalyzeSummary_htm), pushed as markup by the Analyzer grid (ModAnalyzerFT) or the
         Compiler tab's totals band (CompilerOutputFT) through SetAnalyzeSummaryHtml, in column 0 alone.
       The triage buttons act on the analyzer grid, so they show with the ANALYZER's pushes only
       (SetTriageButtons); they moved down from the top strip 2026-07-10, so that strip reads as the pure
       Compile -> Analyze -> Run Fix Plan sequence.

       CLEAR AND CLOSE both tear down - Clear closes the two bottom output areas and forgets what they held,
       Close hides THIS dock - so both run in OnButtonClick, after the web click has returned (the library's
       click relay). On Close the busy state stays HONEST: a still-running compile keeps it True, so a reopen
       shows the marquee again until FinishCompile clears it.

       IN THE DESIGNER the row shows as on the Analyzer tab: the counts sample and all five buttons. The
       program starts it as on the Output tab (End_Construct_Object: ShowAnalyzeSummary False). */
    Object oLowerRow is a cFTGridHost
        Set Location to 22 9     // the lower row: 24 units, an FT button's row, since the dock grew (2026-09-30)
        Set Size to 24 516       // from the left inset to where the Clear / Close host ended (401 + 124)
        Set peAnchors to anTopLeftRight
        Set piColumnCount to 77         // the readout's column 0, the triage buttons' 53, one empty, Clear's 11 and Close's 11
        Set psColumnWidths to "0/minmax(0,1fr)"     // the readout takes the extra width, so the buttons stay at the right end

        Object oAnalyzeSummary_htm is a cFTWebHtmlBox
            Set piColumnSpan to 1
            // The counts are a readout - nothing in them is clickable.
            Set pbServerOnClick to False
            // DESIGN-TIME PLACEHOLDER for the Studio's Windows Designer, which models the LAST literal
            // psHtml it can parse. A SAMPLE, not the runtime seed (Nils 2026-09-29): the seed is a plain dark
            // bar, so nothing flashes before the first analyze push, and the Designer showed it as a black
            // area. This is the band an analyze pushes instead - the analyzer grid's SummaryHtml with made-up
            // counts. At the design width it is cut off on the right, as a narrow bar cuts it at runtime.
            // Tools\RegenerateDesignPlaceholders.py skips this box (SKIP_BOX): edit it by hand. The runtime
            // expression sits in End_Construct_Object below.
            Set psHtml to """
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="RDCFlexTron/palette.css">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="RDCFlexTron/buttons.css">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="RDCFlexTron/bars.css">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="CssStyle/modanalyzer.css">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="RDCFlexTron/pills.css">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="CssStyle/testcompile.css">
            <div class="tcsum"><div class="sbar"><span class="lbl">64 rows</span><span class="sep">|</span><span class="pill ok">38 auto-fix</span><span class="pill review">12 review</span><span class="pill mute">14
                explain</span><span class="pill mute">2 ignored</span><span class="sep">|</span><span class="pill warn">61 warnings</span><span class="pill err">3 errors</span></div></div>
            """

            // RUNTIME content - here rather than in the object body, so the Designer keeps the literal above.
            Procedure End_Construct_Object
                Forward Send End_Construct_Object
                Set psHtml to (CS_PaletteCssLink + (CssLink("testcompile")) + (DivOpen("tc-fill")) + CS_DivEnd)
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        // The result line. Not rendered in the Designer's picture of the row (the story above); the program
        // renders it from its first moment, and PushResultLine composes it.
        Object oResult_htm is a cFTWebHtmlBox
            Set piColumnIndex to 0
            Set piColumnSpan to 55      // column 0, the triage buttons' 53 and the empty one: up to Clear
            // A readout - nothing in it is clickable.
            Set pbServerOnClick to False
            Set pbRender to False
        End_Object

        // Toggle Explain just refilters the already-realized analyzer grid, which re-pushes the counts.
        Object oToggleExplainBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piColumnIndex to 1
            Set piColumnSpan to 17
            Set psCaption to "Toggle Explain"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-toggle"
            Set pbEnabled to False
            Set psToolTip to "Run Analyze first - this then shows or hides the Explain and Unknown rows in the analyze grid."

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                If (ghoModAnalyzerDock <> 0) ;
                    Send ToggleInformational of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        // Ignore and Clear Ignored reload the grid, and can pop a YesNo - after the click.
        Object oIgnoreBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piColumnIndex to 18
            Set piColumnSpan to 16
            Set psCaption to "Ignore Row(s)"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-close"
            Set pbEnabled to False
            Set psToolTip to "Run Analyze first - then select the grid row(s) to dismiss."

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                If (ghoModAnalyzerDock <> 0) ;
                    Send IgnoreSelectedRow of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        Object oClearIgnoredBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piColumnIndex to 34
            Set piColumnSpan to 20              // wide enough for "Clear Ignored (999)"
            Set psCaption to "Clear Ignored"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-eraser"
            Set pbEnabled to False
            Set psToolTip to "Run Analyze first - rows dismissed with Ignore can then be brought back here."

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                If (ghoModAnalyzerDock <> 0) ;
                    Send ClearIgnored of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        Object oClearBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piColumnIndex to 55
            Set piColumnSpan to 11
            Set psCaption to "Clear"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-eraser"
            Set psToolTip to "Clear - close the Compiler Output + Modernization Analyzer docks."

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send Clear of oTestCompile_dg
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        Object oCloseBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piColumnIndex to 66
            Set piColumnSpan to 11
            Set psCaption to "Close"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-close"
            Set psToolTip to "Close this Test Compile bar."

            // A user close: hide the dock, clear the user-wants flag (no auto-restore), and land on the
            // dashboard cockpit, whose Activating repaints Last-analyze.
            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send CloseTestCompileDialog
                Send ModernizeCloseByUser of oTestCompile_dg
                Send Activate_oDashboard_vw
            End_Procedure
        End_Object
    End_Object

    /* One framework button's state: shown, enabled, the main action's amber, caption, glyph, tooltip and its own
       classes - through the library's setters (RDCFlexTron 0.9.13: UpdateRender, UpdateEnabled, UpdateCaption,
       UpdateToolTip; 0.9.8: UpdateGlyph, UpdatePrimary, UpdateClasses), each stored for a page that loads and
       pushed to a page that is up, only when it changed. "" keeps the button's caption or glyph. */
    Procedure BarButtonState Handle hoBtn Boolean bShow Boolean bEnabled Boolean bNext String sCaption String sGlyph String sTip String sClasses
        Send UpdateRender  of hoBtn bShow
        Send UpdateEnabled of hoBtn bEnabled
        If (sCaption <> "") ;
            Send UpdateCaption of hoBtn sCaption
        Send UpdateToolTip of hoBtn sTip
        If ((sGlyph <> "") and (psGlyph(hoBtn) <> sGlyph)) ;
            Send UpdateGlyph of hoBtn sGlyph
        Send UpdatePrimary of hoBtn bNext
        Send UpdateClasses of hoBtn sClasses
    End_Procedure

    /* The three grid-TRIAGE buttons - Toggle Explain / Ignore Row(s) / Clear Ignored (N) - shown with the
       ANALYZER's pushes of the counts, hidden (pbRender) with the Compiler tab's (Nils 2026-07-10: they were
       showing on the Compiler tab too). Freshness needs no extra hook: every event that changes a gate
       (analyze, Ignore/Clear refilter, Ctrl+I, saved view) funnels through the grid's DisplayFiltered, which
       re-pushes the counts. The gates: pbAnalyzeDataReady lights Toggle Explain, +visible rows lights Ignore,
       +ignored rows lights Clear Ignored - which carries the live count, so the user sees there is something to
       bring back (at 0 the plain "Clear Ignored", no "(0)" noise). */
    Procedure SetTriageButtons Boolean bShow
        String sTip sIgnored
        Integer iIgnored iVisible
        Boolean bGridReady
        Handle hoHost

        // What the last counts push asked for is remembered; the buttons show only while the counts band does
        // (ShowAnalyzeSummary) - on the Output tab the result line runs over their columns.
        Set pbTriageWanted to bShow
        Move (bShow and pbSummaryShown(Self)) to bShow
        Move (oLowerRow(Self)) to hoHost
        Move False to bGridReady
        Move 0 to iIgnored
        Move 0 to iVisible
        If (ghoModAnalyzerDock <> 0) Begin
            Get pbAnalyzeDataReady of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to bGridReady
            Get piSumVisible       of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to iVisible
            Get piSumIgnored       of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to iIgnored
        End
        Move "" to sIgnored
        If (iIgnored > 0) ;
            Move (" (" + String(iIgnored) + ")") to sIgnored
        If (bGridReady = True) ;
            Move "(Ctrl+I) Show or hide the Explain and Unknown rows in the analyze grid." to sTip
        Else ;
            Move "Run Analyze first - this then shows or hides the Explain and Unknown rows in the analyze grid." to sTip
        Send BarButtonState (oToggleExplainBtn(hoHost)) bShow bGridReady False "" "" sTip ""
        If (bGridReady = False) ;
            Move "Run Analyze first - then select the grid row(s) to dismiss." to sTip
        Else If (iVisible = 0) ;
            Move "Nothing to ignore - the grid shows no rows (any warnings are in files outside the workspace). Press Toggle Explain (Ctrl+I) to reveal read-only rows if you want to dismiss those." to sTip
        Else ;
            Move "Hide the selected row(s) and all their occurrences from the grid and the refactor plan, on every future analyze of this workspace (no source changed; Clear Ignored reverses it). Best for report-only rows like Type-to-Struct that you will not act on." to sTip
        Send BarButtonState (oIgnoreBtn(hoHost)) bShow (bGridReady and (iVisible > 0)) False "" "" sTip ""
        If (iIgnored > 0) ;
            Move "Bring back every row you have dismissed with Ignore for this workspace." to sTip
        Else If (bGridReady = True) ;
            Move "Nothing to bring back - no rows are dismissed with Ignore for this workspace." to sTip
        Else ;
            Move "Run Analyze first - rows dismissed with Ignore can then be brought back here." to sTip
        Send BarButtonState (oClearIgnoredBtn(hoHost)) bShow (bGridReady and (iIgnored > 0)) False ("Clear Ignored" + sIgnored) "" sTip ""
    End_Procedure

    /* Push a counts band into the lower row (it overlays the result text while a tab band is shown). TWO
       cross-dock callers share it: the ANALYZER grid (PushSummaryHtml, bWithTriage True) and the COMPILER tab's
       totals band (RefreshCompileSummaryChips, bWithTriage False) - the triage buttons act on the analyzer grid,
       so they show with the analyzer's pushes only. The band is wrapped in .tcsum (testcompile.css), 25 px tall,
       so its counts centre on the buttons' line. Stored for a page that loads and pushed to a page that is up:
       a push before the bar was ever shown is kept, not lost. */
    Procedure SetAnalyzeSummaryHtml String sHtml Boolean bWithTriage
        Handle hoHost
        Move (oLowerRow(Self)) to hoHost
        Send UpdateHtml of (oAnalyzeSummary_htm(hoHost)) ((CssLink("testcompile")) + (DivOpen("tcsum")) + sHtml + CS_DivEnd)
        Send SetTriageButtons bWithTriage
        If (not(pbInitWebApp(hoHost))) ;
            Send CheckUpdateWebUI of hoHost
    End_Procedure

    // Swap the lower row's readout between the counts band (with the triage buttons) and the compile result
    // line. bShow True (the Analyzer tab is up, or the Compiler tab has totals) renders the counts and takes away
    // the now-stale "Failed: ..." line; False renders the result line (Output tab / compiling). Driven by
    // OutputDockFT.ShowTab. Both are boxes of the one host, so this sets what is RENDERED - until 2026-10-12 it
    // hid a host and showed a Windows form.
    Procedure ShowAnalyzeSummary Boolean bShow
        Handle hoHost
        Move (oLowerRow(Self)) to hoHost
        Set pbSummaryShown to bShow
        Send BoxRender (oResult_htm(hoHost)) (not(bShow))
        Send BoxRender (oAnalyzeSummary_htm(hoHost)) bShow
        Send SetTriageButtons (pbTriageWanted(Self))
        If (FTHostPageIsUp(hoHost)) ;
            Send CheckUpdateWebUI of hoHost
    End_Procedure

    // Whether a markup box is rendered. The library has this setter for its buttons (UpdateRender), not for a
    // box: stored always, pushed only to a page that is up (FTHostPageIsUp), and only when it changed.
    Procedure BoxRender Handle hoBox Boolean bRender
        Handle hoHost
        If (pbRender(hoBox) = bRender) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set pbRender of hoBox to bRender
        Get Owner of hoBox to hoHost
        If (FTHostPageIsUp(hoHost)) ;
            WebSet pbRender of hoBox to bRender
    End_Procedure

    /* THE RESULT LINE - what the Windows form oResult_fm showed until 2026-10-12. SetResultText and
       SetResultColor stand where "Set Value of" and "Set TextColor of" that form stood, one for one, so every
       caller reads as before. Each composes the line again: stored for a page that loads, pushed to a page that
       is up. The colour is a class, so it follows the theme: the form's clRed is the palette's error red,
       clGreen its mint, the amber of "compiled but not linked" its amber, and anything else the ink. */
    Procedure SetResultText String sText
        Set psResultText to sText
        Send PushResultLine
    End_Procedure

    Procedure SetResultColor Integer iColor
        String sClass
        Move "" to sClass
        If (iColor = clRed) ;
            Move "tc-err" to sClass
        Else If (iColor = clGreen) ;
            Move "tc-ok" to sClass
        Else If (iColor = RGB(224, 166, 77)) ;
            Move "tc-warn" to sClass
        Set psResultClass to sClass
        Send PushResultLine
    End_Procedure

    Procedure PushResultLine
        String sText sClass
        Handle hoHost
        Get psResultText to sText
        Get psResultClass to sClass
        If (sClass <> "") ;
            Move ("tcres " + sClass) to sClass
        Else ;
            Move "tcres" to sClass
        Move (oLowerRow(Self)) to hoHost
        // The whole text is the tooltip as well: the line is cut off when the bar is narrow.
        Send UpdateHtml of (oResult_htm(hoHost)) ((CssLink("testcompile")) + (TagTipText("div", sClass, sText, sText)))
        If (FTHostPageIsUp(hoHost)) ;
            Send CheckUpdateWebUI of hoHost
    End_Procedure

    // Clear the result/details area between invocations.
    Procedure Clear
        Send SetResultText ""
        Send SetBusyMarquee False
        Send SetErrFile ""
        Send CloseCompileErrorDialog of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
        // Hiding the dock keeps what it shows; Clear is what forgets it - the compile's output here, the
        // analysis below.
        Send ForgetCompileOutput of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
        Send CloseModAnalyzer        of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
        /* THE ANALYSIS GOES TOO (Nils 2026-09-30: Clear "clears the Output grid, but not the Analyzer grid").
           CloseModAnalyzer only forgets which listing was analyzed; the grid kept its rows and its ready latch,
           so the Analyzer tab showed them again. ResetAnalyzerData drops the rows, counts and caches together.
           The dashboard's findings board renders from the same grid, so the broadcast resets its counters and
           tick stamps with it (DashboardFT's AnalysisCleared): it says "not analyzed" rather than presenting
           the emptied grid as a Check that found nothing. */
        If (ghoModAnalyzerDock <> 0) ;
            Send ResetAnalyzerData of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock))
        Broadcast Send AnalysisCleared of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
        Send SyncWebButtonStates
    End_Procedure

    Object oCompileProcess is a cRDCAsyncProcess
    End_Object

    Object oCompilePoll_tm is a cTimer
        Set piTimeout to 100
        Set pbEnabled to False

        // Fires every 100 ms while a compile is in flight. Detects process
        // exit (-> FinishCompile) and otherwise hands off to the dialog's
        // UpdateCompileProgress, which sub-samples expensive per-tick work
        // (elapsed-time text every ~1 s, log-file tail every ~500 ms).
        Procedure OnTimer
            Boolean bRunning bFinalizing

            // Post-compile FINALIZE phase: the compile verdict is already shown, and the timer keeps
            // running only to finish consuming the .prn tail off the UI thread (no pumping, no freeze,
            // live "Finalizing..." status). Must be tested FIRST - the process is long gone, so the
            // bRunning branch below would re-enter FinishCompile.
            Get pbPrnFinalizing of (Parent(Self)) to bFinalizing
            If (bFinalizing = True) Begin
                Delegate Send PrnFinalizeTick
                Procedure_Return
            End
            Get IsProcessRunning of oCompileProcess to bRunning
            If (bRunning = False) Begin
                Set pbEnabled to False
                Delegate Send FinishCompile
                // Re-sync the chip gates AFTER the post-mortem: FinishCompile is what resolves/clears
                // psErrFilePath + psPrnFilePath (the Analyze gate), and its own early RefreshActionButtons
                // (the Stop->Compile morph near its top) runs BEFORE those paths are known. One call here
                // covers every terminal branch - success, warnings-only, failure, cancel, precompile - and
                // the df-cli auto-retry (StartCompile relaunched: pbActive reads True, so the strip renders
                // the compiling state again, correctly).
                Delegate Send SyncWebButtonStates
                Procedure_Return
            End
            Delegate Send UpdateCompileProgress
        End_Procedure

    End_Object

    // FT: action deferral for the flows that start from inside other work - an auto-analyze (the post-compile
    // and guided-Check paths), the Deep Scan / Tidy Up / name-collision offers' Yes and the post-plan verify
    // re-scan: an action that activates/realizes another dock, pops a modal or closes a panel must run off the
    // current stack or it faults (invalid handle, err 4410). Each arms this 1-ms one-shot with its action code
    // (ArmAction, cFTDeferTimer); OnDeferredAction runs it natively (as DashboardFT.vw's oChkDeferTimer). The bar's buttons armed it
    // too until 2026-09-30 - they are framework buttons now, whose OnButtonClick runs after the click.
    Object oButtonDeferTimer is a cFTDeferTimer
        Procedure OnDeferredAction Integer iAction String sParam
            If (iAction = CI_TCDeferAnalyze) ;
                Delegate Send StartAnalyze
            // Deep Scan - the Deep Scan offer's Yes (MaybeSuggestDeepScan); the bar's Analyze Source button
            // sends the same broadcast from its OnButtonClick. The scan worker
            // (RunDeepScan: scope confirm + panel + grid load) lives on the DASHBOARD view -
            // broadcast resolves it by name at runtime, the same crossing RefreshAnalyze uses;
            // only oDashboard_vw handles it.
            If (iAction = CI_TCDeferDeepScan) ;
                Broadcast Send RunDeepScan of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
            // Post-plan AUTO verify re-scan (Nils 2026-07-16): armed by StartAutoSelect after a LIVE
            // scan-loop run applied. Same broadcast crossing as CI_TCDeferDeepScan, but through
            // RunDeepScanVerify - which replays the confirmed scan scope and skips the scope confirm.
            If (iAction = CI_TCDeferVerifyScan) ;
                Broadcast Send RunDeepScanVerify of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
            // Campaign-exit Tidy Up Yes (Nils 2026-07-19): the dashboard Tidy Up tile's own
            // CI_DashFunctions sequence - run HERE on a fresh tick, NOT inline in
            // MaybeSuggestTidyUp. The offer pops from deep inside RunDeepScan's tail (or the
            // analyze tail), where the analyzer dock's WebView2 work is still settling; the
            // inline activation left the Functions view's Run-button box rendering "Unhandled
            // Program Error on the Client" (LegacyApp2 live test). Same cure as every other action
            // on this timer. Keep the sequence in lockstep with DashboardFT's oChkDeferTimer
            // branch; the view is addressed through Client_Id (+ the Register_Object at the
            // top of this file) - a bare oSelectFunctionsView only resolves for its
            // Client_Area siblings.
            If (iAction = CI_TCDeferTidyUp) Begin
                Set pbWorkspaceMode of ghoApplication to True
                Send Activate_oSelectFoldersView of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
                Send Activate_oSelectFunctionsView of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
                Send SetFnGroupFilter of (oSelectFunctionsView(Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))) eFnGroupCleanup
                Send Switch_Next_View of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
            End
            // Name-collision nudge Yes (Nils 2026-07-20): the same guided view flow as Tidy Up, but with
            // the return-mismatch report instead of a group filter - the user
            // lands on Folders, confirms the source folders, and Runs the report. Deferred here off the
            // deep StartAnalyze tail for the same reason as Tidy Up (the "Unhandled Program Error on the
            // Client" mid-mount lesson).
            If (iAction = CI_TCDeferNameCollision) Begin
                // JUST RUN THE REPORT. This used to re-seed the folder grid (wiping a curated scope), open
                // the ~95-function picker with ONE function pre-ticked, and switch views - leaving the user
                // to find that function and press Run, on a screen whose Run button was disabled anyway
                // because the pre-tick did not reach its selected-count. Nils, 2026-07-21: "it is still
                // very difficult to find the report function. Why show this when it is the
                // only function to run anyway?" - the exact anti-pattern this redesign exists to remove.
                // The Check Workspace view already knows how to run one function over the right files, so
                // hand it over: it selects the function, runs it, restores the user's own selection, and
                // opens the report. No view detour, no picker, nothing for the user to hunt for.
                Broadcast Send RunReturnMismatchReport of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
            End
        End_Procedure
    End_Object

    // FT: one-shot to re-render this dock's hosted web controls a tick after it (re)activates. OnUpdate arms
    // it; a synchronous WebSet in OnUpdate itself faults 4402 (web property system not ready) and the client
    // control may not be realized yet, so we defer one tick - by then it's safe. KEY: a cLocalWebControlHost
    // flushes pending WebSets to WebView2 only while its HOST CONTROL is ACTIVE - the flush idle handler is
    // enabled in Activating, disabled in Deactivating, and Activating fires on the HOST getting focus, NOT on
    // the parent dock. So this re-sync only paints when the host is active. Two triggers arm it:
    //   - OnUpdate on a (re)activation - Modernize, or a switch-views-and-back - re-syncs the buttons + the
    //     Select-Program combo. Fixes the combo showing the PREVIOUS workspace after a switch.
    //   - The post-compile path: oCompilePoll_tm activates the host (oWebActionBar) directly when a compile
    //     finishes (the live-output dock had stolen focus, leaving this host inactive so the Analyze WebSet
    //     never flushed). That fires the host's Activating -> the idle flush re-enables and the buttons paint.
    //     See [[project_webcontrol_swap]] / [[reference_df25_web_combo_controls]] (the host-must-be-active rule).
    Object oSyncDeferTimer is a cFTDeferTimer
        Procedure OnDeferredAction Integer iAction String sParam
            Delegate Send SyncWebButtonStates
            Delegate Send PrepareForShow
        End_Procedure
    End_Object

    // FlexTron-only dark tint (FlexTron FT copy; production uses the un-suffixed file). The dark CodeJock theme
    // skins the chrome but NOT the native control client areas, so once this panel's subtree is built,
    // cascade clDarkWebBg/clDarkThemeText down it (same pattern as SelectFoldersView.vw / oRefactorPlanDialog.dg,
    // scoped to Self). clDarkWebBg (var(--dfr-bg-page, #262624)) is the hosted web bars' backdrop, so the panel + its native
    // controls read as ONE surface with the web bars (cLocalWebControlHost has no per-host CSS hook to do the
    // reverse). (The old post-cascade colour exception for the native marquee went with oProgress_pb -
    // the web marquee styles itself.)
    Procedure End_Construct_Object
        Forward Send End_Construct_Object
        Broadcast Recursive Set Color     of Self to (FTWebBg())
        Broadcast Recursive Set TextColor of Self to (FTThemeInk())
        // The strip's first state - what the chips' psHtml seed used to be - stored for the page that loads.
        Send RefreshActionButtons False
        // The lower row's: the result line, empty, and neither counts nor triage buttons.
        Send PushResultLine
        Send ShowAnalyzeSummary False
    End_Procedure

    // *** Functions/Procedures ***

    Procedure SetErrFile String sPath
        Set psErrFilePath to sPath
    End_Procedure

    // The Compile/Stop web button's action, factored out so BOTH the deferral timer (web click, run
    // off the WebView2 pump) and the F5 On_Key (native) invoke the same logic. While a compile runs
    // the button shows Stop and this kills it; otherwise it starts a fresh compile.
    Procedure DoCompileToggle
        Boolean bActive

        Get pbActive of oCompileProcess to bActive
        If (bActive = True) Begin
            Send KillCompile
        End
        Else Begin
            // Fresh user-initiated compile: reset the df-cli auto-retry budget (the retry in
            // FinishCompile does NOT reset it, so an auto-retry caps at one extra attempt).
            Set piCompileRetries to 0
            Set pbManifestFixTried to False     // a fresh click may offer the manifest auto-fix again
            Set pbManifestSkipTried to False    // ...and the build-without-manifest escape again
            Set pbLinkImageFixTried to False    // ...and the missing-linked-image fix again
            Set psLastPrecompiledPkg to ""      // fresh attempt: the same package may legitimately 4522 again
            Send StartCompile
        End
    End_Procedure

    // The tail box's last markup, so an unchanged tail is not pushed again.
    Property String psTailHtml ""

    /* Set the strip to the LIVE pipeline state: Compile <-> Stop, the progressive-enable gates, the amber next
       step, Analyze Source's violet, and the tail + busy marquee - each button through BarButtonState (only a
       change is applied; stored for a page that loads and pushed to a page that is up), the tail as markup. Sent
       by StartCompile, FinishCompile and the precompile, by SyncWebButtonStates from everywhere else, and once
       from the panel's End_Construct_Object. A dock (re)activation re-syncs via oSyncDeferTimer (OnUpdate).

       HISTORY: dynamic enable/disable was DISABLED 2026-06-17 because the buttons were then native cWebButtons
       whose pbEnabled WebSet only painted while the host was the ACTIVE control - the live-output dock steals
       focus after a compile. The strip became ONE cWebHtmlBox 2026-07-05, the enabled look baked into each
       render. Framework buttons again since 2026-09-30: every change is flushed with CheckUpdateWebUI, which
       paints whether or not the host has focus (the Editor view's bar has done so since that morning), and
       each action keeps its own guard as the safety net. See [[project_webcontrol_swap]]. */
    Procedure RefreshActionButtons Boolean bCompiling
        String sErr sPrn sTip sStamp sTail sHtml
        Integer iActionable iSug iNext
        Boolean bHaveOutput bGridReady bBusy bAnalyzedNow bPostRun bDSMode bScanPlanRan bScanExhausted
        Boolean bGuidedTrail bEnabled
        tAnalyzeSnapshot Snap
        Handle hoBar hoTail

        Move (oWebActionBar(Self)) to hoBar
        Move False to bGridReady
        Move False to bDSMode
        Move (GuidedTrailInUse(hoBar)) to bGuidedTrail
        If (ghoModAnalyzerDock <> 0) Begin
            Get pbAnalyzeDataReady of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to bGridReady
            // iActionable = auto-fix + review rows (0 -> nothing to Run in a fix plan) - gates Run Plan.
            Get piSumAuto          of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to iActionable
            Get piSumSuggest       of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to iSug
            Move (iActionable + iSug) to iActionable
            // DEEP-SCAN lockdown (Nils 2026-07-10): while the grid shows Deep Scan results, Compile is
            // SHUT - compiling would replace the scan view with compiler output. Exits: Run Fix Plan
            // (StartAutoSelect clears the mode after the run) or Analyze (a real analyze replaces the
            // view and clears the mode via ActivateModAnalyzer).
            Get pbDeepScanView     of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to bDSMode
        End
        // A Run Fix Plan applied inside the scan loop -> Compile lights to verify (see pbScanPlanRan).
        Get pbScanPlanRan to bScanPlanRan
        // SCAN LOOP EXHAUSTED (Nils 2026-07-16): the grid shows a Deep Scan result with NOTHING a fix plan
        // can act on (no auto-fix / review rows - empty or Explain-only), and no plan run is awaiting its
        // verify re-scan. The loop is DONE here: the lockdown below lifts (Compile re-enables - the grid's
        // empty-state text already says "Next: Compile + Analyze", and the buttons match it) and the re-scan
        // button shuts (a re-scan would find the same nothing; after source changes a NEW scan still starts
        // from the dashboard).
        Move (bDSMode = True and bScanPlanRan = False and iActionable = 0) to bScanExhausted
        // The busy marquee state (compile / analyze / plan build) - see SetBusyMarquee.
        Get pbBusyMarquee to bBusy
        // The Analyze gate: the same .err (failed build) OR .prn (clean build with warnings) check
        // StartAnalyze guards on - both paths resolved by FinishCompile. Shut while a compile runs: the
        // output on disk is the one being (re)written.
        Get psErrFilePath to sErr
        Get psPrnFilePath to sPrn
        Move (sErr <> "" or sPrn <> "") to bHaveOutput
        // Publish to the app-wide flag so the dashboard guided tile can gate its Analyze step the same
        // way this bar does - the dashboard can't reach these panel-local paths. (Nils 2026-07-12.)
        Move bHaveOutput to ghbHaveCompileOutput
        // WHICH button is the loop's NEXT action - the amber you-are-here, mirroring the dashboard cycle
        // strip (Nils 2026-07-10 second pass). This bar has FRESHER local signals than the dashboard, so they
        // outrank the persisted ones: pbAnalyzed (reset by StartCompile) knows whether THIS compile's output
        // has been analyzed yet, where the dashboard only knows the last persisted analyze. The plan-run
        // stamp (engine WritePlanRunStamp vs the snapshot's analyze date - ISO strings, plain compare)
        // supplies the "loop wrapped" case: a plan run newer than the analyze relights Compile until the
        // next analyze supersedes it.
        Get pbAnalyzed to bAnalyzedNow
        Get ReadPlanRunStamp    of ghoApplication to sStamp
        Get ReadAnalyzeSnapshot of ghoApplication to Snap
        // Cold-open guard (Nils 2026-07-14): the stamp + snapshot are PERSISTED per workspace, so on a fresh
        // program start (nothing analyzed this session) a PRIOR session's "plan applied" stamp would wrongly
        // relight Compile and show the "recompile to verify" tail. Gate on bAnalyzedNow (pbAnalyzed, reset by
        // StartCompile) so the loop-wrap nudge only appears once THIS session has reached an analyze.
        Move (Snap.bFound and (sStamp <> "") and (sStamp > Snap.sDate) and ((Snap.iAuto + Snap.iSuggest) > 0) and bAnalyzedNow) to bPostRun
        Move 0 to iNext
        If (bCompiling = True) ;
            Move 1 to iNext                    // step 1 is RUNNING (the button shows Stop Compile)
        Else If (bHaveOutput = True and bAnalyzedNow = False) ;
            Move 2 to iNext                    // fresh compile output awaiting its Analyze
        Else If (bPostRun = True) ;
            Move 1 to iNext                    // the plan ran after the last analyze -> recompile
        Else If (bGridReady = True and iActionable > 0) ;
            Move 3 to iNext                    // triaged actionable rows -> run the fix plan
        Else If (bGridReady = True) ;
            Move 0 to iNext                    // analyzed clean: no next step on this surface
        Else ;
            Move 1 to iNext                    // nothing yet: the loop starts with a compile
        // Deep-scan view: while the loop still has actionable rows, Compile is locked (below) so the ladder
        // points at Run Fix Plan. Once the scan comes back with nothing actionable the loop is EXHAUSTED
        // (bScanExhausted): the lockdown lifts and the ladder hands over to step 1 (Compile) - the compile
        // loop is the sanctioned next step (Nils 2026-07-16).
        If (bDSMode = True and bCompiling = False) Begin
            If (bGridReady = True and iActionable > 0) ;
                Move 3 to iNext
            Else ;
                Move 1 to iNext
        End
        // Post scan-plan-run (Nils 2026-07-15): a Run Fix Plan applied inside the scan loop, so the scan grid
        // is stale - point the you-are-here at Compile (verify the fixes), the mirror of the compile path's
        // post-run recompile. Re-scan stays available (Analyze Source is still lit below).
        If (bDSMode = True and bScanPlanRan = True and bCompiling = False) ;
            Move 1 to iNext

        // COMPILE - the pipeline's step one, ALWAYS live but in the scan lockdown, and "Stop Compile" while a
        // compile runs (the same OnButtonClick: DoCompileToggle kills or starts). Works for EVERY compiler - the
        // kill is a taskkill /F /T on the cmd wrapper's process TREE (cRDCAsyncProcess.KillProcess), so the
        // synchronous DfCompConsole dies with it (Nils 2026-07-04). The lockdown holds WHILE scanning (Deep Scan
        // <-> Run Fix Plan), but NOT after a plan actually applied - then Compile lights to verify the fixes
        // (bScanPlanRan, Nils 2026-07-15) - and NOT once the scan loop is EXHAUSTED (bScanExhausted, Nils
        // 2026-07-16): nothing actionable is left to protect, and Compile IS the promised next step.
        If (bCompiling = True) ;
            Send BarButtonState (oCompileBtn(hoBar)) True True True "Stop Compile" CS_GlyphClose "(F5) Stop the running compile: the compiler process is terminated. A partial listing/.err is discarded." ""
        // ...and only while Run Plan is on the bar (CB_TestCompilePlanButtons): the lock sends the user to that
        // button, and a bar without it would leave Compile shut with no way on.
        Else If (CB_TestCompilePlanButtons = True and bDSMode = True and bScanPlanRan = False and bScanExhausted = False) ;
            Send BarButtonState (oCompileBtn(hoBar)) True False False "Compile" CS_GlyphCompile "The bar is in the source loop (Analyze Source / Run Fix Plan) - compiling would replace the source findings with compiler output. Run a fix plan (then Compile lights to verify), or return via Analyze / the dashboard's Compile step." ""
        Else ;
            Send BarButtonState (oCompileBtn(hoBar)) True True (iNext = 1) "Compile" CS_GlyphCompile "(F5) Compile the selected program. Click again while compiling to Stop." ""

        // ANALYZE lights once a compile has left output to read.
        If (bCompiling = True) ;
            Move "Available when the running compile finishes." to sTip
        Else If (bHaveOutput = False) ;
            Move "Run a compile first - Analyze reads the compiler's warning and error output." to sTip
        Else ;
            Move "Analyze the warnings and errors and suggest refactoring functions (read-only)." to sTip
        Move (bHaveOutput and not(bCompiling)) to bEnabled
        Send BarButtonState (oAnalyzeBtn(hoBar)) True bEnabled (bEnabled and (iNext = 2)) "" "" sTip ""

        // ANALYZE SOURCE (Nils 2026-07-11): the GUIDED tile's Deep Scan button on the DASHBOARD rules which path
        // is taken - Compile+Analyze OR Deep Scan (Nils 2026-07-12). So on THIS bar it is live ONLY once you are
        // already in the scan view (bDSMode = a re-scan), in violet; in the compile path it stays SHUT -
        // including after the compile finishes (the compile ticks the program's folder into the selection, so a
        // folder-based gate wrongly re-lit it) - EXCEPT once the compile path is EXHAUSTED (iNext = 0: analyzed,
        // nothing left to modernize), where it becomes the meaningful next step (obsolete symbols the compiler
        // is silent about; Nils 2026-07-15 - StartAnalyze also pops a one-time YesNo there, MaybeSuggestDeepScan).
        // An exhausted scan loop SHUTS it (Nils 2026-07-16): an immediate re-scan would find the same nothing,
        // while the real next step (Compile, unlocked + amber) sat beside it. CHECK ALREADY SCANNED (Nils
        // 2026-08-07): a guided Check runs the Deep Scan FIRST and folds its results into this very grid, so on
        // the trail there is no separate scan left to offer - after the two scan-loop branches, so a genuine
        // re-scan loop still works.
        // Not rendered at all while CB_TestCompilePlanButtons is False (the Else below).
        If (CB_DeepScanEnabled = True and CB_TestCompilePlanButtons = True) Begin
            If (bDSMode = True and bScanExhausted = True) ;
                Send BarButtonState (oAnalyzeSourceBtn(hoBar)) True False False "" "" "Source loop exhausted - the last source analyze found nothing a fix plan can act on. Next: Compile + Analyze - the compiler verifies what a source analyze cannot. (After source changes, start a new one from the dashboard.)" ""
            Else If (bDSMode = True) ;
                Send BarButtonState (oAnalyzeSourceBtn(hoBar)) True True False "" "" "Analyze Source: re-analyze the selected source folders for obsolete commands and symbols - no compile needed. Results load into the Analyzer tab." "tc-scan"
            Else If (bGuidedTrail = True) ;
                Send BarButtonState (oAnalyzeSourceBtn(hoBar)) True False False "" "" "Check already analyzed the source - its findings are folded into this grid alongside the compiler's, so a separate pass would repeat work just done. To re-analyze after changing source, run another Check." ""
            Else If (iNext = 0 and bCompiling = False) ;
                Send BarButtonState (oAnalyzeSourceBtn(hoBar)) True True False "" "" "Nothing left for the compiler to flag - analyze the source for obsolete commands and symbols it is silent about. No compile needed; results load into the Analyzer tab." "tc-scan"
            Else ;
                Send BarButtonState (oAnalyzeSourceBtn(hoBar)) True False False "" "" "Analyze Source is the alternative discovery path - this bar is in the Compile path. The button lights once the compiler has nothing left to flag; a guided Check analyzes the source in the same pass." ""
        End
        Else ;
            Send BarButtonState (oAnalyzeSourceBtn(hoBar)) False False False "" "" "" ""

        // RUN PLAN (was "Run Modernization Plan" - renamed 2026-07-10 to match the dashboard cycle strip's
        // step-3 vocabulary) is the "go" action: it lights once the analyzer grid holds ACTIONABLE rows.
        // ...EXCEPT ON THE TRAIL, where it stays shut (Nils 2026-08-09: "when the analyze is done, the
        // testcompile's Run Plan button should still be disabled, as the user's focus should be on the WIP
        // dashboard"). A guided Check ends by putting the plan on the trail AS ITS NEXT ACTION; a second, live
        // Run Plan down here offered the same step from a surface the trail was not pointing at - two front
        // doors for one action. Shut AND explained reads as "not here", not as broken: the tooltip names where
        // the plan lives and the tail points at the dashboard. The guided tooltip goes FIRST because it is
        // right in every trail state. The click route stays (see the button's OnButtonClick).
        If (bGuidedTrail = True) ;
            Move "The guided dashboard is driving this run - start the fix plan from its Run Plan action, so the trail records that it ran and moves on. This bar's copy serves the manual and cockpit flows." to sTip
        Else If (bGridReady = False) ;
            Move "Run Analyze first - the fix plan is built from the analyzed warnings and errors." to sTip
        Else If (iActionable = 0) ;
            Move "Nothing to modernize - Analyze found no auto-fixable or review warnings in the workspace (any warnings are in system / out-of-scope files, not refactorable here)." to sTip
        Else ;
            Move "Auto-select the suggested refactoring functions + files/folders in a separate dialog." to sTip
        Move (bGridReady and (iActionable > 0) and not(bGuidedTrail)) to bEnabled
        // Shown only while CB_TestCompilePlanButtons says so; its state is kept up to date either way.
        Send BarButtonState (oRunPlanBtn(hoBar)) CB_TestCompilePlanButtons bEnabled (bEnabled and (iNext = 3)) "" "" sTip ""

        // THE TAIL after the buttons - the dashboard strip's message language on the working surface: amber
        // "recompile to verify" right after a plan run, teal all-clear once the analyze left nothing
        // actionable, else the standing reminder that modernization LOOPS.
        If ((bPostRun = True or (bDSMode = True and bScanPlanRan = True)) and iNext = 1 and bCompiling = False) ;
            Move (((SpanOpen("tctail tc-recomp")) + ((DashIcon(CS_GlyphRepeat)) + 'plan applied ')) + CS_MidDot + (' recompile to verify' + CS_SpanEnd)) to sTail
        // The exhausted-scan handoff (Nils 2026-07-16): the scan lane is done, Compile is the amber next
        // step - name the hand-over instead of the generic "repeat until clean" loop reminder.
        Else If (bScanExhausted = True and bCompiling = False) ;
            Move (((SpanOpen("tctail tc-recomp")) + ((DashIcon(CS_GlyphRepeat)) + 'scan exhausted ')) + CS_MidDot + (' compile to verify' + CS_SpanEnd)) to sTail
        Else If (iNext = 0 and bGridReady = True) ;
            Move ((SpanOpen("tctail tc-clean")) + CS_Check + (' nothing left to modernize' + CS_SpanEnd)) to sTail
        // Mid-scan-loop (findings on the grid, plan not yet exhausted): name WHY the loop goes around more
        // than once, instead of the generic reminder - staged conversions unlock each other, so one cycle is
        // rarely enough (Nils 2026-07-17).
        Else If (bDSMode = True and iActionable > 0 and bCompiling = False) ;
            Move (((SpanOpen("tctail")) + ((DashIcon(CS_GlyphRepeat)) + 'some fixes unlock others ')) + CS_MidDot + (' repeat until the scan is exhausted' + CS_SpanEnd)) to sTail
        // The trail owns the next step, so SAY so. Without this the bar goes quiet at exactly the moment it has
        // the most to say: Run Plan is shut by design (above), and a shut button never takes the amber
        // you-are-here, so the strip would show no next action at all and read as stalled.
        // Hidden with the two buttons (CB_TestCompilePlanButtons): the standing reminder shows instead.
        Else If (CB_TestCompilePlanButtons = True and bGuidedTrail = True and iNext = 3 and bCompiling = False) ;
            Move (((SpanOpen("tctail")) + ((DashIcon(CS_GlyphArrowRight)) + 'run the fix plan from the guided dashboard')) + CS_SpanEnd) to sTail
        Else ;
            Move (((SpanOpen("tctail")) + ((DashIcon(CS_GlyphRepeat)) + 'repeat until clean')) + CS_SpanEnd) to sTail
        Move ((CssLink("testcompile")) + (DivOpen("tctailbar")) + sTail) to sHtml
        // The busy marquee (right edge, via margin-left:auto) - rendered only while work is in flight, so an
        // idle strip shows no dead track.
        If (bBusy = True) ;
            Move (sHtml + (((DivOpenTip("tcmarq", "Working - compiling, analyzing or building the fix plan.")) + CS_DivOpen) + CS_DivEnd + CS_DivEnd)) to sHtml
        Move (sHtml + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml
        If (sHtml <> psTailHtml(Self)) Begin
            Set psTailHtml to sHtml
            Move (oTail_htm(hoBar)) to hoTail
            // UpdateHtml keeps the value for a page that loads and pushes it to one that is up.
            Send UpdateHtml of hoTail sHtml
        End
        If (not(pbInitWebApp(hoBar))) ;
            Send CheckUpdateWebUI of hoBar
    End_Procedure

    // DEEP-SCAN lockdown (combo half) RETIRED with the program combo (branch guided-flow-redesign): it only
    // greyed oWebProgram_cf while the grid showed Deep Scan results. With the combo gone (program selection
    // lives in the Check Workspace view) there is nothing to lock; the Compile button's own shut state
    // (RefreshActionButtons) already conveys the mid-scan state.

    /* BUSY MEANS THE COMPILER IS RUNNING - not "running or still being read".

       Both halves of this have now been reported, from opposite sides. 2026-07-24: Analyze went
       amber while the Compile button still read Stop Compile, so busy was widened to cover the
       .prn finalize. 2026-08-31: "the Output shows compile finished, but the x Stop Compile
       button is still lit ... it takes a long long time before the Analyze chip is lit".
       ONE FLAG CANNOT CARRY BOTH STATES. What actually confused the first report was the chip
       still offering STOP over a process that had already exited - Stop is meaningless then, and
       DoCompileToggle would call KillCompile on nothing. So the flag goes back to meaning the
       process, which drops Stop and advances the stepper to Analyze the moment the compiler is
       done - and the finalize is covered where it belongs: the status bar says what it is doing,
       and StartAnalyze WAITS for it (see the pending-analyze latch there) instead of racing it
       into a full re-read of the listing. */
    Procedure SyncWebButtonStates
        Boolean bActive
        Get pbActive of oCompileProcess to bActive
        Send RefreshActionButtons bActive
    End_Procedure

    // Load the compiler's .err output into a single string.
    // Returns "" on failure (missing / empty / locked file).
    //
    // Delegates to the byte-safe rfReadEncodedFileAsString helper - same
    // rationale as LoadCompileLogTail: one Read_Block plus a single decode
    // pass instead of a text-mode Readln loop, so OEM / extended bytes in
    // compiler error text aren't mangled and there's no O(n^2) per-line
    // string concatenation.
    Function LoadErrFileContents String sFilePath Returns String
        Function_Return (rfReadEncodedFileAsString(sFilePath))
    End_Function

    // Resolve the .prn the last compile actually wrote. The output-file suffix is
    // per-program (its .cfg [Compiler] 32BitSuffix/64BitSuffix), so we delegate to
    // the compile mixin: it reads the cfg and returns whichever candidate exists,
    // freshest by write time. Returns "" if neither is present.
    Function ResolvePrnFile String sProgram tSwsFile TargetWS Returns String
        String sPrn
        Get ResolveCompiledFile of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "prn" to sPrn
        Function_Return sPrn
    End_Function

    // Count non-overlapping occurrences of sNeedle in sText. Pure string scan (no
    // control state) - used for the error/warning summary header.
    Function CountInString String sText String sNeedle Returns Integer
        Integer iCount iPos iLen

        Move (Length(sNeedle)) to iLen
        If (iLen = 0) ;
            Function_Return 0
        Move 0 to iCount
        Move (Pos(sNeedle, sText)) to iPos
        While (iPos > 0)
            Increment iCount
            Move (Mid(sText, (Length(sText)), (iPos + iLen))) to sText
            Move (Pos(sNeedle, sText)) to iPos
        Loop
        Function_Return iCount
    End_Function

    // The Analyze step: drive the ModAnalyzer dock to (re)load + classify the latest compile
    // output. ActivateModAnalyzer sets the path, takes the shared bottom slot (hiding the
    // Compiler Output dock) and its grid LoadData parses the .prn + fills the grid. Busy feedback
    // (wait cursor + marquee) wraps the synchronous parse; pbAnalyzing blocks re-entry (LoadData
    // pumps the queue, so a queued Analyze click must be ignored). (Was the Analyze button body.)
    Procedure StartAnalyze
        String sPath sStamp sListIso sText
        Boolean bBusy bExists bGuided
        Integer iHH iMM iSS iRetval
        Date dDate
        DateTime dtList

        Get pbAnalyzing to bBusy
        If (bBusy) ;
            Procedure_Return
        /* WAIT FOR THE LISTING, do not race it (see pbAnalyzeWhenFinalized). While the .prn tail
           is still draining the cache is not stamped, so LoadData's fast paths both miss and it
           re-reads the whole listing synchronously - on the same file the background tail is
           consuming. Deferring costs the finalize's few seconds and saves a full multi-MB read.
           The status bar is already narrating the finalize, and PrnFinalizeTick re-arms us. */
        If (pbPrnFinalizing(Self) = True) Begin
            Set pbAnalyzeWhenFinalized to True
            Procedure_Return
        End
        // Phase stamp: the compiler has finished by the time Analyze starts, so this closes the
        // COMPILE phase for the guided board's timing line (see cRefactorApplication). Only
        // stamped when a Check is in flight - a dock-driven analyze leaves the stamps alone.
        If (piChkTickStart(ghoApplication) <> 0 and piChkTickCompile(ghoApplication) = 0) ;
            Set piChkTickCompile of ghoApplication to (GetTickCount())
        Get psErrFilePath to sPath
        // No .err (a clean build with warnings) - analyze the .prn FinishCompile resolved.
        If (sPath = "") ;
            Get psPrnFilePath to sPath
        /* NOTHING TO ANALYZE - but a guided Check still needs its answer (Nils 2026-08-07).

           A TRULY clean build leaves no .err and no warning .prn, so this bails before the tail -
           and the tail is where the guided handback and the one-shot reset both live. The board
           that asked for the Check therefore sat unchanged, and pbGuidedCheckRun stayed True to
           hijack whatever the user analyzed NEXT. Harmless while Analyze was a manual click after
           a dirty compile; not harmless now that a Check drives it automatically, where a clean
           compile is the expected happy ending.

           So the bail hands back the same way the tail does. RenderGuidedFindings on an empty grid
           is exactly right: the trail reads "nothing actionable", which is the true verdict for a
           clean build, and its stage-2 logic already treats that as done.

           BUT THE GRID IS NOT EMPTY AFTER A RUN (2026-09-11). It holds the previous analysis, so this
           exit re-rendered the pre-run findings as the board's verdict. A clean build no longer lands
           here - FinishCompile records its listing, and the analyze replaces the rows. This stays for
           the rare build that leaves no listing at all. */
        If (sPath = "") Begin
            Get pbGuidedCheckRun of ghoApplication to bGuided
            If (bGuided = False) ;
                Get pbGuidedSurfaceUsed of ghoApplication to bGuided
            If (bGuided = True) Begin
                Set pbGuidedCheckRun of ghoApplication to False
                If (piChkTickStart(ghoApplication) <> 0) ;
                    Set piChkTickAnalyze of ghoApplication to (GetTickCount())
                Broadcast Send RenderGuidedFindings of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
            End
            Procedure_Return
        End
        // STALE-LISTING GUARD (Nils 2026-07-11): an Analyze right after a plan run re-reads the
        // pre-run compile listing - the SAME warnings show, a convincing plan builds, and the run
        // comes back all zeroes because the previous run already fixed everything (the 17:34
        // listing vs the 18:27 run). The listing's file time vs the engine's plan-run stamp
        // detects exactly that; both are "yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm", so a plain string compare orders them.
        Get ReadPlanRunStamp of ghoApplication to sStamp
        Get vFilePathExists sPath to bExists
        If (sStamp <> "" and bExists = True) Begin
            Get_File_Mod_Time sPath to dDate iHH iMM iSS
            Move dDate to dtList
            Move (String(DateGetYear(dtList)) + "-" + Right("0" + String(DateGetMonth(dtList)), 2) + "-" + Right("0" + String(DateGetDay(dtList)), 2) + " " + Right("0" + String(iHH), 2) + ":" + Right("0" + String(iMM), 2)) to sListIso
            If (sListIso < sStamp) Begin
                Move ("The compiler output is OLDER than the last plan run.\n\nCompiled:" * sListIso + "\nLast plan run:" * sStamp + "\n\nIts warnings describe the code BEFORE that run, so most are probably fixed already - a plan built from this usually comes back with nothing to do.\n\nRecompile now instead? (Yes compiles fresh; No analyzes the old output anyway.)") to sText
                Get FTYesNoBox sText "Analyze - output predates the plan run" to iRetval
                If (iRetval = MBR_Yes) Begin
                    Send StartCompile
                    Procedure_Return
                End
            End
        End
        Set pbAnalyzing to True
        Send Cursor_Wait of Cursor_Control
        Send SetBusyMarquee True
        // NAME THE STEP BEFORE THE DOCK GOES UP (Nils 2026-08-31): on a first Check the analyzer
        // panel is created and docked here, and the .prn is opened right after - seconds to tens of
        // seconds during which the status bar still read "Ready. Compiled in Ns.", so the analyze
        // looked like it had not started. LoadData's own per-phase beats take the text over from here.
        Set StatusText of (Statusbar_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication))) to "Opening the Modernization Analyzer..."
        // Pump now so the wait cursor paints + the marquee push lands immediately on click -
        // otherwise they wouldn't show until LoadData's pump, i.e. after the (slow, first-time)
        // panel dock. (The web marquee then animates on WebView2's compositor - no further pumping
        // needed to keep it moving, unlike the old Win32 bar.)
        Send PumpMsgQueue of Desktop
        Send ActivateModAnalyzer of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sPath
        Send SetBusyMarquee False
        Send Cursor_Ready of Cursor_Control
        Set pbAnalyzing to False
        // Analyze has run for this output - let the Auto Select button enable.
        Set pbAnalyzed to True
        Send SyncWebButtonStates
        // GUIDED CHECK (branch guided-flow-redesign): when the Check Workspace view drove this
        // compile+analyze, hand the analyzed grid to ITS one severity-ranked findings list instead of the
        // dashboard-era nudges + Folders/Functions detour (the scope-reset / 95-function-picker anti-pattern
        // this redesign kills). One-shot flag, set by the view's Check button and consumed here.
        Get pbGuidedCheckRun of ghoApplication to bGuided
        // STICKY guided surface (Nils 2026-07-23): once the Check Workspace view has been used this
        // session, every analyze hands back there - a dock-driven compile+analyze that left the user
        // in the grid+editor read as the flow losing its place.
        If (bGuided = False) ;
            Get pbGuidedSurfaceUsed of ghoApplication to bGuided
        If (bGuided = True) Begin
            Set pbGuidedCheckRun of ghoApplication to False
            // Phase stamp: analyze + grid fill are done; the board renders next and reports the split.
            If (piChkTickStart(ghoApplication) <> 0) ;
                Set piChkTickAnalyze of ghoApplication to (GetTickCount())
            Broadcast Send RenderGuidedFindings of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
            Procedure_Return
        End
        // Blocker nudge FIRST (Nils 2026-07-20): a flood of name-collision errors (4544/4390) has no
        // auto-fix and traps the normal cycle, so point the user at the report before anything else.
        // It only fires when the output is DIRTY with those errors, and the clean-state nudges below
        // only fire when the output is CLEAN - mutually exclusive, so no coordination is needed.
        Send MaybeSuggestNameCollision
        // Campaign-ladder handoffs, in rung order (Nils 2026-07-19): latch this analyze's
        // compile-lane verdict first; then BOTH lanes exhausted -> the one-time Tidy Up offer
        // (the campaign exit); else compile lane exhausted alone -> the one-time Deep Scan nudge
        // (the other lane). At most ONE dialog pops: when Tidy Up fires, the scan-lane latch it
        // required also makes MaybeSuggestDeepScan bow out.
        Send NoteCompileLaneVerdict
        Send MaybeSuggestTidyUp
        Send MaybeSuggestDeepScan
    End_Procedure

    // One-time nudge toward FunctionReturnMismatchReport when the analyzed output is FLOODED with
    // error 4544 (ambiguous return type in expression). The LegacyApp lesson (2026-07-20): a few
    // conflicting declarations manufacture hundreds of errors, NO auto-fix at the call sites clears
    // them, and they TRAP the normal cycle - the plan "fixes" the call sites, recompiles, and they are
    // all still there. So catch the user where they get stuck and point them at the report, which
    // pinpoints the handful of declarations to fix. Fires at most once per workspace per session
    // (psNameCollisionSuggestedWS), like MaybeSuggestDeepScan. Popping the modal inline is safe here
    // (StartAnalyze runs off the web pump); Yes arms CI_TCDeferNameCollision, whose timer branch hands
    // over to the guided view's report runner (never an inline view flow from this deep tail - the
    // 4402/banner lesson). The guided path suppresses this nudge entirely: its findings list already
    // carries the same offer as a row.
    //
    // GATED ON THE 4544 COUNT ALONE, not the combined collision total (Nils 2026-07-22: "I'm not sure
    // the text is correct now?"). The report used to have a PART 2 inventorying 4390 backports, so
    // lumping both counts into one number and one offer made sense. That part is gone - it scanned
    // install SOURCE and so could not see runtime-owned names - and answering Yes on a pure-4390 flood
    // would now open a report that says "no return-type mismatches". 4390 has its own route (the
    // findings list's Guard action), so the text names it separately and never counts it in the offer.
    Procedure MaybeSuggestNameCollision
        Boolean bGridReady
        Integer iRet iBack iRetval
        String sWS sText

        If (ghoModAnalyzerDock = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get psSWSFile of ghoApplication to sWS
        If (sWS = "" or sWS = psNameCollisionSuggestedWS(Self)) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get pbAnalyzeDataReady  of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to bGridReady
        Get piSumReturnTypeErr  of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to iRet
        Get piSumBackportErr    of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to iBack
        // A cluster, not a stray: 10+ ambiguous-return-type errors is unmistakably a declaration
        // conflict (a lone 4544 is a legitimate call-site case, and IS convertible - not this).
        If (bGridReady = False or iRet < 10) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set psNameCollisionSuggestedWS to sWS               // one-shot latch for this workspace (this session)
        Move (String(iRet) + " errors in this program come from a handful of DECLARATIONS, not from the call") to sText
        Move (sText + " sites the compiler points at. One message name is declared with more than one return") to sText
        Move (sText + " type, and that makes every expression-style call of that name fail - including in files") to sText
        Move (sText + " that have nothing to do with the class responsible. Changing those call sites cannot") to sText
        Move (sText + " clear it, so running the Refactoring Plan on them will not help.\n\n") to sText
        If (iBack > 0) Begin
            Move (sText + "(The " + String(iBack) + " 'already defined' error(s) are a separate matter - a global function whose") to sText
            Move (sText + " name DataFlex now ships itself. This report does not cover those.)\n\n") to sText
        End
        Move (sText + "Open the function return-type mismatch report? It lists every declaration of each") to sText
        Move (sText + " contested name - the workspace's and the ones DataFlex ships - so you can see which to rename.") to sText
        Get FTYesNoBox sText "Return-type mismatches detected" to iRetval
        If (iRetval = MBR_Yes) Begin
            Send ArmAction of oButtonDeferTimer CI_TCDeferNameCollision
        End
    End_Procedure

    // One-time nudge toward Deep Scan when the COMPILE path is exhausted (Nils 2026-07-15). Called at the tail of
    // StartAnalyze: when the analyzed compiler output yields NOTHING actionable (auto-fix + review = 0) and the
    // grid is not already showing a scan, the compiler has nothing left to flag - exactly where Deep Scan
    // (obsolete symbols the compiler is silent about) is the meaningful next step. Pops a ONE-TIME YesNo, keyed
    // by WORKSPACE identity (psDeepScanSuggestedWS) so it fires at most once per workspace per session -
    // re-analyzing the same clean output or re-activating the dock never re-nags; a different workspace re-arms
    // it. YES defers to the EXISTING CI_TCDeferDeepScan (so RunDeepScan runs on the timer's fresh stack and
    // confirms the folder scope itself). The bar's Analyze Source button is ALSO live at this exhausted state
    // (RefreshActionButtons), independent of this one-shot, so the door stays open after EITHER answer. Popping the
    // modal inline is safe here: StartAnalyze already pops its stale-listing YesNo, and it runs OFF the web pump
    // (F5, or the CI_TCDeferAnalyze timer), never on the WebView2 click stack that the deferral pattern guards.
    Procedure MaybeSuggestDeepScan
        Boolean bGridReady bDSMode
        Integer iActionable iSug iRetval
        String sWS sText

        If (CB_DeepScanEnabled = False) ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (ghoModAnalyzerDock = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        // Once per workspace per session: skip if we've already suggested for THIS .sws (or none is loaded).
        Get psSWSFile of ghoApplication to sWS
        If (sWS = "" or sWS = psDeepScanSuggestedWS(Self)) ;
            Procedure_Return
        // Scan lane already reported itself exhausted this session: suggesting a scan we KNOW is
        // exhausted would be dishonest - that state belongs to the campaign exit (MaybeSuggestTidyUp,
        // which the StartAnalyze tail runs just before this). (Nils 2026-07-19.)
        If (sWS = psScanLaneDoneWS(Self)) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get pbAnalyzeDataReady of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to bGridReady
        Get pbDeepScanView     of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to bDSMode
        Get piSumAuto          of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to iActionable
        Get piSumSuggest       of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to iSug
        Move (iActionable + iSug) to iActionable
        // Only when the COMPILE path is truly exhausted: analyzed (grid ready), clean (nothing actionable), and
        // NOT already a deep-scan view (never suggest a scan from inside a scan's own empty result set).
        If (bGridReady = False or bDSMode = True or iActionable > 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set psDeepScanSuggestedWS to sWS               // one-shot latch for this workspace (this session)
        Move ("Nothing left for the compiler to flag in this program.\n\nAnalyze Source finds obsolete commands, functions and symbols the compiler is silent about - across the selected source folders.\n\nAnalyze the source now?") to sText
        Get FTYesNoBox sText "Analyze Source?" to iRetval
        If (iRetval = MBR_Yes) Begin
            Send ArmAction of oButtonDeferTimer CI_TCDeferDeepScan
        End
    End_Procedure

    // Latch (or clear) the compile lane's verdict for the current workspace after an analyze -
    // the memory MaybeSuggestTidyUp reads. Both directions on purpose: an analyze that finds
    // actionable rows must UN-latch a stale clean (the source is dirty again), or a later
    // scan-exhausted would claim the campaign exit off an old verdict. A Deep Scan grid is
    // skipped entirely - that view is the SCAN lane, whose verdict lands via RunDeepScan
    // (psScanLaneDoneWS). (Nils 2026-07-19.)
    Procedure NoteCompileLaneVerdict
        Boolean bGridReady bDSMode
        Integer iActionable iSug iExplain
        String sWS

        If (ghoModAnalyzerDock = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get psSWSFile of ghoApplication to sWS
        If (sWS = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get pbAnalyzeDataReady of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to bGridReady
        Get pbDeepScanView     of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to bDSMode
        If (bGridReady = False or bDSMode = True) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get piSumAuto    of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to iActionable
        Get piSumSuggest of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to iSug
        Get piSumExplain of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to iExplain
        If (iActionable + iSug = 0) Begin
            Set psCompileLaneCleanWS to sWS
            // Capture the hand-migration count FRESH here (the grid is this analyze's) - the campaign-exit
            // handoff reads the latch, not the live grid, which a later scan load would have zeroed.
            Set piCompileLaneExplain to iExplain
        End
        Else If (psCompileLaneCleanWS(Self) = sWS) ;
            Set psCompileLaneCleanWS to ""
    End_Procedure

    // --- The campaign EXIT: Tidy Up (Nils 2026-07-19) ---
    // The two modernization lanes hand off to each other (scan exhausted -> "Compile to verify";
    // compile exhausted -> MaybeSuggestDeepScan above), but the ladder had no exit: with BOTH
    // lanes exhausted the modernization phase is done - and the app went quiet at its moment of
    // triumph. The dashboard's "then:" band (Tidy Up / x64 + Unicode) names the next phase; this
    // one-time YesNo walks the user there. Called at the tail of StartAnalyze (after
    // NoteCompileLaneVerdict latched this analyze's verdict) and from RunDeepScan's exhausted
    // verdict (DashboardFT latches psScanLaneDoneWS first) - so it fires at whichever lane
    // finishes SECOND, in either order. With Deep Scan disabled (CB_DeepScanEnabled) the scan
    // lane does not exist, so the compile lane alone qualifies - otherwise those users would
    // never see the offer. One question only (Studio convention); Yes ARMS CI_TCDeferTidyUp,
    // whose timer branch runs the dashboard Tidy Up tile's own view flow on a fresh tick (never
    // via a toolbar Execute; never inline from these deep tails - see the branch); No changes
    // nothing - the dashboard tile remains the door. Popping the MODAL inline is safe at both
    // call sites: the analyze tail runs off the web pump (see MaybeSuggestDeepScan), and
    // RunDeepScan runs from a defer timer.
    Procedure MaybeSuggestTidyUp
        String sWS sText
        Integer iRetval iManual
        Boolean bScanDone

        Get psSWSFile of ghoApplication to sWS
        If (sWS = "" or sWS = psTidyUpSuggestedWS(Self)) ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (sWS <> psCompileLaneCleanWS(Self)) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Move (sWS = psScanLaneDoneWS(Self)) to bScanDone
        If (CB_DeepScanEnabled = False) ;
            Move True to bScanDone
        If (bScanDone = False) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set psTidyUpSuggestedWS to sWS                 // one-shot latch for this workspace (this session)
        // "Exhausted" only means the AUTOMATED work is done - the cycle never surfaces hand-migration
        // items (obsolete commands, DB-API/registry/SQL patterns, ...) because they have no converter.
        // So when the last clean analyze left in-scope Explain rows (piCompileLaneExplain, latched by
        // NoteCompileLaneVerdict), say so here instead of the old "Nothing left to modernize" - that
        // was the FALSE "done" Nils flagged 2026-07-20. The reports list every site; this names them.
        Get piCompileLaneExplain to iManual
        If (iManual > 0) Begin
            Move ("The AUTOMATED modernization is exhausted for this workspace - but " + String(iManual) + " item(s) still need a HAND migration the tool can't safely auto-convert (obsolete commands, and DB-API / registry / SQL / dfpath / field-option patterns).") to sText
            Move (sText + "\n\nThe detailed reports in the Modernization group - Legacy DB-API, Registry, SQL, Set_dfpath, Field-Options, Gosub - list every site. Run them from Select Functions when you are ready.") to sText
        End
        Else If (CB_DeepScanEnabled = True) ;
            Move "Nothing left to modernize: the compiler analysis and the source analyze both report this workspace exhausted." to sText
        Else ;
            Move "Nothing left to modernize: the compiler analysis reports this workspace exhausted." to sText
        Move (sText + "\n\nThe Tidy Up group holds the tidy-and-remove passes that follow modernization. (The x64 + Unicode pass is also available, from the dashboard.)\n\nOpen the Tidy Up function selection now?") to sText
        Get FTYesNoBox sText "Tidy Up?" to iRetval
        If (iRetval = MBR_Yes) Begin
            // Defer the view flow to the button timer's fresh tick (CI_TCDeferTidyUp - the
            // sequence lives there). Running it inline HERE - deep inside RunDeepScan's tail or
            // the analyze tail, with the analyzer dock's WebView2 work still settling - left the
            // Functions view's Run-button box rendering "Unhandled Program Error on the Client"
            // (Nils's LegacyApp2 live test, 2026-07-19). Same Yes-defers idiom as
            // MaybeSuggestDeepScan above.
            Send ArmAction of oButtonDeferTimer CI_TCDeferTidyUp
        End
    End_Procedure

    // Auto Select / "Refactor Plan": turn the analyzed warnings/errors into a run. Resolves the
    // controller's OWN compile output (psErrFilePath/psPrnFilePath, set by FinishCompile), scopes
    // the messages to the selected workspace (the SAME FilterToWorkspaceFiles the triage grid
    // uses), builds the plan (PlanFromMessages), shows the DD-bound review/select dialog, then
    // selects the ticked functions and hands the engine a FILE-PRECISE list of the ticked files.
    // (Was StartRefactoring in the read-only ModAnalyzer.dg; the controller now owns the run.)
    // Tick count when StartAutoSelect began building the Refactoring Plan; 0 = no build running. Drives the
    // status-bar elapsed readout in DoYieldToUI + the final "Ready." note in StopBuildFeedback, mirroring the
    // Compile/Analyze elapsed-time status (Nils 2026-07-03: a long plan build only showed the wait cursor +
    // marquee - the status bar should count the seconds like Compile and Analyze do).
    Property Integer piPlanBuildStartTick 0

    // Guided-flow handshake for StartAutoSelect (Nils 2026-07-25: after the last plan run consumed
    // every auto-fixable row, the guided list still offered "Run the refactoring plan" and the click
    // dead-ended in the generic empty-plan Info_Box). pbGuidedPlanRun is a ONE-SHOT the guided caller
    // sets right before Send StartAutoSelect - consumed (read + cleared) at entry; while it is set the
    // empty-plan bail stays SILENT here and pbAutoSelectPlanEmpty carries the outcome back, so the
    // guided surface can explain the state and retire its own spent action instead.
    Property Boolean pbGuidedPlanRun       False
    Property Boolean pbAutoSelectPlanEmpty False

    // FT: cModAnalyzer's plan build calls this (via phoUIYieldTarget) every ~64 rows so the marquee keeps
    // animating through a long build. Lives on the FT dock so Desktop/PumpMsgQueue stay out of cModAnalyzer.
    // Also ticks the status-bar elapsed time ("Loading Refactoring Plan... (Ns)") while a plan build runs.
    Procedure DoYieldToUI
        Integer iTick
        Handle hoStatusBar
        Get piPlanBuildStartTick to iTick
        If (iTick <> 0) Begin
            Get Statusbar_Id of (phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)) to hoStatusBar
            Set StatusText of hoStatusBar to ("Loading Refactoring Plan... (" + String((GetTickCount() - iTick) / 1000) + "s)")
        End
        Send PumpMsgQueue of Desktop
    End_Procedure

    // FT: the plan dialog calls this (via phoTestCompileFT) once its lists load, to stop the build marquee +
    // wait cursor that StartAutoSelect leaves running across ShowPlan. Also the belt-stop after ShowPlan.
    Procedure StopBuildFeedback
        Integer iTick
        Handle hoStatusBar
        Send SetBusyMarquee False
        Send Cursor_Ready of Cursor_Control
        // Finalize the elapsed status ONCE per plan build ("Ready. ..."), mirroring Analyze. Idempotent: the
        // belt-stop second call - and any caller outside a plan build - sees 0 and leaves the status alone.
        Get piPlanBuildStartTick to iTick
        If (iTick <> 0) Begin
            Set piPlanBuildStartTick to 0
            Get Statusbar_Id of (phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)) to hoStatusBar
            Set StatusText of hoStatusBar to ("Ready. Refactoring Plan loaded in " + String((GetTickCount() - iTick) / 1000) + "s.")
        End
    End_Procedure

    Procedure StartAutoSelect
        String sErrFile sPrnFile sFolder
        String[] asSelFns asSelFiles asPrevSelFns asNoExtraKeys asOccKeys
        String[] asKeepFns asKeepHints asDroppedFns
        String   sHint
        Boolean  bIsReport
        tSavedPlanRow[] aLiveRows aPlanRows
        tSavedPlanFlood[] aFloods
        String[] asNeeded asKept asAutoFns asShowFiles asUnticked
        Integer iRowFn iRowMax iP
        String sRowFns sRowFn
        Boolean bExists bProceed bSaved bOK bDSMode bGuidedLaunch bModSelect bHasSelect bIndConv
        Integer i iCount iFiltered
        tCompileError[] aMessages
        tModRefactorPlan Plan
        tSavedPlan SavedPlan
        tModIgnore[] aIgnored
        tRefactorFiles RefactorFiles
        Handle ho hoDD hoEditor hoStatusBar

        // Consume the guided one-shot + reset the outcome flag (see the property block).
        Get pbGuidedPlanRun to bGuidedLaunch
        Set pbGuidedPlanRun       to False
        Set pbAutoSelectPlanEmpty to False

        Send Cursor_Wait of Cursor_Control
        // Marquee 'preparing' feedback for the (synchronous) plan build, mirroring Compile/Analyze.
        // (On the dashboard's un-shown-dock path the state is kept for when the bar is shown - see RefreshActionButtons.)
        Send SetBusyMarquee True
        // Status-bar note so the wait is explained - shown for BOTH entry points: the dock's "Refactor Plan"
        // action (which calls this directly) and the dashboard's StartSavedPlanReview. PumpMsgQueue flushes the
        // paint so the text appears BEFORE the synchronous plan build blocks the message loop. The build's
        // DoYieldToUI beats then append the elapsed seconds (like Compile/Analyze); StopBuildFeedback finalizes
        // to "Ready. ..." - the error paths below reset the tick and clear the text instead.
        Get Statusbar_Id of (phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)) to hoStatusBar
        Set piPlanBuildStartTick to (GetTickCount())
        Set StatusText of hoStatusBar to "Loading Refactoring Plan..."
        Send PumpMsgQueue of Desktop
        // Two ways to get the plan, depending on what the analyzer grid is showing:
        //  - SAVED-PLAN view (View last plan): no live messages exist (LoadSavedPlan doesn't populate
        //    paScopedMessages, and nothing was compiled this session). Rebuild the plan from the frozen JSON
        //    rows so "Refactor Plan" still works on a saved plan. The user reviews / deselects in ShowPlan,
        //    and the run applies to the CURRENT source (the review is the safety net for a stale snapshot).
        //  - LIVE analyze: use the messages the grid already parsed + workspace-scoped (the grid is the
        //    SINGLE parser); the .prn re-parse is just a defensive fallback for an empty cache.
        Get pbSavedView of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to bSaved
        If (bSaved = True) Begin
            Get Create (RefClass(cModAnalyzer)) to ho
            Set phoUIYieldTarget of ho to Self
            Get ReadSavedPlan of ho (&SavedPlan) to bOK
            If (bOK = True) Begin
                // The per-workspace Ignore list rides along - the saved JSON keeps ALL rows and
                // the saved-VIEW grid re-filters at load, so the plan must too (an ignored row's
                // function came back ticked after a restart; Nils's WinServ lap 2026-07-17).
                Get ReadIgnored of ho to aIgnored
                Get PlanFromSavedRows of ho SavedPlan.aRows aIgnored asNoExtraKeys to Plan
                // A collapsed scan rule's summary row is ONE row with ONE file; its file list rides in the
                // saved plan (v3) - fold it in, or a restored plan runs the converter on one file per Check.
                Send ExpandFloodFilesIntoPlan of ho SavedPlan.aFloods aIgnored (&Plan)
                Move SavedPlan.aRows   to aPlanRows      // for the post-dialog file intersection below
                Move SavedPlan.aFloods to aFloods
            End
            Get psSavedPlanStaleWhy of ho to sHint       // read before the Destroy; "" unless the plan's provenance failed
            Send Destroy of ho
            If (bOK = False) Begin
                Set piPlanBuildStartTick to 0
                Send StopBuildFeedback
                Set StatusText of hoStatusBar to ""
                // 2026-08-17: a plan whose compile output no longer matches this workspace is refused
                // by ReadSavedPlan with a reason - show that, not "could not be read".
                If (sHint <> "") ;
                    Send FTInfoBox sHint "Last plan is out of date"
                Else ;
                    Send FTInfoBox "The saved plan file could not be read."
                Procedure_Return
            End
        End
        Else Begin
            /* LIVE path, FROM THE GRID'S ROWS (2026-07-23). The grid's LoadData already classified
               and verified every message - recipe matching, source guards, whole-file dry-runs, the
               lot - and PlanFromSavedRows builds a plan from verdict rows without re-deriving any of
               it (the saved-view path has proved that for weeks). The old live path went back to the
               RAW messages and re-ran the whole verify pipeline a second time; every "keep grid and
               plan in lock-step" comment in this codebase is the tax that duplication charged, and
               building from the rows makes lock-step true BY CONSTRUCTION instead (Nils, 2026-07-23:
               "isn't a lot of the work done more than once?" - yes, it was). PlanFromMessages
               survives below as the fallback for a session with no analyzed grid.
               IndicatorOccurrenceLineKeys hands the companion oracle every folded warned line, so
               merged rows do not hide qualifying indicator uses. */
            Get BuildSavedPlanRows of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to aLiveRows
            If (SizeOfArray(aLiveRows) > 0) Begin
                Get Create (RefClass(cModAnalyzer)) to ho
                Set phoUIYieldTarget of ho to Self
                Get ReadIgnored of ho to aIgnored
                Get IndicatorOccurrenceLineKeys of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to asOccKeys
                Get PlanFromSavedRows of ho aLiveRows aIgnored asOccKeys to Plan
                /* THE COLLAPSED SCAN RULES REACH EVERY FILE (2026-08-16). A rule at or above
                   CI_DSFloodThreshold is ONE summary row with ONE file, and this plan is built from
                   the rows - so a rule with 22,000 findings advanced by ONE FILE per Check on the
                   first live run (Nils, WinServ: "never shows more than 12 at a time"). The grid
                   kept every file the rule fired in; fold them in here. */
                Get FloodsForPlan of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to aFloods
                Send ExpandFloodFilesIntoPlan of ho aFloods aIgnored (&Plan)
                Move aLiveRows to aPlanRows              // for the post-dialog file intersection below
                Send Destroy of ho
            End
            Else Begin
            Get paScopedMessages of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to aMessages
            If (SizeOfArray(aMessages) = 0) Begin
                // Fallback: resolve + parse + scope exactly as the grid's LoadData does. Prefer the
                // .err, fall back to the .prn (a clean build with only warnings has no .err); the .prn
                // is then derived + preferred. SAME FilterToWorkspaceFiles the grid uses.
                Get psErrFilePath to sErrFile
                If (sErrFile = "") ;
                    Get psPrnFilePath to sErrFile
                If (sErrFile = "") Begin
                    Set piPlanBuildStartTick to 0
                    Send StopBuildFeedback
                    Set StatusText of hoStatusBar to ""
                    Send FTInfoBox "No compiler output is loaded to refactor from."
                    Procedure_Return
                End
                Move (Left(sErrFile, Length(sErrFile) - 4) + ".prn") to sPrnFile
                Get vFilePathExists sPrnFile to bExists
                If (bExists = False) ;
                    Move sErrFile to sPrnFile
                // Fast path: the live tail's harvested diagnostics (empty = no valid cache -> full read).
                Get CachedPrnMessages sPrnFile to aMessages
                If (SizeOfArray(aMessages) = 0) ;
                    Get ReadCompilerMessages sPrnFile to aMessages
                Get FilterToWorkspaceFiles of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) aMessages (&iFiltered) to aMessages
            End
            Get Create (RefClass(cModAnalyzer)) to ho
            Set phoUIYieldTarget of ho to Self
            Get PlanFromMessages of ho aMessages "" to Plan
            Send Destroy of ho
            End
        End

        /* REPORT FUNCTIONS DO NOT BELONG IN A PLAN RUN (Nils 2026-07-26: "many of the report
           functions that also takes a long time, doesn't need to be run again for each
           refactoring plan"). PlanFromSavedRows admits eModSuggest rows as well as eModAutoFix
           ones, and EVERY Deep Scan rule that names a report function is Suggest-tier - 76 rules
           across 8 reports - so a plan quietly dragged in up to 8 eReportFunctionAll passes. The
           list is de-duplicated, so each ran once rather than once per rule, but each one still
           walks the WHOLE selected file set and changes nothing.

           The cost is the smaller half. A report writes its worksheet DURING the run, and
           functions execute alphabetically within a run: LegacyDbApiReport sorts before
           RefactorDbGridTocDbCJGrid, Win64AddressToPointer sorts after most converters. So every
           worksheet described a HALF-REFACTORED workspace - partly pre-conversion, partly post -
           a state the source never actually held. That is not a stale report you can re-run; it
           is a misleading one.

           Filtered here because this is the single funnel both entry points pass through (the
           guided launch and the dock's own "Refactor Plan" action), and because it sits ABOVE the
           empty-plan check below - a plan that was ONLY reports correctly reports as empty rather
           than running nothing. The Suggest ROWS stay in the grid and the Review band with their
           existing "run the report first" hints, so the user runs a report deliberately, against
           source that is holding still.

           asFunctionHints is rebuilt in lockstep and padded where a hint is missing: downstream
           code indexes both arrays by the same i, and a short hints array faults with err 4509. */
        Get Create (RefClass(cFunctionsDataDictionary)) to hoDD
        Move (SizeOfArray(Plan.asFunctions) - 1) to iCount
        For i from 0 to iCount
            Get IsReportOnlyFunction of hoDD Plan.asFunctions[i] to bIsReport
            If (bIsReport = True) Begin
                Move Plan.asFunctions[i] to asDroppedFns[-1]
            End
            Else Begin
                Move "" to sHint
                If (i <= (SizeOfArray(Plan.asFunctionHints) - 1)) ;
                    Move Plan.asFunctionHints[i] to sHint
                Move Plan.asFunctions[i] to asKeepFns[-1]
                Move sHint               to asKeepHints[-1]
            End
        Loop
        Send Destroy of hoDD
        Move asKeepFns   to Plan.asFunctions
        Move asKeepHints to Plan.asFunctionHints

        If ((SizeOfArray(Plan.asFunctions) = 0) or (SizeOfArray(Plan.asFiles) = 0)) Begin
            Set piPlanBuildStartTick to 0
            Send StopBuildFeedback
            Set StatusText of hoStatusBar to ""
            // Guided launch: stay silent and hand the outcome back - the guided surface says what
            // this means (its list is stale) and what to do next, instead of a bare info box.
            Set pbAutoSelectPlanEmpty to True
            If (bGuidedLaunch = False) ;
                Send FTInfoBox "No auto-fixable warnings or errors were found to refactor."
            Procedure_Return
        End

        /* THE SELECT COMPANION BELONGS IN THE PLAN, not sprung after it (Nils 2026-07-26: the plan
           dialog said 37 functions and the engine's start question then said 38). The engine pulls
           DefineSelectMacro in at run time whenever an indicator converter runs under eModernizeSelect
           and the scope really holds a [Select] / Indicate Select - correct, because a bare Select only
           compiles where the SELECT macro is defined, but INVISIBLE, and the user could not see which
           function had appeared. In the guided flow nothing here was picked by hand anyway: the plan IS
           our logic's choice, so a companion it requires belongs on the list the user reviews.
           Same two conditions as the engine, in the same order, so the cheap Parameter read gates the
           expensive source scan (the eLeaveSelect case - the common one - never reads the file set).
           The engine still calls its own SelectDefineSelectMacro; selecting an already-listed function
           is idempotent, so preview and run agree either way. */
        Get Create (RefClass(cFunctionsDataDictionary)) to hoDD
        Get HasModernizeSelectConverterInList of hoDD Plan.asFunctions to bModSelect
        Send Destroy of hoDD
        If (bModSelect = True and SearchArray("DefineSelectMacro", Plan.asFunctions) = -1 and ghoFuncLib <> 0) Begin
            Get _ScopeHasSelectIndicator of ghoFuncLib Plan.asFiles to bHasSelect
            If (bHasSelect = True) Begin
                Move "DefineSelectMacro" to Plan.asFunctions[-1]
                // Keep asFunctionHints PARALLEL to asFunctions (downstream indexes both by the same i).
                Move "Defines the SELECT macro the [Select] conversion leaves behind - required for the result to compile" ;
                    to Plan.asFunctionHints[-1]
            End
        End

        // Review + SELECT the plan in the DD-bound dialog (functions all ticked, editable
        // Parameter column; affected files). It returns the user's ticked subset. The editable
        // Parameter column replaces the old parameterized-functions heads-up box.
        // Leave the marquee + wait cursor RUNNING into ShowPlan so they cover the dialog list-load too;
        // the dialog stops them (StopBuildFeedback via phoTestCompileFT) once its grids load. Belt-stop below.
        // The elapsed status stays up INTO ShowPlan for the same reason - StopBuildFeedback finalizes it to
        // "Ready. Refactoring Plan loaded in Ns." once the dialog's grids have loaded.
        Set StatusText of hoStatusBar to ("Loading Refactoring Plan... (" + String((GetTickCount() - piPlanBuildStartTick(Self)) / 1000) + "s)")
        /* OPEN HONEST (Nils 2026-08-16: the board said 67 rows convert automatically, the dialog opened
           with 8 functions ticked and 756 files - "Is that really correct?"). Review rows ride in the
           same plan by design ("applied, but check the result"), but they are the user's to opt INTO:
           tick the functions that have an Auto-fix row; list the Review ones unticked; and show only the
           files the ticked functions need. When the plan has no Auto-fix function at all (a Review-only
           board) everything ticks, as before. The .prn-fallback path has no rows and keeps the old shape. */
        Move Plan.asFiles to asShowFiles
        If (SizeOfArray(aPlanRows) > 0) Begin
            Move (SizeOfArray(aPlanRows) - 1) to iRowMax
            For iRowFn from 0 to iRowMax
                If (aPlanRows[iRowFn].eCategory = eModAutoFix) Begin
                    Move (Trim(aPlanRows[iRowFn].sFn)) to sRowFns
                    While (sRowFns <> "")
                        Move (Pos(", ", sRowFns)) to iP
                        If (iP > 0) Begin
                            Move (Trim(Left(sRowFns, iP - 1)))                    to sRowFn
                            Move (Trim(Mid(sRowFns, (Length(sRowFns)), (iP + 2)))) to sRowFns
                        End
                        Else Begin
                            Move sRowFns to sRowFn
                            Move ""      to sRowFns
                        End
                        // ... unless the user's "never pick" list names it (2026-09-23): then it is
                        // not pre-ticked and its files are not shown - the dialog lists it unticked.
                        If (sRowFn <> "" and SearchArray(sRowFn, Plan.asFunctions, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) <> -1 and SearchArray(sRowFn, asAutoFns, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1 and IsNeverPickFunction(ghoApplication, sRowFn) = False) ;
                            Move sRowFn to asAutoFns[-1]
                    Loop
                End
            Loop
            /* THE INDICATOR COMPANION RIDES WITH AN INDICATOR CONVERTER, never on its own (Nils
               2026-09-11: a plan run of "2 changes" ran it beside ChangeDfTimerTocTimer and changed
               0 lines). The plan carries it because an indicator converter matched SOMEWHERE - on
               that board a Review row, listed unticked - and it was pre-ticked whenever ANY Auto-fix
               function was, so it ran alone on files no indicator converter touched. It declares
               only what those converters leave undeclared; with none ticked it has nothing to do.
               Ticked in the dialog by hand, it still runs. */
            Move False to bIndConv
            Get Create (RefClass(cModAnalyzer)) to ho
            Move (SizeOfArray(asAutoFns) - 1) to iP
            For iRowFn from 0 to iP
                If (_IsIndicatorConverterName(ho, asAutoFns[iRowFn])) ;
                    Move True to bIndConv
            Loop
            Send Destroy of ho
            If (bIndConv = True and SearchArray("DeclareRefactoredIndicators", Plan.asFunctions) <> -1 and SearchArray("DeclareRefactoredIndicators", asAutoFns) = -1) ;
                Move "DeclareRefactoredIndicators" to asAutoFns[-1]
            If (SizeOfArray(asAutoFns) > 0) Begin
                Set pasPlanPreTicked of oRefactorPlanDialog to asAutoFns
                Get Create (RefClass(cModAnalyzer)) to ho
                Get FilesForFunctions of ho (&aPlanRows) (&aFloods) aIgnored asAutoFns to asShowFiles
                Send Destroy of ho
                If (SizeOfArray(asShowFiles) = 0) ;
                    Move Plan.asFiles to asShowFiles
            End
        End
        Get ShowPlan of oRefactorPlanDialog Plan.asFunctions asShowFiles "" to bProceed
        Send StopBuildFeedback
        If (bProceed = False) ;
            Procedure_Return

        Get SelectedFunctions of oRefactorPlanDialog to asSelFns
        Get SelectedFiles     of oRefactorPlanDialog to asSelFiles
        If ((SizeOfArray(asSelFns) = 0) or (SizeOfArray(asSelFiles) = 0)) ;
            Procedure_Return
        /* ONLY THE FILES THE TICKED FUNCTIONS NEED (Nils 2026-08-16: he approved the auto-fixes of a
           plan whose 784 files came mostly from Review rows - "Total number of files changed: 31 out
           of 784 Files ... a big waste of time"). The dialog's file list is the union of every row's
           file whatever was unticked among the functions; the run now opens a file only when at
           least one TICKED function has an actionable row in it (FilesForFunctions - the plan's own
           predicate, per function), intersected with the dialog's own ticks so a file the user
           unticked stays out. The .prn-fallback path has no rows and keeps the full list. */
        If (SizeOfArray(aPlanRows) > 0) Begin
            // The files the TICKED functions need (a Review function ticked in the dialog brings its
            // files in here, though the dialog listed only the pre-ticked set's) ...
            Get Create (RefClass(cModAnalyzer)) to ho
            Get FilesForFunctions of ho (&aPlanRows) (&aFloods) aIgnored asSelFns to asNeeded
            Send Destroy of ho
            // ... minus the files the user UNTICKED among those the dialog showed.
            Move (SizeOfArray(asShowFiles) - 1) to iCount
            For i from 0 to iCount
                If (SearchArray(asShowFiles[i], asSelFiles, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1) ;
                    Move asShowFiles[i] to asUnticked[-1]
            Loop
            Move (SizeOfArray(asNeeded) - 1) to iCount
            For i from 0 to iCount
                If (SearchArray(asNeeded[i], asUnticked, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1) ;
                    Move asNeeded[i] to asKept[-1]
            Loop
            Move asKept to asSelFiles
            If (SizeOfArray(asSelFiles) = 0) Begin
                Send FTInfoBox "None of the ticked files has a finding for the ticked function(s) - nothing to run. Tick a function that has rows in these files, or leave the file list as the plan proposed."
                Procedure_Return
            End
        End

        // Select exactly the TICKED functions (UnSelect first, so the run is only these).
        // SNAPSHOT the user's standing selection FIRST and RESTORE it after the run (Nils
        // 2026-07-16, the LegacyApp2 incident): Functions.Selected is PERSISTENT, and the plan's
        // ticks used to replace the user's set and then LINGER - the next MANUAL run silently
        // ran the whole leftover plan set too (a Tidy Up with ONE function ticked ran 8: the
        // 7 stale plan functions were invisible behind the view's group filter). A plan run is
        // a transient, file-precise operation - it must leave the standing selection exactly
        // as it found it.
        Get Create (RefClass(cFunctionsDataDictionary)) to hoDD
        Get SelectedPublishedFunctionNames of hoDD to asPrevSelFns
        Send UnSelectAllRecords of hoDD
        Send SelectRecordsFromStringArray of hoDD asSelFns
        Send Destroy of hoDD

        // File-precise tRefactorFiles: exactly the TICKED files + their distinct folders.
        Move (SizeOfArray(asSelFiles) - 1) to iCount
        For i from 0 to iCount
            Move asSelFiles[i] to RefactorFiles.asFullFileNames[i]
            Get ParseFolderName asSelFiles[i] to sFolder
            If (SearchArray(sFolder, RefactorFiles.asFolders) = -1) ;
                Move sFolder to RefactorFiles.asFolders[-1]
        Loop

        // NOTE (Nils 2026-07-11): the plan run used to TICK its folders into the folder-selection
        // grid here ("so the grid reflects the session") - REMOVED. The run itself is file-precise
        // (RefactorFiles above) and never needed it, and the silent tick kept WIDENING the user's
        // standing folder selection - which then silently widened the next Deep Scan and manual
        // run. Folder scope is the USER's decision alone; only the FUNCTION ticks above remain
        // (the engine genuinely runs the selected functions).

        If (phoRefactorView(ghoApplication) <> 0) Begin
            Send DoFreshGrids of (phoFoldersView(ghoApplication))
            Send DoFreshGrids of (phoRefactorView(ghoApplication))
        End

        Get phoEditor of ghoApplication to hoEditor
        // Run SOURCE for the summary log's "Run source:" line (Nils 2026-07-15): BOTH Compile+Analyze and Deep
        // Scan reach here, so stamp WHICH one. A LIVE run is deep-scan-sourced when the grid is in scan view
        // (pbDeepScanView = bDSMode); a SAVED-plan run ("View/Run last plan") reads its provenance off the frozen
        // file (SavedPlan.bDeepScan). bDSMode is ALSO the bar's live scan LOOP flag, reused for pbScanPlanRan below.
        Move False to bDSMode
        If (bSaved = False and ghoModAnalyzerDock <> 0) ;
            Get pbDeepScanView of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to bDSMode
        If (bDSMode = True or (bSaved = True and SavedPlan.bDeepScan = True)) ;
            Set peRunOrigin of ghoEngine to eRunDeepScan
        Else ;
            Set peRunOrigin of ghoEngine to eRunPlan
        Send StartEngine of ghoEngine RefactorFiles hoEditor
        // The run may have rewritten source, so BOTH campaign-exit latches are stale now - un-latch
        // them (the honesty rule behind MaybeSuggestTidyUp: each lane's verdict must be re-earned
        // AFTER the last source change before the Tidy Up offer may fire). Cheap in practice: the
        // auto verify re-scan below and the post-run recompile re-latch them as the ladder's own
        // next rungs. Applies to cancelled runs too - fixes may already have been applied.
        Set psCompileLaneCleanWS to ""
        Set psScanLaneDoneWS     to ""
        // RESTORE the user's pre-plan selection (see the snapshot above) - the plan's transient
        // ticks must never leak into the next manual run. ResetSysFile then recounts the
        // selection totals the dashboard's "Functions selected" pill reads (the run summary is
        // already written by now, so re-zeroing the run counters loses nothing).
        Get Create (RefClass(cFunctionsDataDictionary)) to hoDD
        Send UnSelectAllRecords of hoDD
        Send SelectRecordsFromStringArray of hoDD asPrevSelFns
        Send ResetSysFile of hoDD
        Send Destroy of hoDD
        // NOTE (Nils 2026-07-11): scan MODE is deliberately NOT cleared here - the Deep Scan <-> Run Fix Plan
        // loop stays, so the bar's Deep Scan chip is still the one-click RE-SCAN door. The deliberate EXITS
        // from scan mode: Analyze (re-reads the last compile output), the dashboard's Compile step, a saved-plan
        // view. BUT (Nils 2026-07-15) a plan that APPLIED changed the source, so the scan grid is now stale and
        // Compile should light to VERIFY the fixes - the mirror of the compile path's post-run recompile. Set
        // pbScanPlanRan when the run was the LIVE scan loop (bDSMode above): RefreshActionButtons then lights Compile
        // + "recompile to verify" and SyncDeepScanLockdown re-enables the combo, WITHOUT leaving scan mode.
        If (bDSMode = True) Begin
            Set pbScanPlanRan to True
            // AUTO VERIFY RE-SCAN (Nils 2026-07-16): the plan changed the source, and the incremental
            // cache makes re-reading only the touched files a matter of seconds - so close the loop
            // NOW instead of leaving the stale pre-run grid up. Deferred one tick (fresh stack, after
            // this call unwinds); RunDeepScanVerify replays the confirmed scan scope with no confirm.
            // pbScanPlanRan above stays the safety net: if the user cancels the verify scan (or no
            // prior scope exists this session), the bar's manual "recompile to verify" nudge remains.
            // Fires for ANY run outcome - even a cancelled run may have applied fixes, and a fresh
            // grid is the honest state either way.
            Send ArmAction of oButtonDeferTimer CI_TCDeferVerifyScan
        End
        // The engine stamped the plan run (WritePlanRunStamp) - re-render the strip NOW so the
        // bar state is fresh the moment the run dialog closes (the engine's own RefreshAfterRun
        // broadcast reaches the MDI views, not this dock).
        Send SyncWebButtonStates
    End_Procedure

    /* THE LAUNCH BOOKKEEPING StartCompile and TryStartPrecompile share - kept in lockstep by hand until 2026-10-10:
       the working folder (the target's Programs folder, so any compiler-relative output lands next to the .exe;
       the workspace home when Programs was not resolved - a DF26 .sws that omits "programs" AND no convention
       default applied; cRDCAsyncProcess.StartProcess treats "" / "." as "use current directory", but the right
       folder explicitly is safer than whatever CWD DFRefactor has at the moment), the command line recorded for
       the user (psCompiledCmdLine - the df-cli command itself, not the cmd.exe wrapper that captures its stdout:
       the wrapper is transport, not what the user wants to debug), the stdout log in DFRefactor's OWN Programs
       folder (always writable, locale-independent; overwritten per compile, never deleted, so the dock can be
       reopened) and the tick counters UpdateCompileProgress runs on. */
    Procedure _ArmCompileLaunch tSwsFile TargetWS String sCmdPath String sCmdArgs String ByRef sWorkDir String ByRef sLogFile
        tSwsFile OrgWS
        Boolean bOK

        Get PrimaryPrograms of ghoApplication TargetWS to sWorkDir
        If (sWorkDir = "") ;
            Move TargetWS.RuntimePaths.sHome to sWorkDir
        Set psCompiledCmdLine to (sCmdPath + " " + sCmdArgs)
        Get pOwnSws of ghoApplication to OrgWS
        Get PrimaryPrograms of ghoApplication OrgWS to sLogFile
        Move (vFolderFormat(sLogFile) + "dfrefactor_compile.log") to sLogFile
        If (vFilePathExists(sLogFile)) ;
            Move (rfDeleteFile(sLogFile)) to bOK
        Set psCompileLogFile to sLogFile
        Set piCompileTicks   to 0
        Set piCompileStartTick to (GetTickCount())
        Set piLastLogSize    to 0
    End_Procedure

    /* THE FAILED-COMPILE ENDING both exits of FinishCompile share - a non-zero exit that may have left a .err,
       and a zero exit that did not compile. A LINK-ONLY .err (the code compiled; the .exe stage failed on a
       manifest or an image) gets the three offers, each of which may relaunch the compile - True then, and the
       caller returns at once; else the link-only result. Any other failure gets sHeadline in red. A .err then
       goes to the dock: the streamed output capped with the parsed errors for df-cli, the error dialog for a
       legacy compiler. The no-.err case stays with each caller - they end it differently. */
    Function _ShowCompileFailure String sProgram tSwsFile TargetWS String sLogFile String sErrPath String sHeadline Returns Boolean
        Boolean bLinkOnly bManifestFixing

        Move False to bLinkOnly
        If (sErrPath <> "") ;
            Get IsLinkOnlyFailure sErrPath to bLinkOnly
        If (bLinkOnly = True) Begin
            // The offers below pop a question: stop the busy marquee FIRST so it does not keep running
            // behind a modal (Nils 2026-09-18). StartCompile re-arms it when an offer relaunches the compile.
            Send SetBusyMarquee False
            Get OfferAndFixMissingManifest sProgram TargetWS sErrPath to bManifestFixing
            If (bManifestFixing = True) ;
                Function_Return True
            // A linked image the .cfg lists exists nowhere (DacAbout.bmp under DF26): offer to drop it.
            Get OfferAndFixMissingLinkImages sProgram TargetWS sErrPath to bManifestFixing
            If (bManifestFixing = True) ;
                Function_Return True
            // Manifest present but the compiler couldn't ADD it (df-cli x86 defect): offer BuildManifestFile=0
            Get OfferBuildWithoutManifest sProgram TargetWS sErrPath to bManifestFixing
            If (bManifestFixing = True) ;
                Function_Return True
            Send ShowLinkOnlyResult sProgram TargetWS sErrPath
        End
        Else Begin
            Send SetResultText sHeadline
            Send SetResultColor clRed
        End
        If (sErrPath <> "") Begin
            Send SetErrFile sErrPath
            If (pbStreamOutput(Self) = True) Begin
                // df-cli: keep the streamed output, capped with the parsed errors (Studio-style).
                Send FeedDockCompileWarnings sProgram TargetWS
                Send ActivateCombinedCompileOutput of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sLogFile sErrPath
            End
            Else Begin
                Send ActivateCompileErrorDialog of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sErrPath
            End
        End
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    // Kick off a compile and return immediately. The actual compiler
    // runs as a hidden background process (cRDCAsyncProcess) so the dialog's
    // message pump keeps draining - which is what lets oCompilePoll_tm
    // tick the elapsed time and live output. (The busy marquee itself no
    // longer needs the pump - it is a CSS animation on WebView2's own
    // compositor.) The post-mortem (success/fail message and
    // .err file load) runs from FinishCompile, fired by oCompilePoll_tm
    // once the child process has exited.
    Procedure StartCompile
        String sProgram sErrorText sCmdPath sCmdArgs sWorkDir sLogFile sLaunchArgs sErrPath sSWS
        String sPlat sBanName sBanPath sBanBits s32Suffix s64Suffix sErrAlt sPrnPrimary sPrnAlt sNoCompCheck sChainPkg
        Integer iNoCompAnswer
        Boolean bPrnArmed bVoid
        UChar[] ucaEmpty
        String[] asEmpty asProjects
        Boolean bOK
        tSwsFile TargetWS

        // New compile generation: makes the Compiler Output grid's view signature differ from the previous
        // compile's, so its highlight keeper drops a stale selection (e.g. a warning) instead of re-pinning it
        // when errors are added on a recompile - the loader then keeps its error-summary/first-error position.
        Move (ghiCompileGen + 1) to ghiCompileGen

        // Program to compile = CS_LastCompileKeyPrefix, written by the Check Workspace view's main-program
        // picker (the program combo that used to live on this bar is gone - one setting, one source of truth).
        Get psSWSFile of ghoApplication to sSWS
        Get ReadString of ghoApplication CS_Settings (CS_LastCompileKeyPrefix + sSWS) "" to sProgram
        // Nothing picked yet (e.g. F5 on a fresh workspace, now the program combo is gone): fall back to
        // the workspace's FIRST project so a compile still has a sensible target.
        If (sProgram = "" and sSWS <> "") Begin
            // The first program ON DISK (2026-09-23), as the board's combo and its auto-pick do.
            Get WorkspaceProgramsOnDisk of ghoApplication sSWS to asProjects
            If (SizeOfArray(asProjects) > 0) ;
                Move asProjects[0] to sProgram
        End
        If (sProgram = "") Begin
            Send SetResultText "Please pick a main program first (Select Program, on the Guided Dashboard)."
            Send SetResultColor clRed
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // Compile against the user-selected target workspace. The dialog
        // is only enabled (see oTestCompile_btn.IsEnabled) when psSWSFile
        // is set, so pTargetWS is populated here.
        Get pCurrentSws of ghoApplication to TargetWS

        /* RETIRED 2026-07-28: the "we rewrote a precompiled source, offer to precompile it" prompt.
           It predicted a failure the compiler does not have. Settled by experiment on DF25 x64
           (DfCompConsole, minimal #HEADER program): change a symbol INSIDE the header and the next
           compile says "Header for <program> not found, compiling whole program" and recompiles the
           lot - 0 errors, 0 warnings, correct output. It never compiles against the stale symbol
           table, so the phantom errors this guarded against cannot occur. The belief came from
           LegacyApp's 389 phantoms, which were later root-caused to stranded object-level
           declarations (05c7b82); the theory outlived its evidence.

           It also fired wrongly: the note was armed from the run's whole SCOPE rather than the files
           written, so any run including the .src re-armed it whether or not a byte changed - and the
           existence test looked for <base>.pkd while the 64-bit compiler writes <base>.x64.pkd, so
           on an x64 workspace it could only ever be triggered by a leftover 32-bit artifact the
           compile does not consult.

           Error 4522 is a DIFFERENT thing and is untouched: a real, loud, specific compiler error
           about a stale PACKAGE .pkd. We still react to that (TryStartPrecompile below). */

        // Clean any old .err from the last attempt.
        Get DeleteCompileErrorsFile of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS to bOK
        If (bOK = False) Begin
            Send SetResultText "Could not delete old .err file. Aborting."
            Send SetResultColor clRed
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // Resolve compiler exe + arg string up front. Catches the
        // "compiler not found" case before any async machinery spins up.
        Get BuildCompileCommand of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS ;
            (&sErrorText) (&sCmdPath) (&sCmdArgs) to bOK
        If (bOK = False) Begin
            // NO COMPILER SELECTED = ASK + OPEN THE PICKER (Nils 2026-07-17, "make it pointed"):
            // the plain red text in the result area was easy to miss, and the fix is one dialog
            // away - so offer Compiler Settings directly, and when a compiler actually got picked
            // RETRY the compile at once. A No, or closing the dialog without picking, just leaves
            // the plain message - no nagging loop. (The path-not-found case keeps the plain text:
            // there the setup EXISTS and points somewhere broken, a different conversation.)
            Get psCompiler of ghoApplication to sNoCompCheck
            If (sNoCompCheck = "") Begin
                Get FTYesNoBox "No compiler is selected.\n\nOpen Compiler Settings now to pick the DataFlex version to compile with?" "No compiler selected" to iNoCompAnswer
                If (iNoCompAnswer = MBR_Yes) Begin
                    Send Popup of (oCompilerSetup_dg(Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)))
                    Get psCompiler of ghoApplication to sNoCompCheck
                    If (sNoCompCheck <> "") Begin
                        Send StartCompile
                        Procedure_Return
                    End
                End
            End
            Send SetResultText sErrorText
            Send SetResultColor clRed
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // Prime the result area and kick the marquee. The Compile button
        // stays ENABLED throughout the compile - its label flips to
        // "&Stop" right after a successful launch (see below) so the same
        // button can also kill the running compiler.
        // A compile that actually launches invalidates the prior-analysis flag (bookkeeping - the chip
        // gates read the grid's pbAnalyzeDataReady, so the triage chips stay usable on the still-loaded
        // previous analyze while this compile runs; only Analyze dims until the fresh output lands).
        // Set AFTER the guard early-returns so a failed launch (no program / bad compiler) changes nothing.
        Set pbAnalyzed to False
        Set pbScanPlanRan to False   // a real compile supersedes the "scan plan applied -> recompile to verify" nudge
        // A compile REPLACES the (now-stale) Deep Scan view with compiler output, so leave the scan loop cleanly:
        // drop the scan grid + mode (guarded no-op outside scan mode). Reachable now that the post-plan-run bar
        // lights Compile in scan mode - and it also tidies an F5 compile taken mid-scan. Nils 2026-07-15.
        If (ghoModAnalyzerDock <> 0) ;
            Send ClearScanView of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock))
        Set pbCancelled to False
        Set pbAnalyzeWhenFinalized to False   // a parked analyze belongs to the PREVIOUS compile
        // Chained from a green precompile? (Read-and-clear; set by FinishCompile's
        // precompile-success branch just before it Sends StartCompile.) The result text then
        // carries the precompile verdict forward instead of a bare "Compiling...".
        Get psChainedFromPkg to sChainPkg
        Set psChainedFromPkg to ""
        If (sChainPkg <> "") Begin
            Send SetResultText ("Precompiled " * sChainPkg * "- compiling " * sProgram * "...")
        End
        Else If (piCompileRetries(Self) > 0) Begin
            Send SetResultText ("Re-compiling " * sProgram * " (retry)...")
        End
        Else Begin
            Send SetResultText ("Compiling " * sProgram * "...")
        End
        Send SetResultColor (FTThemeInk())
        // New compile: drop the previous compile's chip band and reveal the "Compiling..." progress text (the band
        // overlays it on the Compiler tab). LoadCombined / LoadData re-raise the band with this run's totals.
        // Zero the totals too - the warnings count is only re-fed when a cache (or df-cli's full read)
        // delivers one, so without this a cache-less run would wear the PREVIOUS compile's warnings chip.
        Set pbCompileSummaryReady of ghoCompilerOutputDock to False
        Set piCompileErrors       of ghoCompilerOutputDock to 0
        Set piLinkErrors          of ghoCompilerOutputDock to 0
        Set piCompileWarnings     of ghoCompilerOutputDock to 0
        Send ShowAnalyzeSummary False
        // New compile: clear the bottom .err readout + disable Open, and hide any error dock
        // left from a previous compile. The live "still working" signal is the elapsed-time
        // counter in the result line (UpdateCompileProgress).
        Send SetErrFile ""
        Send CloseCompileErrorDialog of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
        Send SetBusyMarquee True

        // Remember the in-flight compile so FinishCompile can finish it.
        Set psCompilingProgram to sProgram
        Set pCompilingWS       to TargetWS

        // Feed the output dock the Studio-style banner inputs: program name (".src" ensured), its full
        // AppSrc path, and the target bitness (from the program's .cfg [Compiler]Platform - contains "64"
        // -> 64-bit). The live stream (LoadRawOutput) and the final combined view (LoadCombined) print
        // "----Compiling <prog> (<bits>) ----" + "- Compiling Program: <path>" from these. Elapsed
        // seconds is set later by FinishCompile, once it is known. Set BEFORE ShowCompileOutputLive
        // below, so the banner shows from the first paint.
        Move sProgram to sBanName
        If (not(Uppercase(sBanName) contains ".SRC")) ;
            Move (sBanName + ".src") to sBanName
        Get AppSrcFolderFor of ghoApplication TargetWS sBanName to sBanPath
            Move (vFolderFormat(sBanPath) + sBanName) to sBanPath
        /* Banner bitness: ASK THE SAME FUNCTION THE COMMAND ASKS (Nils 2026-08-20). This used to
           read the cfg [Compiler]Platform for df-cli and psCompilerBitness for the legacy
           compilers - two sources for one fact. Once the build started passing --toolchain, the
           cfg stopped deciding anything for df-cli, and WinServ (no Platform line) was announced
           as "Compiling WINSERV.SRC (32-bit)" while writing WINSERVx64.exe.
           EffectiveCompilePlatform is the single answer both the banner and the command line
           derive from, so a build cannot be labelled as something it is not. It also still
           covers the 2026-07-08 case that put the old rule here: an x64 DfCompConsole with a
           cfg that names no Platform - the exe decides, and it says 64. */
        Get EffectiveCompilePlatform of ghoApplication to sPlat
        If (sPlat contains "64") Begin
            Move "64-bit" to sBanBits
        End
        Else Begin
            Move "32-bit" to sBanBits
        End
        Set psCompileProgramName of ghoCompilerOutputDock to sBanName
        Set psCompileProgramPath of ghoCompilerOutputDock to sBanPath
        Set psCompileBitness     of ghoCompilerOutputDock to sBanBits
        Set piCompileSeconds     of ghoCompilerOutputDock to 0
        Set pbCompileIsPrecompile of ghoCompilerOutputDock to False
        Set psCompiledExe        of ghoCompilerOutputDock to ""   // a fresh compile clears the success footer's exe
        // A chained compile KEEPS the precompile phase note FinishCompile just fed (the output
        // then reads pre-compile-succeeded -> compiling); any other compile clears it so a stale
        // note from an earlier chain can't head an unrelated build's output.
        If (sChainPkg = "") ;
            Set psCompileChainNote of ghoCompilerOutputDock to ""

        // Temporary output-suffix injection (cRefactorApplication.InjectCfgSuffixes): sweep any crash
        // leftover FIRST (breadcrumb-driven), then inject the Compiler Setup suffixes into a legacy .cfg
        // that has none - so this compile writes Studio-style suffixed output (Skinningx64.exe). Restored
        // at FinishCompile; a retry relaunch passes through here again and re-injects.
        Send RestoreInjectedCfgSuffixes of ghoApplication
        Get InjectCfgSuffixes of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS to bVoid
        // Same pattern for the manifest toggle (AFTER the suffixes - the manifest name reads them):
        // a cfg saying EmbedManifestFile=1 + BuildManifestFile=0 whose <output>.exe.manifest is
        // absent gets BuildManifestFile=1 for THIS compile only, so the toolchain generates the
        // missing manifest itself instead of failing the link. Restored at FinishCompile.
        Send RestoreInjectedCfgManifestBuild of ghoApplication
        Get InjectCfgManifestBuild of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS to bVoid
        /* And force compiler warnings ON for this compile. Since the switch to
           "build --target", df-cli honours the project .cfg - so a project that sets
           [Application]CompilerWarnings=0 (WinServ does) produced a listing with
           "Total Warnings : 0" and left the Analyzer nothing to read. Warnings are the
           product here. Swept-then-injected in the same order as the manifest key above,
           so a crash leftover is restored before a new one is taken. */
        Send RestoreInjectedCfgCompilerWarnings of ghoApplication
        Get InjectCfgCompilerWarnings of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS to bVoid

        // Launch hidden + non-blocking (the last StartProcess arg, bShowWindow = False, keeps the compiler
        // off-screen). The working folder, the recorded command line, the stdout log and the counters are
        // _ArmCompileLaunch's; UpdateCompileProgress refreshes the live error dock from the log as it grows.
        Send _ArmCompileLaunch TargetWS sCmdPath sCmdArgs (&sWorkDir) (&sLogFile)

        // Cache the legacy compile's .err path (same formula FinishCompile uses) + reset the live
        // size tracker, so UpdateCompileProgress can poll it for live error display. The .err was
        // just deleted above (BOTH suffix candidates), so it starts absent until the first error is
        // written. Prediction follows the cfg's [Compiler]Platform, but the compiler decides for
        // itself - so ALSO cache the OTHER 32/64-suffix candidate and let the poll adopt whichever
        // actually grows (resolve-by-existence can't apply before the file exists).
        Get PredictedCompiledFile of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "err" to sErrPath
        Set psLiveErrPath to sErrPath
        Get CfgCompileValue of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "32BitSuffix" ""    to s32Suffix
        Get CfgCompileValue of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "64BitSuffix" "x64" to s64Suffix
        Get CompiledFileCandidate of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "err" s32Suffix to sErrAlt
        If (sErrAlt = sErrPath) ;
            Get CompiledFileCandidate of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "err" s64Suffix to sErrAlt
        Set psLiveErrPathAlt to sErrAlt
        Set piLastErrSize to 0

        // ---- Arm the live .prn tail (ALL compilers) ----
        // Delete BOTH stale .prn suffix candidates FIRST: the compiler recreates the listing, and a
        // leftover multi-MB .prn from the previous build must never be tailed as if it were new output
        // (mirrors the .err delete above). If a stale one cannot be deleted (locked by a viewer), the
        // tail is DISARMED for this run - every consumer then falls back to the full post-compile read,
        // exactly today's behaviour. The predicted name + the other-suffix candidate follow the same
        // adopt-on-growth pattern as the .err tail.
        Move True to bPrnArmed
        Get CompiledFileCandidate of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "prn" s32Suffix to sPrnPrimary
        Get CompiledFileCandidate of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "prn" s64Suffix to sPrnAlt
        If (vFilePathExists(sPrnPrimary)) ;
            Move (rfDeleteFile(sPrnPrimary)) to bVoid
        If (vFilePathExists(sPrnAlt)) ;
            Move (rfDeleteFile(sPrnAlt)) to bVoid
        If (vFilePathExists(sPrnPrimary) or vFilePathExists(sPrnAlt)) ;
            Move False to bPrnArmed
        // ... and the legacy compilers' FIXED ""/"x64" candidates (their .prn naming ignores
        // the cfg suffix keys - see ResolveCompiledFile; Nils 2026-07-09): a stale multi-MB
        // "<base>.x64.prn" must not be tailed as new output now that PredictedCompiledFile
        // watches that name for a legacy compile.
        Get CompiledFileCandidate of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "prn" ""    to sPrnPrimary
        Get CompiledFileCandidate of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "prn" "x64" to sPrnAlt
        If (vFilePathExists(sPrnPrimary)) ;
            Move (rfDeleteFile(sPrnPrimary)) to bVoid
        If (vFilePathExists(sPrnAlt)) ;
            Move (rfDeleteFile(sPrnAlt)) to bVoid
        If (vFilePathExists(sPrnPrimary) or vFilePathExists(sPrnAlt)) ;
            Move False to bPrnArmed
        Get PredictedCompiledFile of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "prn" to sPrnPrimary
        Get CompiledFileCandidate of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "prn" s32Suffix to sPrnAlt
        If (sPrnAlt = sPrnPrimary) ;
            Get CompiledFileCandidate of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "prn" s64Suffix to sPrnAlt
        Set psStreamFloodPrn to ""   // fresh listing, fresh streaming chance
        If (bPrnArmed = True) Begin
            Set psLivePrnPath to sPrnPrimary
            Set psPrnTailNote to ""
        End
        Else Begin
            Set psLivePrnPath to ""
            Set psPrnTailNote to "prn cache OFF: stale listing could not be deleted (locked?)"
        End
        Set psLivePrnPathAlt to sPrnAlt
        Set piPrnTailOffset  to 0
        Set pucaPrnTailCarry to ucaEmpty
        Set pbPrnTailUtf8    to False
        Set pasPrnTailMsgs   to asEmpty
        Set piPrnTailWarn    to 0
        Set piPrnTailErr     to 0
        Set piLastPrnStreamCount to 0   // nothing streamed to the live view yet for THIS compile
        Set pbPrnCacheReady  to False
        Set pbPrnFinalizing  to False
        Set pbStreamDraining to False
        // A NEW compile writes to the SAME .prn path, so the streamer's lazy path-keyed re-arm never
        // fires on the second compile of a session - and its surviving state (cursor, messages, the
        // grid's built rows) would impersonate THIS compile at adoption, stamp-blessed by the fresh
        // tail cache. Live-caught 2026-07-23: a revert-then-recompile analyze served the PREVIOUS
        // compile's rows, hiding exactly the two errors the revert brought back. Invalidate HERE,
        // where the tail state itself resets; the first poll tick re-arms a fresh stream.
        Send InvalidateStreamedRows "new compile"

        // Only df-cli streams progress to stdout (-> the cmd-redirect log; verified: DfCompConsole
        // leaves the redirect log at 0 bytes), so only it drives the stdout-log refresh in
        // UpdateCompileProgress. The legacy DFComp/DFCompConsole get their live view from the
        // OTHER branch there: the growing .err tail, which pops the Compiler Output tab on the
        // FIRST error and refreshes it as errors accumulate (a clean compile never pops the dock).
        If (sCmdPath contains "df-cli") Begin
            Set pbStreamOutput to True
        End
        Else Begin
            Set pbStreamOutput to False
        End

        // Wrap with cmd.exe /c for stdout+stderr redirection. The outer
        // quotes around the /c argument let cmd parse the inner command
        // (which contains > and quoted paths) correctly: cmd strips the
        // first and last " on the /c argument, leaving the real command
        // for parsing.
        Move ('/c " "' + sCmdPath + '" ' + sCmdArgs + ' > "' + sLogFile + '" 2>&1 "') to sLaunchArgs
        Get StartProcess of oCompileProcess "cmd.exe" sLaunchArgs sWorkDir False to bOK
        If (bOK = False) Begin
            Get psLastError of oCompileProcess to sErrorText
            Send SetBusyMarquee False
            Send SetResultText ("Could not launch compiler: " + sErrorText)
            Send SetResultColor clRed
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // We now have a live child process. Hand off to the poll timer; StartCompile returns
        // immediately and the UI is free to repaint (the web marquee animates on WebView2's own
        // compositor - it needs no pumping at all).
        Set pbEnabled of oCompilePoll_tm to True
        Send RefreshActionButtons True    // the Compile button becomes "Stop Compile" (any compiler)

        // df-cli streams to stdout: open the error dock now and show that log live (the poll
        // timer refreshes it; FinishCompile swaps to the parsed .err). Legacy compilers don't
        // stream, so they stay hidden until the .err is ready.
        If (pbStreamOutput(Self) = True) ;
            Send ShowCompileOutputLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sLogFile
        Send SyncWebButtonStates
    End_Procedure

    // Stop button handler (the Compile button acts as Stop while a compile
    // is in flight). Forcibly terminates the compiler child process. The
    // poll timer will see IsProcessRunning go False on its next tick and
    // FinishCompile takes over from there - so all the cleanup (handle
    // close, marquee stop, button-label restore, UI message) happens in
    // exactly one place.
    Procedure KillCompile
        Set pbCancelled to True
        Send SetResultText "Cancelling..."
        Send SetResultColor (FTThemeInk())
        // Non-zero exit code by convention; we use pbCancelled rather
        // than the OS exit code to detect cancellation in FinishCompile
        // (compile failures also produce non-zero codes).
        Send KillProcess of oCompileProcess 1
    End_Procedure

    // Reads the entire compile log file into a String. Returns "" when the
    // file is missing, locked, or empty. Called by FinishCompile after the
    // process exits, to capture whatever df-cli wrote before exiting. (The
    // live, mid-compile refresh reads the log via the shared dock, not here.)
    //
    // Delegates to the byte-safe rfReadEncodedFileAsString helper instead of
    // a local Readln loop: one Read_Block plus a single decode pass avoids
    // both the lossy text-mode pipeline (DF 2025 Unicode mode mangles OEM /
    // extended bytes - see cEncodingHelpers.pkg) and the O(n^2) per-line
    // string concatenation the old loop incurred. The helper already returns
    // "" on a missing / empty / locked file, so a polling caller can just
    // retry on its next tick.
    Function LoadCompileLogTail String sLogFile Returns String
        Function_Return (rfReadEncodedFileAsString(sLogFile))
    End_Function

    // Scans the compiler output (stdout log or .err) for the Error-4522 bail. THREE observed
    // message shapes, one per compiler family/build - never key on one (the recurring lesson):
    //   df-cli stdout:    "... Error 4522: ... , please precompile ORDERPRECOMPILE.PKG"
    //   DfCompConsole A:  "WINSERV.SRC(2,1) Error 4522: ... please precompile E:\...\WINSERV.SRC"
    //   DfCompConsole B (classic .err, x86 DF25 - WinServ 2026-07-19):
    //     "ERROR: 4522 ... please precompile E:\...\WINSERV.SRC ON LINE: 2 (1) OF FILE: E:\...\WINSERV.SRC"
    // Returns the package/source file it asks for ("" when the message is absent): everything from
    // after the "please precompile " marker to the end of that line (names with spaces survive),
    // then CUT at the classic format's " ON LINE:" locator tail - without that cut the tail + the
    // doubled path rode along, the resolve failed on the junk name, and the offer silently never
    // appeared for shape B.
    Function ExtractPrecompilePackage String sLog Returns String
        Integer iPos iEol
        String  sPkg

        Move (Pos("please precompile ", Lowercase(sLog))) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Mid(sLog, Length(sLog), iPos + Length("please precompile "))) to sPkg
        Move (Pos(Character(13), sPkg)) to iEol
        If (iEol > 0) ;
            Move (Left(sPkg, iEol - 1)) to sPkg
        Move (Pos(Character(10), sPkg)) to iEol
        If (iEol > 0) ;
            Move (Left(sPkg, iEol - 1)) to sPkg
        Move (Pos(" on line:", Lowercase(sPkg))) to iEol
        If (iEol > 0) ;
            Move (Left(sPkg, iEol - 1)) to sPkg
        Function_Return (Trim(sPkg))
    End_Function

    // Scans the compiler stdout log for df-cli's OTHER precompile message - the
    // self-healing variant that does NOT bail:
    //   "Warning: Precompiled package ORDERPRECOMPILE.PKG.PKG is not compatible, recompiling."
    // Here df-cli recompiles the package IN MEMORY and continues, but the on-disk
    // .pkd is never rewritten - so the inline recompile (and this warning) repeats
    // on EVERY compile, silently taxing each build. Returns the named package (""
    // when the message is absent), with the message's doubled ".PKG.PKG" extension
    // collapsed to the real ".PKG" file name.
    Function ExtractIncompatiblePrecompilePackage String sLog Returns String
        Integer iPos iEnd
        String  sPkg

        Move (Pos("warning: precompiled package ", Lowercase(sLog))) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Mid(sLog, Length(sLog), iPos + Length("warning: precompiled package "))) to sPkg
        Move (Pos(" is not compatible", Lowercase(sPkg))) to iEnd
        If (iEnd = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Trim(Left(sPkg, iEnd - 1))) to sPkg
        // df-cli doubles the extension in THIS message; the real file has one.
        If (Right(Lowercase(sPkg), 8) = ".pkg.pkg") ;
            Move (Left(sPkg, (Length(sPkg) - 4))) to sPkg
        Function_Return sPkg
    End_Function

    // Incrementally harvest the growing .prn listing WHILE the compiler runs (ANY compiler): read the
    // new bytes via the open-handle slice reader (never disturbs the writer) and keep ONLY the
    // diagnostic lines - rfPrnTailConsume filters natively, so the ~250k source-echo lines of a big
    // listing are never decoded or stored. By FinishCompile the filtered messages are already in
    // memory (see CachedPrn*). TIME-BUDGETED (~50 ms per poll, as many 512 KB chunks as fit): the
    // listing is written FASTER than one small chunk per tick - DfCompConsole streams LegacyApp's 60 MB
    // .prn at ~2.5 MB/s and the original single 256 KB read per 500 ms tick (0.5 MB/s) fell ~48 MB
    // behind, so the 2 s drain box (correctly) invalidated the cache: the analyze fell back to the
    // full read and no warnings showed (Nils' "full-read" status report 2026-07-03). A 10% duty
    // cycle outruns any compiler while the UI stays responsive; DrainPrnTail mops up the rest.
    /* ---- STREAMING ANALYZE (milestone 3b, 2026-07-23) ----

       The tail harvest already parses the growing .prn DURING the compile; this feeds those
       diagnostics through the full analyze pipeline as they arrive - parse to tCompileError,
       proxy-drop, with-file filter, then the grid's _BuildRowsForSlice - in budgeted bites on the
       same poll timer. By FinishCompile the grid rows are BUILT, and LoadData adopts them instead of
       re-deriving (StreamedMessagesFor): the analyze becomes its cheap post-passes + display.

       Conservative by construction - ANY doubt falls back to the classic path with no behaviour
       change: the stream arms only while the analyzer grid's COM object exists (its column ids are
       meaningless before; so the FIRST analyze of a session is always classic, every later compile
       streams); a collision flood (>= 100) invalidates mid-stream (flooded runs build rows
       differently - LoadData re-checks and rebuilds classically regardless); any tail-cache
       invalidation invalidates the stream with it; and adoption re-validates the stamp
       (PrnCacheMatches) at analyze time. */
    Property tCompileError[] paStreamMessages
    Property tModAnalyzer[]  pStreamRecipes
    Property Handle  phoStreamAnalyzer 0
    Property String  psStreamPrn ""
    Property Integer piStreamCursor 0
    Property Integer piStreamExProxy 0
    Property Integer piStreamExFile 0
    Property Integer piStreamCollisions 0
    Property Boolean pbStreamValid False
    Property Boolean pbStreamComplete False
    /* The listing this session PROVED cannot stream: a collision-flood invalidation. The flood
       is deterministic - same listing, same collisions, same threshold at the same line - so
       re-arming can only grind back to the identical death. Without this latch it did exactly
       that: InvalidateStreamedRows clears psStreamPrn, the next tick's re-arm check read that
       as "new listing" and restarted from line 0 - measured 2026-08-10 on a 280 MB listing:
       12 restart cycles, ~35 s of row-building each, 458 s of finalize in total, every second
       of it discarded because a flood forces the classic rebuild anyway. Cleared when a new
       compile arms the tail (a fresh listing deserves a fresh chance). */
    Property String psStreamFloodPrn ""

    Procedure InvalidateStreamedRows String sReason
        Handle ho
        Set pbStreamValid    to False
        Set pbStreamComplete to False
        Set psStreamPrn      to ""
        Get phoStreamAnalyzer to ho
        If (ho <> 0) ;
            Send Destroy of ho
        Set phoStreamAnalyzer to 0
    End_Procedure

    // Rows still to build for a stream that is valid and not yet complete (see pbStreamDraining).
    Function StreamDrainPending Returns Boolean
        Function_Return (pbStreamValid(Self) = True and pbStreamComplete(Self) = False)
    End_Function

    Procedure StreamAnalyzeTick Boolean bDrain
        tCompileError[] aParsed aStream aEmptyMsgs
        tModAnalyzer[] aRecipes
        String[] asLines asBatch asEmptyStr
        String sPrn
        Handle hoGrid hoAna
        Integer iTotal iCursor iN i iTick iColl iExP iExF iBase iBudget
        Boolean bValid

        Get psLivePrnPath to sPrn
        If (sPrn = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (ghoModAnalyzerDock = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Move (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to hoGrid
        // The grid's column ids exist only once its COM object does - before that, built rows would
        // carry garbage indexes. First analyze of a session creates it; until then, classic path.
        If (not(IsComObjectCreated(hoGrid))) ;
            Procedure_Return
        // Lazy (re-)arm whenever the live listing changes - also covers the alt-candidate adoption
        // (cursor back to 0: the same cached lines simply re-feed a freshly reset builder).
        // A listing that already collision-flooded can never stream (see psStreamFloodPrn) -
        // skip the whole pipeline; the harvest above still fills the .prn cache for the analyze.
        If (sPrn = psStreamFloodPrn(Self)) ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (psStreamPrn(Self) <> sPrn) Begin
            Send InvalidateStreamedRows "re-arm"
            Get Create (RefClass(cModAnalyzer)) to hoAna
            Send EnsureRecipesLoaded of hoAna
            Get paRecipes of hoAna to aRecipes
            Set pStreamRecipes to aRecipes
            Set phoStreamAnalyzer to hoAna
            Set paStreamMessages to aEmptyMsgs
            Set piStreamCursor to 0
            Set piStreamExProxy to 0
            Set piStreamExFile to 0
            Set piStreamCollisions to 0
            Set pbStreamComplete to False
            Set pbStreamValid to True
            Set psStreamPrn to sPrn
            Send _ResetRowBuildState of hoGrid
            Set pbBuildPumpEnabled of hoGrid to False   // this runs ON the pump already
            Set pbBuildFlood       of hoGrid to False   // a flood invalidates the stream instead
        End
        Get pbStreamValid to bValid
        If (bValid = False) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get pasPrnTailMsgs to asLines
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines)) to iTotal
        Get piStreamCursor to iCursor
        // IMPOSSIBLE-STATE GUARD: a cursor BEYOND the tail array means the tail was reset behind the
        // stream's back (a reset path without an invalidation hook). Such a stream must never reach
        // adoption - invalidate; the next tick re-arms cleanly from line 0 of the current tail.
        If (iCursor > iTotal) Begin
            Send InvalidateStreamedRows "tail reset behind the stream (cursor past end)"
            Procedure_Return
        End
        If (iCursor >= iTotal) Begin
            If (bDrain = True) ;
                Set pbStreamComplete to True
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Get phoStreamAnalyzer to hoAna
        Get pStreamRecipes to aRecipes
        Get piStreamCollisions to iColl
        Get piStreamExProxy to iExP
        Get piStreamExFile to iExF
        Move (GetTickCount()) to iTick
        /* The accumulated-message state crosses this call as ONE Get here and ONE Set after the
           Loop - never per batch. The Get/Set pair used to sit INSIDE the batch loop, deep-copying
           the ENTIRE accumulated array to append 200 lines: quadratic, and the reason finalizing a
           200 MB listing crawled at a few MB/s while its own budget comment promised ~28 (Nils
           2026-08-10). Same lesson as the slice builder's pBuild* batching, two pages up. */
        Get paStreamMessages to aStream
        // bDrain = the listing is complete: a larger slice (the compiler is gone, so the timer is ours), and the
        // stream is marked complete once nothing is left. It was UNBUDGETED until 2026-10-03 - one call that held
        // the UI for a minute on a big listing; PrnFinalizeTick now calls it a slice per tick (pbStreamDraining).
        Move 90 to iBudget
        If (bDrain = True) ;
            Move 250 to iBudget
        While (iCursor < iTotal and pbStreamValid(Self) = True and (GetTickCount() - iTick) < iBudget)
            // one batch of raw diagnostic lines -> parsed messages
            Move (iTotal - iCursor) to iN
            If (iN > 200) ;
                Move 200 to iN
            Move asEmptyStr to asBatch
            For i from 0 to (iN - 1)
                Move asLines[iCursor + i] to asBatch[i]
            Loop
            Move (iCursor + iN) to iCursor
            Get MassageErrorFile asBatch to aParsed
            // collision watch: a flood builds rows DIFFERENTLY (summary row, guards skipped) - bail to
            // the classic path the moment the threshold is plausible.
            For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(aParsed) - 1)
                If (aParsed[i].iErrorNo = 4544 or aParsed[i].iErrorNo = 4390) ;
                    Increment iColl
            Loop
            If (iColl >= 100) Begin
                Send InvalidateStreamedRows "collision flood"
                Set psStreamFloodPrn to sPrn   // deterministic for this listing - never re-arm it
            End
            Else Begin
                // the SAME canonical filters the classic path applies, in the same order
                Move (SizeOfArray(aParsed)) to iBase
                Get DropCOMProxyMessages of hoAna (&aParsed) to aParsed
                Move (iExP + (iBase - SizeOfArray(aParsed))) to iExP
                Move (SizeOfArray(aParsed)) to iBase
                Get MessagesWithAFile of hoGrid (&aParsed) to aParsed
                Move (iExF + (iBase - SizeOfArray(aParsed))) to iExF
                If (SizeOfArray(aParsed) > 0) Begin
                    For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(aParsed) - 1)
                        Move aParsed[i] to aStream[-1]
                    Loop
                    Send _BuildRowsForSlice of hoGrid (&aParsed) 0 (SizeOfArray(aParsed) - 1) hoAna (&aRecipes)
                End
            End
        Loop
        Set paStreamMessages to aStream
        Set piStreamCursor to iCursor
        Set piStreamExProxy to iExP
        Set piStreamExFile to iExF
        Set piStreamCollisions to iColl
        If (bDrain = True and iCursor >= iTotal and pbStreamValid(Self) = True) ;
            Set pbStreamComplete to True
    End_Procedure

    /* Adoption gate for the analyzer: hands over the streamed message set when - and only when - the
       stream is complete, still valid, for THIS listing, and the tail stamp still matches the disk.
       On True the grid's pBuild* properties already hold the finished rows (the streamer built them
       through the same _BuildRowsForSlice) and aMsgs is the proxy-dropped, with-file message set with
       its two drop-counts - exactly what the classic path would have produced. */
    Function StreamedMessagesFor String sPrnFile tCompileError[] ByRef aMsgs Integer ByRef iExProxy Integer ByRef iExFile Returns Boolean
        Boolean bMatch

        If (pbStreamValid(Self) = False or pbStreamComplete(Self) = False) ;
            Function_Return False
        If (Lowercase(psStreamPrn(Self)) <> Lowercase(sPrnFile)) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get PrnCacheMatches sPrnFile to bMatch
        If (bMatch = False) Begin
            Send InvalidateStreamedRows "stamp mismatch at adopt"
            Function_Return False
        End
        Get paStreamMessages to aMsgs
        Get piStreamExProxy  to iExProxy
        Get piStreamExFile   to iExFile
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    Procedure PollPrnTail
        String   sPrn sAlt
        Integer  iSize iOffset iChunk iVoid iWarn iErr iBudgetTick
        UChar[]  ucaChunk ucaCarry
        String[] asMsgs
        Boolean  bUtf8 bStop

        Get psLivePrnPath to sPrn
        If (sPrn = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get piPrnTailOffset to iOffset
        Move (rfFileSizeLive(sPrn)) to iSize
        // Prediction miss? Adopt the other 32/64-suffix candidate once it grows (mirrors the .err tail).
        If (iSize = 0 and iOffset = 0) Begin
            Get psLivePrnPathAlt to sAlt
            If (sAlt <> "" and sAlt <> sPrn) Begin
                Move (rfFileSizeLive(sAlt)) to iSize
                If (iSize > 0) Begin
                    Move sAlt to sPrn
                    Set psLivePrnPath to sAlt
                End
            End
        End
        If (iSize <= iOffset) ;
            Procedure_Return
        // First bytes: skip a UTF-8 BOM (a df-cli listing) so it never pollutes line 1's bytes.
        If (iOffset = 0 and iSize >= 3) Begin
            Move (rfReadFileSliceBytes(sPrn, 0, 3)) to ucaChunk
            If (SizeOfArray(ucaChunk) = 3) Begin
                If (ucaChunk[0] = 239 and ucaChunk[1] = 187 and ucaChunk[2] = 191) Begin
                    Set pbPrnTailUtf8 to True
                    Move 3 to iOffset
                    Set piPrnTailOffset to 3
                End
            End
        End
        // Fetch the array state ONCE per poll (not per chunk - the copies are the expensive part).
        Get pucaPrnTailCarry to ucaCarry
        Get pasPrnTailMsgs   to asMsgs
        Get piPrnTailWarn    to iWarn
        Get piPrnTailErr     to iErr
        Get pbPrnTailUtf8    to bUtf8
        // Budget 120 ms / 1 MB chunks: the measured pipeline speed is ~28 MB/s (Nils' trace: 155 MB
        // consumed live over a 55 s compile at the old 50 ms x 2 polls/s = 10% duty), while the DF25
        // compiler WRITES its 281 MB listing at ~5.1 MB/s. 120 ms x 2 polls/s = 24% duty = ~6.7 MB/s
        // of processing - comfortably ahead of the writer, so the drain remainder stays small.
        Move (GetTickCount()) to iBudgetTick
        Move False to bStop
        While (iSize > iOffset and bStop = False and (GetTickCount() - iBudgetTick) < 120)
            Move (iSize - iOffset) to iChunk
            If (iChunk > 1048576) ;
                Move 1048576 to iChunk
            Move (rfReadFileSliceBytes(sPrn, iOffset, iChunk)) to ucaChunk
            If (SizeOfArray(ucaChunk) = 0) Begin
                Move True to bStop                         // read failed/raced - retry next tick
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (iOffset + SizeOfArray(ucaChunk)) to iOffset
                Get rfPrnTailConsume (&ucaChunk) (&ucaCarry) bUtf8 (&asMsgs) (&iWarn) (&iErr) to iVoid
                // Runaway insurance: a sane listing line is short, so a carry past 1 MB means we are
                // NOT looking at line-structured text - disarm rather than balloon memory.
                If (SizeOfArray(ucaCarry) > 1048576) Begin
                    Send InvalidatePrnCache "carry overflow (not line-structured text?)"
                    Procedure_Return
                End
                Move (rfFileSizeLive(sPrn)) to iSize       // the file keeps growing while we read
            End
        Loop
        Set piPrnTailOffset  to iOffset
        Set pucaPrnTailCarry to ucaCarry
        Set pasPrnTailMsgs   to asMsgs
        Set piPrnTailWarn    to iWarn
        Set piPrnTailErr     to iErr
        // milestone 3b: feed newly harvested diagnostics through the analyze pipeline, budgeted.
        Send StreamAnalyzeTick False
    End_Procedure

    // End of compile: the process has exited so the listing is complete - read whatever the budget
    // left over, flush the final (possibly LF-less) line, and STAMP the cache so CachedPrn* can
    // trust the harvest until the file changes on disk. Each PollPrnTail call below burns ~50 ms of
    // native decode (several MB), so the 2 s box covers far more remainder than any real .prn leaves.
    Procedure DrainPrnTail
        String   sPrn
        Integer  iSize iOffset iVoid iWarn iErr iStartTick
        UChar[]  ucaCarry
        String[] asMsgs
        Boolean  bUtf8

        Get psLivePrnPath to sPrn
        If (sPrn = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        // BOUNDED (2 s) so the verdict shows promptly; anything left switches to the BACKGROUND
        // FINALIZE phase (pbPrnFinalizing + the still-armed poll timer): the tail keeps consuming
        // off the timer with a live "Finalizing compiler listing..." status - the UI never freezes
        // and the cache ALWAYS lands, just a few seconds after the verdict when the compile left a
        // big backlog. FinishCompile's own fast-paths see a not-yet-ready cache and fall back to
        // their full reads - exactly the pre-feature behaviour.
        Move (GetTickCount()) to iStartTick
        Move (rfFileSizeLive(sPrn)) to iSize
        Get piPrnTailOffset to iOffset
        While (iSize > iOffset and (GetTickCount() - iStartTick) < 2000)
            Send PollPrnTail
            Get psLivePrnPath to sPrn                 // PollPrnTail may have adopted the alt candidate
            Get piPrnTailOffset to iOffset
            Move (rfFileSizeLive(sPrn)) to iSize
        Loop
        If (iSize > iOffset) Begin
            Set psPrnTailNote to ("prn finalizing in background, " + String((iSize - iOffset) / 1048576) + " MB left")
            Set pbPrnFinalizing to True
            Set pbEnabled of oCompilePoll_tm to True
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Send FinalizePrnCache
    End_Procedure

    // Complete the harvest: flush the final (possibly LF-less) line, STAMP the cache, and reload the
    // visible .err view ONCE so the warnings append (AppendCachedPrnWarnings) actually lands - the
    // mid-compile refreshes load errors-only BY DESIGN (the cache exists only from this point on).
    // Called by DrainPrnTail (fast path) and PrnFinalizeTick (background path).
    Procedure FinalizePrnCache
        String   sPrn
        Integer  iSize iVoid iWarn iErr
        UChar[]  ucaCarry
        String[] asMsgs
        Boolean  bUtf8

        Get psLivePrnPath to sPrn
        If (sPrn = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get pucaPrnTailCarry to ucaCarry
        Get pasPrnTailMsgs   to asMsgs
        Get piPrnTailWarn    to iWarn
        Get piPrnTailErr     to iErr
        Get pbPrnTailUtf8    to bUtf8
        /* THE LAST LINE OF A CRASHED COMPILE IS HALF A LINE (2026-10-05). A listing that does not end in a
           line break normally still ends in a whole line, and that line is flushed and read. After a crash
           it does not: the compiler died with its buffers unwritten, and the carry held
           "ERROR: 52 ... OF FILE: E:\Lib\AppSrc\" - a diagnostic whose file was a folder. Dropped then. */
        If (pbCompileCrashed(Self) = True) ;
            Move (ResizeArray(ucaCarry, 0)) to ucaCarry
        Else ;
            Get rfPrnTailFlushCarry (&ucaCarry) bUtf8 (&asMsgs) (&iWarn) (&iErr) to iVoid
        Set pucaPrnTailCarry to ucaCarry
        Set pasPrnTailMsgs   to asMsgs
        Set piPrnTailWarn    to iWarn
        Set piPrnTailErr     to iErr
        // Stamp - only a listing that actually materialised is a cache (a no-.prn build stays invalid).
        Move (rfFileSizeLive(sPrn)) to iSize
        Set psPrnCacheFile   to sPrn
        Set piPrnCacheSize   to iSize
        Set pdtPrnCacheStamp to (rfFileDate(sPrn))
        Set pbPrnCacheReady  to (iSize > 0)
        If (iSize > 0) Begin
            Set psPrnTailNote to ("prn cached: " + String(SizeOfArray(asMsgs)) + " diagnostics (" + String(iWarn) + " warn/" + String(iErr) + " err)")
            // No-tail reload: this runs at COMPILE END (fast-drain before the verdict, or the
            // background-finalize tick after it) - tail-scrolling here left the LAST row selected
            // on a finished legacy compile instead of the error summary (Nils 2026-07-09).
            Send RefreshErrorOutputFinal of ghoCompilerOutputDock
        End
        Else Begin
            Set psPrnTailNote to "prn cache OFF: no listing was written"
        End
        // milestone 3b: the listing is complete and stamped - build the rows the compile left over. One slice
        // here; whatever remains is built on the timer (PrnFinalizeTick, see pbStreamDraining), with the finalize
        // phase kept open so an Analyze waits for it and the buttons stay busy.
        Send StreamAnalyzeTick True
        If (StreamDrainPending(Self) = True) Begin
            Set pbStreamDraining       to True
            Set piStreamDrainStartTick to (GetTickCount())
            Set pbPrnFinalizing        to True
            Set pbEnabled of oCompilePoll_tm to True
        End
    End_Procedure

    // One background-finalize tick (dispatched by oCompilePoll_tm while pbPrnFinalizing): consume more
    // of the listing tail (PollPrnTail's budget per 100 ms tick = near-full duty now that no compiler
    // is running), show live progress, and complete + stop once caught up. Latched like the compile
    // ticks so a slow tick is skipped, never nested.
    /* "0 MB left" for the whole of the last megabyte (Nils 2026-08-31). Integer division on a
       byte count spends most of a finalize saying zero and then keeps going, which reads as stuck
       rather than nearly done. MB above a megabyte, KB below it, and "almost done" under a KB. */
    Function PrnRemainingText Integer iBytes Returns String
        If (iBytes >= 1048576) ;
            Function_Return (String(iBytes / 1048576) + " MB")
        If (iBytes >= 1024) ;
            Function_Return (String(iBytes / 1024) + " KB")
        Function_Return "less than 1 KB"
    End_Function

    /* An Analyze asked for while the listing is still being read. StartAnalyze sets it and returns;
       PrnFinalizeTick fires it the moment the cache is stamped. Without this the analyze raced the
       finalize, found pbPrnCacheReady False, and fell back to reading the whole multi-MB .prn
       AGAIN - synchronously, while the background tail was still consuming the same file. That is
       the "long long time" (Nils 2026-08-31); the wait is now the finalize alone, which is seconds. */
    Property Boolean pbAnalyzeWhenFinalized False

    /* THE ROWS THE COMPILE LEFT OVER ARE BUILT ON THE TIMER (2026-10-03). During a compile the stream builds
       analyzer rows at 90 ms per tick; whatever was left when the listing was in used to be built in ONE call -
       about a minute on a 20,164-diagnostic listing, with the UI frozen and the bar still on "Finalizing compiler
       listing... 1 KB left", because a status bar paints only when the message loop runs (Nils 2026-10-03: "it
       seems the process has hung"). FinalizePrnCache now keeps the finalize phase open while rows are left -
       pbPrnFinalizing stays True, so an Analyze still waits for it and the buttons stay busy - and PrnFinalizeTick
       builds the rest a slice per tick, saying how far it has come. */
    Property Boolean pbStreamDraining False
    Property Integer piStreamDrainStartTick 0

    // The end of the finalize phase: the listing is in and every streamed row is built. Restores the compile
    // verdict, re-syncs the buttons and runs an Analyze that was asked for meanwhile.
    Procedure _EndFinalizePhase Handle hoSB
        Integer iSec
        Set pbStreamDraining to False
        Set pbPrnFinalizing  to False
        Set pbEnabled of oCompilePoll_tm to False
        // Restore the compile verdict the "Finalizing..." progress overwrote - the elapsed was
        // computed at FinishCompile and stamped on the dock (piCompileSeconds, the banner's
        // source). No cache statistics here (the trace stays internal in psPrnTailNote).
        Get piCompileSeconds of ghoCompilerOutputDock to iSec
        If (hoSB <> 0) Begin
            If (iSec > 0) ;
                Set StatusText of hoSB to ("Ready. Compiled in " + String(iSec) + "s.")
            Else ;
                Set StatusText of hoSB to "Ready."
        End
        // The listing is in and the cache is stamped. Re-sync the strip (the plan/analyze
        // gates read the cache), then run an Analyze that was asked for while we drained.
        Send SyncWebButtonStates
        If (pbAnalyzeWhenFinalized(Self) = True) Begin
            Set pbAnalyzeWhenFinalized to False
            Send ArmAction of oButtonDeferTimer CI_TCDeferAnalyze
        End
    End_Procedure

    Procedure PrnFinalizeTick
        String   sPrn
        String[] asLines
        Integer  iSize iOffset iCursor iBefore
        Handle   hoSB

        If (pbInCompileTick(Self) = True) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set pbInCompileTick to True
        Get Statusbar_Id of (phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)) to hoSB
        // The listing is in: build the rows the compile left over, one slice per tick (see pbStreamDraining).
        If (pbStreamDraining(Self) = True) Begin
            Get piStreamCursor to iBefore
            Send StreamAnalyzeTick True
            Get piStreamCursor to iCursor
            // A slice that made no progress (the stream could not run - no analyzer grid, an invalidation)
            // ends the phase rather than ticking for ever: the Analyze then takes its classic full read.
            If (StreamDrainPending(Self) = True and iCursor > iBefore) Begin
                If (hoSB <> 0) Begin
                    Get pasPrnTailMsgs to asLines
                    Set StatusText of hoSB to ("Finalizing compiler listing - preparing the analysis: " + String(iCursor) + " of " + String(SizeOfArray(asLines)) + " diagnostics (" + String((GetTickCount() - piStreamDrainStartTick(Self)) / 1000) + "s)")
                End
            End
            Else ;
                Send _EndFinalizePhase hoSB
            Set pbInCompileTick to False
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Send PollPrnTail
        Get psLivePrnPath to sPrn
        Get piPrnTailOffset to iOffset
        Move (rfFileSizeLive(sPrn)) to iSize
        If (sPrn = "" or iSize <= iOffset) Begin
            Set pbPrnFinalizing to False
            Set pbEnabled of oCompilePoll_tm to False
            // Stamps the cache - and keeps the phase open when streamed rows are left to build.
            Send FinalizePrnCache
            If (pbStreamDraining(Self) = False) ;
                Send _EndFinalizePhase hoSB
        End
        Else Begin
            Set StatusText of hoSB to ("Finalizing compiler listing... " + (PrnRemainingText(Self, (iSize - iOffset))) + " left")
        End
        Set pbInCompileTick to False
    End_Procedure

    // Live warning tally for the chip band DURING a compile: the .prn tail counts warnings as it
    // harvests, even though the grid rows only append at compile end (a 25k-row live grid rebuild
    // would be the old stutter at 30x scale - Nils 2026-07-04). -1 when no compile is running, so
    // the dock leaves the chip to the stamped-cache append path outside a live compile.
    Function LivePrnWarnCount Returns Integer
        Boolean bActive
        Get pbActive of oCompileProcess to bActive
        If (bActive = False) ;
            Function_Return -1
        Function_Return (piPrnTailWarn(Self))
    End_Function

    // One-line trace of the tail's LAST outcome ("prn cached: ..." / "prn cache OFF: <why>") so a
    // cache miss NAMES its gate instead of leaving us guessing. INTERNAL ONLY - never shown in the
    // status bar (Nils 2026-07-03: no diagnostic statistics there); read it in the debugger when a
    // warnings display or an analyze cache hit doesn't behave.
    Property String psPrnTailNote ""

    // Disarm the tail + invalidate the cache: a cancelled compile (truncated listing) or a precompile
    // (a PACKAGE build - the tracked program .prn is not being written) must never serve stale data.
    Procedure InvalidatePrnCache String sReason
        Set pbPrnCacheReady to False
        Send InvalidateStreamedRows sReason   // streamed rows can never outlive the tail cache
        Set psLivePrnPath   to ""
        Set psLivePrnPathAlt to ""
        Set psPrnTailNote to ("prn cache OFF: " + sReason)
        // A mid-finalize invalidation must also stop the finalize phase (and ITS timer) - with the
        // flag cleared but the timer left running, OnTimer would fall through to the process-exit
        // branch and re-enter FinishCompile. Never touches the timer during a live compile.
        If (pbPrnFinalizing(Self) = True) Begin
            Set pbPrnFinalizing to False
            Set pbStreamDraining to False
            Set pbEnabled of oCompilePoll_tm to False
            // Buttons were held busy through the finalize phase - release them, or an aborted
            // finalize leaves the panel stuck disabled.
            Send SyncWebButtonStates
            /* AN ANALYZE PARKED ON THIS FINALIZE MUST NOT BE STRANDED. StartAnalyze defers
               while the tail drains and relies on PrnFinalizeTick to re-arm it - which never
               runs if the finalize is abandoned here. A guided Check would then simply stop,
               with nothing on screen to say why. Let it run: with no cache it falls back to the
               full listing read, which is exactly the behaviour that existed before the
               deferral, and slow beats silent. A KILLED compile clears the latch instead -
               there is no output worth analysing and the user asked for it to stop. */
            If (pbAnalyzeWhenFinalized(Self) = True) Begin
                Set pbAnalyzeWhenFinalized to False
                If (pbCancelled(Self) = False) Begin
                    Send ArmAction of oButtonDeferTimer CI_TCDeferAnalyze
                End
            End
        End
    End_Procedure

    // True while the harvested diagnostics still describe the on-disk .prn (same file, size and write
    // time). Any mismatch - a compile from the Studio, a deleted listing, an analyze of some OTHER
    // program's .prn - and every consumer falls back to the full ReadCompilerMessages read.
    Function PrnCacheMatches String sPrnFile Returns Boolean
        Boolean  bReady
        DateTime dtStamp

        Get pbPrnCacheReady to bReady
        If (bReady = False or sPrnFile = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        // Each mismatch NAMES itself in the trace note - the analyzer surfaces it on a full-read.
        If (Lowercase(sPrnFile) <> Lowercase(psPrnCacheFile(Self))) Begin
            Set psPrnTailNote to ("prn cache MISS: asked for '" + sPrnFile + "' but cached '" + psPrnCacheFile(Self) + "'")
            Function_Return False
        End
        If (rfFileSizeLive(sPrnFile) <> piPrnCacheSize(Self)) Begin
            Set psPrnTailNote to "prn cache MISS: listing size changed on disk since the compile"
            Function_Return False
        End
        Get pdtPrnCacheStamp to dtStamp
        If (rfFileDate(sPrnFile) <> dtStamp) Begin
            Set psPrnTailNote to "prn cache MISS: listing write-time changed on disk since the compile"
            Function_Return False
        End
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    // The Analyzer's fast path: the tail-harvested diagnostic lines parsed EXACTLY like
    // ReadCompilerMessages would (same MassageErrorFile -> ExtractData
    // pipeline - the tail only replaced the read+filter). An empty return = no valid cache; the
    // caller falls back to the full read.
    Function CachedPrnMessages String sPrnFile Returns tCompileError[]
        tCompileError[] aEmpty aResult
        String[] asMsgs
        Boolean  bMatch
        Get PrnCacheMatches sPrnFile to bMatch
        If (bMatch = False) ;
            Function_Return aEmpty
        Get pasPrnTailMsgs to asMsgs
        If (SizeOfArray(asMsgs) = 0) ;
            Function_Return aEmpty
        Get MassageErrorFile asMsgs to aResult
        Function_Return aResult
    End_Function

    // Total diagnostic-line count from the cache; -1 = no valid cache (caller does the full read).
    Function CachedPrnMessageCount String sPrnFile Returns Integer
        Boolean bMatch
        Get PrnCacheMatches sPrnFile to bMatch
        If (bMatch = False) ;
            Function_Return -1
        Function_Return (SizeOfArray(pasPrnTailMsgs(Self)))
    End_Function

    // WARNING-line count from the cache; -1 = no valid cache (caller does the full read).
    Function CachedPrnWarningCount String sPrnFile Returns Integer
        Boolean bMatch
        Get PrnCacheMatches sPrnFile to bMatch
        If (bMatch = False) ;
            Function_Return -1
        Function_Return (piPrnTailWarn(Self))
    End_Function

    // The stamped cache's own .prn path, re-validated against the disk right now; "" when there is
    // no (longer a) live cache. Lets a consumer with no .prn path of its own (the compiler output
    // grid's warnings display) both locate and trust the harvest in one call.
    Function CachedPrnStampedFile Returns String
        Boolean bMatch
        String sPrn
        Get psPrnCacheFile to sPrn
        Get PrnCacheMatches sPrn to bMatch
        If (bMatch = False) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return sPrn
    End_Function

    // The cached WARNING messages only (bError False after the shared parse) - the compiler output
    // grid appends these to its parsed .err so the post-compile view carries the FULL diagnostic
    // picture for EVERY compiler (the .err holds errors only). Empty when no valid cache.
    Function CachedPrnWarningMessages Returns tCompileError[]
        tCompileError[] aAll aWarn
        Integer i iMax iOut
        String sPrn

        Get CachedPrnStampedFile to sPrn
        If (sPrn = "") ;
            Function_Return aWarn
        Get CachedPrnMessages sPrn to aAll
        Move (SizeOfArray(aAll) - 1) to iMax
        Move 0 to iOut
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (aAll[i].bError = False) Begin
                Move aAll[i] to aWarn[iOut]
                Increment iOut
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aWarn
    End_Function

    // Per-tick progress refresh, dispatched by oCompilePoll_tm. Sub-samples
    // the expensive bits so we're not formatting strings or hitting disk on
    // every 100 ms tick.
    Procedure UpdateCompileProgress
        Integer iTicks iSec iMin iLogSize iLastLogSize iErrSize iLastErrSize iLastPrnCount
        String  sProgram sText sLogFile sErrPath sErrAlt sVerb sPreview
        String[] asPrnLines
        Boolean bWrote
        Handle  hoSB

        /* THE CLOCK RUNS ABOVE THE LATCH (Nils 2026-08-10).
           Counting ticks and painting the elapsed time is cheap and re-entrant-safe, so it must
           never be hostage to the expensive tail below (the .prn harvest, the streaming build, the
           dock rebuilds - the parts the latch actually protects). That ordering was decided while
           hunting the 2026-08 status-bar freeze: the real cause was the broken install-cache readers
           (see cRefactorFuncLib ReadObsoleteScanCache and siblings) dragging a full install-Pkg walk
           onto tick 5, but the hunt showed how easily one slow statement in the tail silences the
           whole readout. NO PumpMsgQueue here: a timer handler returns to the message loop on its
           own, and pumping outside the latch could re-enter OnTimer and recurse into this block. */
        Get piCompileTicks to iTicks
        Increment iTicks
        Set piCompileTicks to iTicks

        // Elapsed time (every 10 ticks ~= 1 second)
        If (Mod(iTicks, 10) = 0) Begin
            Get psCompilingProgram to sProgram
            Move ((GetTickCount() - piCompileStartTick(Self)) / 1000) to iSec   // WALL-CLOCK seconds (latch-skipped ticks made iTicks/10 undercount)
            Move (iSec / 60) to iMin
            Move (Mod(iSec, 60)) to iSec      // seconds within minute
            // During an Error-4522 precompile run, psCompilingProgram holds
            // the PACKAGE name - say so rather than "Compiling".
            If (pbPrecompiling(Self) = True) Begin
                Move "Precompiling" to sVerb
            End
            Else Begin
                Move "Compiling" to sVerb
            End
            Move (sVerb * sProgram * "... (" ;
                  + String(iMin) + ":" + Right(("0" + String(iSec)), 2) + ")") to sText
            Send SetResultText sText
            // Mirror the same progress text to the app status bar, matching the Analyze phase's live note.
            Get Statusbar_Id of (phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)) to hoSB
            Set StatusText of hoSB to sText
        End

        // ---- everything below is the EXPENSIVE tail the latch actually protects ----
        // Re-entrancy latch (see pbInCompileTick): skip a tick that arrives while one is in flight.
        // NOTE: the body below must stay free of early Procedure_Returns, or the latch never clears.
        If (pbInCompileTick(Self) = True) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set pbInCompileTick to True

        // Live .prn tail (ALL compilers): harvest the new listing bytes every 5 ticks, so the
        // diagnostics are already in memory at FinishCompile (warning chips + the Analyzer fast path).
        If (Mod(iTicks, 5) = 0) ;
            Send PollPrnTail

        // Live compiler output (df-cli): every 20 ticks (~2 s), refresh the error dock
        // if the stdout log grew. rfFileSizeLive reads the size via an OPEN HANDLE - the old
        // metadata read (rfFileSize) returns the stale directory entry while a writer holds
        // the file open, so it could sit at 0 for a whole compile. The grid rebuild only runs
        // when there's actually new output; ~2 s cadence for the same COM-rebuild-cost reason
        // as the legacy .err branch below.
        If (pbStreamOutput(Self) = True and Mod(iTicks, 20) = 0) Begin
            Get psCompileLogFile to sLogFile
            Move (rfFileSizeLive(sLogFile)) to iLogSize
            Get piLastLogSize to iLastLogSize
            If (iLogSize > iLastLogSize) Begin
                Set piLastLogSize to iLogSize
                Send RefreshCompileOutputLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
            End
        End

        // Live compiler output (legacy compiler, no stdout stream): every 20 ticks (~2 s), poll the
        // .err. It's absent until the first error is written (StartCompile deleted BOTH suffix
        // candidates), so a clean compile leaves size 0 and the dock never pops; on growth
        // RefreshCompileErrorsLive shows it live, activating only on that first error. rfFileSizeLive
        // (open-handle size) is what makes this WORK: the directory-metadata size never updates while
        // the compiler holds the .err open. CADENCE ~2 s, not 500 ms: each refresh REBUILDS the CJ
        // grid (COM, ~0.5-2 s once hundreds of errors accumulate) - at 500 ms the rebuilds dominated
        // the ticks, inflating a 24 s compile to 56 s and starving the .prn tail (Nils 2026-07-03).
        // Still feels live; costs a quarter of the rebuild work.
        If (pbStreamOutput(Self) = False and Mod(iTicks, 10) = 0) Begin
            Get psLiveErrPath to sErrPath
            Move (rfFileSizeLive(sErrPath)) to iErrSize
            If (iErrSize = 0) Begin
                Get psLiveErrPathAlt to sErrAlt
                If (sErrAlt <> "" and sErrAlt <> sErrPath) Begin
                    Move (rfFileSizeLive(sErrAlt)) to iErrSize
                    If (iErrSize > 0) Begin
                        Move sErrAlt to sErrPath
                        Set psLiveErrPath to sErrAlt
                    End
                End
            End
            Get piLastErrSize to iLastErrSize
            // ADAPTIVE refresh (Nils 2026-07-04: rows landed in ~7-row chunks at the fixed ~2s):
            // every ~1s while the .err is SMALL (< ~64 KB / ~430 errors - the rebuild is cheap and
            // the chunkiness most visible), stretching back to ~2s once it grows. The growth gate +
            // the re-entrancy latch protect a slower machine either way (an overrunning rebuild just
            // skips ticks) - only a big grid rebuilt at a fast fixed cadence caused the 2026-07-03
            // stutter, and that combination stays excluded.
            If (iErrSize > iLastErrSize and (iErrSize < 65536 or Mod(iTicks, 20) = 0)) Begin
                Set piLastErrSize to iErrSize
                Send RefreshCompileErrorsLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sErrPath
            End
        End

        // Live compiler output (legacy compiler, WARNING phase): DfCompConsole / DFComp write NOTHING to stdout
        // (the redirect log stays 0 bytes) and no .err until the FIRST error - so a warning-only compile left the
        // dock FOLDED for its whole duration, the warnings surfacing only at FinishCompile (Nils, x86 SALES
        // 2026-07-07). But PollPrnTail above HAS been harvesting those warnings live. Feed them into the SAME
        // live view df-cli uses: dump the harvested diagnostic lines to a sibling ".live" preview file and drive
        // the existing ShowCompileOutputLive / RefreshCompileOutputLive (-> LoadRawOutput) path. Guards: ONLY
        // while the .err is still empty (once ERRORS land, the .err branch above takes over with the parsed grid
        // and clears psRawLogFile), and ONLY when the harvest grew - a text-row rebuild every ~2 s, matching the
        // df-cli stdout cadence, NOT the every-tick rebuild the warning-row defer was avoiding. bIsUtf8 False =
        // raw byte write, so ReadErrorFile's binary read round-trips the lines exactly. The preview is transient:
        // FinishCompile's Activate* swaps psRawLogFile to the parsed, clickable grid. (Depends on the compiler
        // FLUSHING its .prn as it runs - which PollPrnTail already relies on for all compilers.) Nils 2026-07-07.
        If (pbStreamOutput(Self) = False and Mod(iTicks, 20) = 0) Begin
            Get psLiveErrPath to sErrPath
            Move (rfFileSizeLive(sErrPath)) to iErrSize
            Get pasPrnTailMsgs to asPrnLines
            Get piLastPrnStreamCount to iLastPrnCount
            If (iErrSize = 0 and SizeOfArray(asPrnLines) > iLastPrnCount) Begin
                Get psCompileLogFile to sLogFile
                Move (sLogFile + ".live") to sPreview
                Get rfWriteEncodedFileFromStringArray (&asPrnLines) sPreview C_NewLine False to bWrote
                If (bWrote = True) Begin
                    Set piLastPrnStreamCount to (SizeOfArray(asPrnLines))
                    // First diagnostic of the compile POPS the dock (like the .err tail's first-error pop);
                    // later growth just refreshes the already-open live view. A truly clean compile never
                    // harvests a line, so the dock stays folded - the pre-existing behaviour.
                    If (iLastPrnCount = 0) ;
                        Send ShowCompileOutputLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sPreview
                    Else ;
                        Send RefreshCompileOutputLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
                End
            End
        End
        Set pbInCompileTick to False
    End_Procedure

    // Launches a targeted precompile of sPkgName (the bare file name from one
    // of df-cli's precompile messages - the Error-4522 bail OR the "is not
    // compatible, recompiling" warning) against TargetWS, reusing the same
    // hidden cmd.exe-redirect + poll-timer machinery as StartCompile. Asks
    // the user first (Yes/No; sReason is the message-specific middle of the
    // question) - the precompile is a build they didn't explicitly click.
    // Returns True when the precompile child process is up and running -
    // FinishCompile then re-enters with pbPrecompiling True once it exits
    // and, on a verified-green result, CHAINS straight back into the
    // program compile the user originally clicked (auto-chain, 2026-07-19 -
    // see the bWasPrecompile branch); a failed precompile is terminal.
    // Returns False when the precompile should NOT (or could not) run: the
    // package source missing from the target's AppSrc, a non-df-cli
    // compiler, or the user answered No.
    Function TryStartPrecompile String sPkgName tSwsFile TargetWS String sReason Returns Boolean
        String  sErrorText sCmdPath sCmdArgs sPkgFile sLogFile sWorkDir sLaunchArgs sProgram sText
        Boolean bOK
        Integer iRetval

        // df-cli's messages give a BARE file name (resolved against the target's
        // AppSrc, next to its .pkd); the classic DfCompConsole 4522 names the FULL
        // PATH - and can name the .SRC itself, since a #HEADER program precompiles
        // its own source (Nils's WinServ, 2026-07-17: the doubled-path resolve here
        // failed silently and the offer never appeared). Accept both: a full path
        // is used verbatim, and everything downstream (display, the ticker, the
        // .err delete/resolve) works with the BARE name it assumes.
        If ((sPkgName contains ":") or (sPkgName contains "\")) Begin
            Move sPkgName to sPkgFile
            Get ParseFileName sPkgName to sPkgName
        End
        Else Begin
            Get AppSrcFolderFor of ghoApplication TargetWS sPkgName to sPkgFile
        Move (vFolderFormat(sPkgFile) + sPkgName) to sPkgFile
        End
        If (sPkgName = "" or vFilePathExists(sPkgFile) = False) ;
            Function_Return False

        Get BuildPrecompileCommand of ghoApplication sPkgFile TargetWS ;
            (&sErrorText) (&sCmdPath) (&sCmdArgs) to bOK
        If (bOK = False) ;
            Function_Return False

        // All guards passed - the precompile CAN run. Ask before launching
        // a build the user didn't explicitly click. Asked AFTER the guards,
        // so the user never confirms something we then cannot do. Still ONE
        // question - the precompile; the compile that follows a green
        // precompile is not a second action, it RESUMES the compile the
        // user already clicked (the 4522 bail interrupted it) - see
        // FinishCompile's green-precompile branch (auto-chain, 2026-07-19).
        Get psCompilingProgram to sProgram
        Move ("The compiler reports that the precompiled package" * sPkgName * ;
              (sReason + ".\n\nPrecompile it now?")) to sText
        Get FTYesNoBox sText to iRetval
        If (iRetval <> MBR_Yes) ;
            Function_Return False

        // From here on, StartCompile's launch mechanics: the shared _ArmCompileLaunch, then the hidden
        // cmd.exe wrapper redirecting stdout+stderr into the same overwritten log, the poll timer, the
        // Stop-mode button, the live dock.
        Send SetResultText ("Precompiling " * sPkgName * ("(needed by " + sProgram + ")..."))
        Send SetResultColor (FTThemeInk())
        Send SetErrFile ""
        // A PACKAGE build errors into the package's OWN .err (PRECOMPILE.err), which df-cli
        // can write while still exiting 0. Delete stale candidates now so FinishCompile's
        // precompile verdict can trust an .err found afterwards to be THIS run's.
        Get DeleteCompileErrorsFile of ghoApplication sPkgName TargetWS to bOK

        Set pbCancelled to False
        Set pbAnalyzeWhenFinalized to False   // a parked analyze belongs to the PREVIOUS compile
        Set pbPrecompiling to True
        // The elapsed-time ticker (UpdateCompileProgress) reads
        // psCompilingProgram - point it at the package for the duration; the
        // user's next StartCompile sets it back to the program.
        Set psCompilingProgram to sPkgName
        Set pCompilingWS to TargetWS

        // Feed the dock's banner the PACKAGE (name + path + precompile flag) - the live
        // stream and the combined error view then read "----Pre-compiling <pkg>----" /
        // "----Pre-compile Finished ...----" instead of still naming the program, so a
        // precompile attempt is unmistakable in the output. Bitness is left as StartCompile
        // set it - the package builds in the same compiler run context as the program.
        Set psCompileProgramName  of ghoCompilerOutputDock to sPkgName
        Set psCompileProgramPath  of ghoCompilerOutputDock to sPkgFile
        Set piCompileSeconds      of ghoCompilerOutputDock to 0
        Set pbCompileIsPrecompile of ghoCompilerOutputDock to True
        Set psCompiledExe         of ghoCompilerOutputDock to ""   // a precompile writes no exe - no success footer

        Send _ArmCompileLaunch TargetWS sCmdPath sCmdArgs (&sWorkDir) (&sLogFile)
        // df-cli streams the build to the stdout log (the live feed); the legacy
        // DfCompConsole leaves the redirect log empty, so its live view just stays
        // quiet for the (short) package build - the verdict comes from the exit
        // code + the package's own .err either way (the bWasPrecompile branch).
        Set pbStreamOutput to True

        Move ('/c " "' + sCmdPath + '" ' + sCmdArgs + ' > "' + sLogFile + '" 2>&1 "') to sLaunchArgs
        Get StartProcess of oCompileProcess "cmd.exe" sLaunchArgs sWorkDir False to bOK
        If (bOK = False) Begin
            Set pbPrecompiling to False
            Function_Return False
        End

        // A precompile builds a PACKAGE - the tracked program .prn is not being written, so the tail
        // must not keep polling it (nor may a later Analyze trust a cache from before the precompile).
        Send InvalidatePrnCache "precompile run"
        Set pbEnabled of oCompilePoll_tm to True
        Send RefreshActionButtons True    // the precompile is stoppable the same way
        Send ShowCompileOutputLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sLogFile
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    // Feed the output dock the warning count for its "- Total Warnings" summary line. The .err the
    // dock shows carries ERRORS only - warnings live in the .prn LISTING - so read the .prn (when
    // present) and count its warnings (bError False). A no-.prn build leaves the count at 0. Called
    // only on the failure paths that show the combined view, so the happy (clean-build) path never
    // pays for this read.
    Procedure FeedDockCompileWarnings String sProgram tSwsFile TargetWS
        String sPrnPath
        Integer iWarn iMax iIdx
        tCompileError[] aPrnMsgs

        Move 0 to iWarn
        Get ResolvePrnFile sProgram TargetWS to sPrnPath
        If (sPrnPath <> "") Begin
            // Fast path: the live tail tallied warnings as they streamed (-1 = no cache, full read).
            Get CachedPrnWarningCount sPrnPath to iWarn
            If (iWarn < 0) Begin
                Move 0 to iWarn
                Get ReadCompilerMessages sPrnPath to aPrnMsgs
                Move (SizeOfArray(aPrnMsgs) - 1) to iMax
                For iIdx from 0 to iMax
                    If (aPrnMsgs[iIdx].bError = False) ;
                        Increment iWarn
                Loop
            End
        End
        Set piCompileWarnings of ghoCompilerOutputDock to iWarn
    End_Procedure

    // True when the .err is a LINK-ONLY failure: the compile STAGE succeeded (no real "ERROR: <n> ...
    // OF FILE: ..." line) and only the LINK stage failed - e.g. "LINK ERROR: UNABLE TO ADD THE MANIFEST"
    // / "UNABLE TO CREATE EXECUTABLE" when a project's .manifest can't be found. The code is fine and the
    // .prn warnings are intact, so the caller presents it as a warnings build (ready to Analyze) rather
    // than a red "did not compile". Returns False for an empty .err or any non-link diagnostic.
    Function IsLinkOnlyFailure String sErrPath Returns Boolean
        String[] asLines
        String sTrim
        Integer iSize iCount
        Boolean bAnyLink

        Get ReadErrorFile sErrPath to asLines
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines)) to iSize
        If (iSize = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move False to bAnyLink
        Decrement iSize
        For iCount from 0 to iSize
            Move (Uppercase(Trim(asLines[iCount]))) to sTrim
            If (Left(sTrim, 11) = "LINK ERROR:") Begin
                Move True to bAnyLink
            End
            Else If (Left(sTrim, 6) = "ERROR:") Begin
                // A real compile-error line - genuine failure, not link-only.
                Function_Return False
            End
            // WARNING: / blank / other lines are ignored - only a real ERROR: vetoes link-only.
        Loop
        Function_Return bAnyLink
    End_Function

    // True when the link failure in sErrPath is a MANIFEST error - a "LINK ERROR: ... MANIFEST ..." line
    // (e.g. "UNABLE TO ADD THE MANIFEST"). Callers then check whether the manifest FILE actually exists:
    // absent -> offer to generate it (OfferAndFixMissingManifest); present -> the compiler could not EMBED
    // it (a compiler/toolchain issue), so ShowLinkOnlyResult hints to try a different compiler instead.
    Function IsManifestLinkFailure String sErrPath Returns Boolean
        String[] asLines
        String sUp
        Integer iSize iCount
        Get ReadErrorFile sErrPath to asLines
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iSize
        For iCount from 0 to iSize
            Move (Uppercase(asLines[iCount])) to sUp
            If ((sUp contains "LINK ERROR") and (sUp contains "MANIFEST")) ;
                Function_Return True
        Loop
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    // When a link-only failure is a MISSING MANIFEST and the file is genuinely absent, offer (Yes/No) to
    // generate it from the DataFlex template (cCompileMixin.GenerateProgramManifest) and compile again.
    // Returns True when it has taken over (manifest written + a fresh compile launched) - the caller must
    // then Procedure_Return. Returns False to fall through to the normal amber "link skipped" result: not a
    // manifest case, already offered this compile, the file already exists, the user declined, or generation
    // failed. One offer per user compile (pbManifestFixTried), so the auto re-compile can never loop.
    Function OfferAndFixMissingManifest String sProgram tSwsFile TargetWS String sErrPath Returns Boolean
        Boolean bTried bMissing bGen
        String sManifest sManifestName sText sGenErr
        Integer iRetval

        Get pbManifestFixTried to bTried
        If (bTried = True) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get IsManifestLinkFailure sErrPath to bMissing
        If (bMissing = False) ;
            Function_Return False
        // Belt: only when the manifest is genuinely absent (not a stale/other MANIFEST link diagnostic).
        Get ManifestFileForProgram of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS to sManifest
        If (vFilePathExists(sManifest) = True) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get ParseFileName sManifest to sManifestName
        Move (sProgram * "did not link: its application manifest" * sManifestName * ;
              "is missing.\n\nGenerate it from the DataFlex template and compile again?") to sText
        Get FTYesNoBox sText to iRetval
        Set pbManifestFixTried to True   // offered - don't ask again this compile, whatever the answer
        If (iRetval <> MBR_Yes) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get GenerateProgramManifest of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS (&sGenErr) to bGen
        If (bGen = False) Begin
            Send FTInfoBox ("Could not create" * sManifestName + "." * sGenErr + ;
                           "\n\nYou can also compile once in the DataFlex Studio to generate the manifest.")
            Function_Return False
        End
        // Manifest written - the link step will now find it. Re-run the compile (mirrors the df-cli auto-retry).
        Send StartCompile
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    // When a link-only failure is a MANIFEST link error but the manifest FILE is PRESENT, the compiler compiled
    // the code fine yet could not ADD the manifest to the .exe - a DF26 df-cli defect on legacy/32-bit workspaces
    // that fires whether the manifest is present or absent (so generating one can't help). The ADD step is gated
    // on BuildManifestFile=1, NOT EmbedManifestFile (verified on the LegacyApp2 x86 workspace: EmbedManifestFile=0
    // left the ADD failing; BuildManifestFile=0 made df-cli link cleanly). So the escape sets BuildManifestFile=0
    // - the linker then skips the manifest step entirely and builds a manifest-less .exe (fine for a test-compile;
    // the code is what matters). Offer (Yes/No) + compile again; returns True when it has taken over (cfg written
    // + fresh compile launched) so the caller Procedure_Returns. If BuildManifestFile is ALREADY 0 and the error
    // persists, no cfg key can reach the step (deeper defect) - return False so ShowLinkOnlyResult gives the "try
    // a different compiler (DF25 console)" hint. One offer per compile (pbManifestSkipTried) caps it. Nils 2026-07-07.
    Function OfferBuildWithoutManifest String sProgram tSwsFile TargetWS String sErrPath Returns Boolean
        Boolean bTried bManifestErr bWritten
        String sManifest sBuildFlag sText sCfgErr
        Integer iRetval

        Get pbManifestSkipTried to bTried
        If (bTried = True) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get IsManifestLinkFailure sErrPath to bManifestErr
        If (bManifestErr = False) ;
            Function_Return False
        // Only when the manifest is PRESENT - a genuinely ABSENT one is OfferAndFixMissingManifest's job (generate).
        Get ManifestFileForProgram of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS to sManifest
        If (vFilePathExists(sManifest) = False) ;
            Function_Return False
        // Already built manifest-less and STILL failing -> the manifest step isn't reachable from the cfg (a
        // deeper df-cli defect). Don't re-offer a no-op; fall through to the compiler-switch hint.
        Get GetProgramCfgValue of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "Application" "BuildManifestFile" "1" to sBuildFlag
        If (Trim(sBuildFlag) = "0") ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (sProgram * ;
              "compiled, but the compiler could not add its manifest to the .exe." * ;
              "This is a known df-cli limitation on legacy / 32-bit workspaces - the code itself is fine." + ;
              "\n\nBuild the .exe WITHOUT a manifest (sets BuildManifestFile=0 in the program configuration) and compile again?") to sText
        Get FTYesNoBox sText to iRetval
        Set pbManifestSkipTried to True   // offered - don't ask again this compile, whatever the answer
        If (iRetval <> MBR_Yes) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get SetProgramCfgValue of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "Application" "BuildManifestFile" "0" (&sCfgErr) to bWritten
        If (bWritten = False) Begin
            Send FTInfoBox ("Could not update the program configuration." * sCfgErr)
            Function_Return False
        End
        // BuildManifestFile=0 written - re-run; the linker now builds the .exe with no manifest step to fail.
        Send StartCompile
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* A LINK-ONLY failure whose .err says "UNABLE TO OPEN FILE <image>": a [Bitmaps] / [Icons] entry of the
       program's .cfg names a file that exists neither in the workspace's Bitmap folder(s) nor in the
       building install's Bitmaps folder (Nils 2026-09-18 - DacAbout.bmp, shipped by every install through
       DF25, gone in DF26, never in the example workspace). Sibling of OfferAndFixMissingManifest, same
       contract: True when it has taken over (the .cfg rewritten + a fresh compile launched - the caller
       Procedure_Returns), False to fall through - no such line, already offered this compile, nothing is
       actually missing, the user declined, or the write failed. EVERY listed image is checked, not only
       the one the linker named, because the linker stops at the first. An image no source file mentions is
       offered for removal; one the source still references is reported instead, with the copy in another
       install named when one is on this machine, because dropping it links but leaves a blank image at run
       time. The .cfg is rewritten line for line - only the dropped lines differ (RemoveCfgLinkedImages). */
    Function OfferAndFixMissingLinkImages String sProgram tSwsFile TargetWS String sErrPath Returns Boolean
        String[] asErr asNamed asCfg asListed asDrop asKeep asElsewhere
        String sBase sCfgName sCfg sWhere sText sList sKeepText
        Integer i iMax iRetval iGone
        Boolean bTried bEnds bRef bOK

        Get pbLinkImageFixTried to bTried
        If (bTried = True) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get ReadErrorFile sErrPath to asErr
        Get LinkImagesMissingFromErr of ghoApplication asErr to asNamed
        If (SizeOfArray(asNamed) = 0) ;
            Function_Return False

        Move sProgram to sBase
        If (sBase contains ".") ;
            Move (Left(sBase, Pos(".", sBase) - 1)) to sBase
        Move (sBase + ".cfg") to sCfgName
        Get AppSrcFolderFor of ghoApplication TargetWS sCfgName to sCfg
        Move (vFolderFormat(sCfg) + sCfgName) to sCfg
        If (vFilePathExists(sCfg) = False) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get rfReadEncodedFileLines sCfg False (&bEnds) to asCfg
        Get CfgLinkedImageNames of ghoApplication asCfg to asListed

        // Every listed image that exists nowhere - split by whether the source still mentions it.
        Move (SizeOfArray(asListed) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Get LinkedImageLocation of ghoApplication asListed[i] TargetWS to sWhere
            If (sWhere = "") Begin
                Get LinkedImageReferencedInSource of ghoApplication asListed[i] TargetWS to bRef
                If (bRef = True) Begin
                    Move asListed[i] to asKeep[-1]
                    Get LinkedImageInAnotherInstall of ghoApplication asListed[i] to sWhere
                    Move sWhere to asElsewhere[-1]
                End
                Else ;
                    Move asListed[i] to asDrop[-1]
            End
        Loop
        If (SizeOfArray(asDrop) = 0 and SizeOfArray(asKeep) = 0) ;
            Function_Return False           // the linker's name is not a listed image after all - not ours
        Set pbLinkImageFixTried to True     // offered (or reported) - don't ask again this compile

        Move "" to sKeepText
        Move (SizeOfArray(asKeep) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (sKeepText + "\n    " + asKeep[i]) to sKeepText
            If (asElsewhere[i] <> "") ;
                Move (sKeepText + "   (a copy is in " + asElsewhere[i] + ")") to sKeepText
        Loop
        If (sKeepText <> "") ;
            Move ("The source still references " + String(SizeOfArray(asKeep)) + " missing image(s), so they stay listed - copy the file into the workspace's Bitmaps folder instead:" + sKeepText) to sKeepText

        If (SizeOfArray(asDrop) = 0) Begin
            Send FTInfoBox (sProgram * "did not link: a linked image listed in" * sCfgName * "cannot be found in the workspace's Bitmaps folder(s) or in the DataFlex install.\n\n" + sKeepText)
            Function_Return False
        End

        Move "" to sList
        Move (SizeOfArray(asDrop) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (sList + "\n    " + asDrop[i]) to sList
        Loop
        Move (sProgram * "did not link:" * String(SizeOfArray(asDrop)) * "linked image(s) listed in" * sCfgName * ;
              "cannot be found in the workspace's Bitmaps folder(s) or in the DataFlex install, and no source file references them:" + sList + ;
              "\n\nRemove them from" * sCfgName * "and compile again?") to sText
        If (sKeepText <> "") ;
            Move (sText + "\n\n" + sKeepText) to sText
        Get FTYesNoBox sText to iRetval
        If (iRetval <> MBR_Yes) ;
            Function_Return False

        Get RemoveCfgLinkedImages of ghoApplication (&asCfg) asDrop to iGone
        Get rfWriteEncodedFileFromStringArrayEx (&asCfg) sCfg C_NewLine False bEnds to bOK
        If (bOK = False) Begin
            Send FTInfoBox ("Could not write" * sCfg + ". Is the AppSrc folder writable?")
            Function_Return False
        End
        // The .cfg no longer names the file - the link step will not ask for it. Compile again.
        Send StartCompile
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    // Headline a LINK-ONLY failure (IsLinkOnlyFailure True): the compile succeeded, only the link failed
    // (e.g. the .manifest wasn't found). Resolve the .prn, count its warnings, arm Analyze (psPrnFilePath),
    // and headline an amber "compiled - N warnings, link skipped" so the user knows the CODE is fine and
    // Analyze is the next step - not a red "did not compile". Shared by the df-cli (non-zero exit) and the
    // legacy (.err-verdict) failure branches, since either compiler can be the one that link-failed.
    Procedure ShowLinkOnlyResult String sProgram tSwsFile TargetWS String sErrPath
        Integer iWarn
        String sPrn sManifest sReason
        String[] asErr asImages
        Boolean bManifestErr
        tCompileError[] aMsgs

        Move 0 to iWarn
        Get ResolvePrnFile sProgram TargetWS to sPrn
        If (sPrn <> "") Begin
            // Fast path: the live tail already harvested + counted the diagnostics (-1 = no cache).
            Get CachedPrnMessageCount sPrn to iWarn
            If (iWarn < 0) Begin
                Get ReadCompilerMessages sPrn to aMsgs
                Move (SizeOfArray(aMsgs)) to iWarn
            End
            // Armed whether or not it holds warnings - see "A CLEAN BUILD IS ANALYZED TOO" in
            // FinishCompile: the code compiled, and only an analyze replaces the previous board.
            Set psPrnFilePath to sPrn
        End
        // Reason phrase: a MANIFEST link error while the manifest FILE is PRESENT means the compiler could not
        // EMBED it - generating one can't help (a compiler/toolchain issue, e.g. DF26 df-cli on a legacy x86
        // workspace), so point the user at a different compiler instead of implying a missing manifest.
        Move False to bManifestErr
        Get IsManifestLinkFailure sErrPath to bManifestErr
        If (bManifestErr = True) Begin
            Get ManifestFileForProgram of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS to sManifest
            If (vFilePathExists(sManifest) = False) ;
                Move False to bManifestErr   // genuinely missing - the offer path owns that, not this hint
        End
        If (bManifestErr = True) ;
            Move "the compiler could not embed the manifest (it IS present - try a different compiler, e.g. the DF25 console)" to sReason
        Else Begin
            // A linked image the .cfg lists could not be opened (the offer path has had its turn by now).
            Get ReadErrorFile sErrPath to asErr
            Get LinkImagesMissingFromErr of ghoApplication asErr to asImages
            If (SizeOfArray(asImages) > 0) ;
                Move ("a linked image listed in the program's .cfg is missing - " + asImages[0]) to sReason
            Else ;
                Move "see the link error below" to sReason
        End
        If (iWarn > 0) ;
            Send SetResultText (sProgram * ("compiled - " + String(iWarn) + " warnings. The .exe was not linked: " + sReason + " - click Analyze."))
        Else ;
            Send SetResultText (sProgram * ("compiled cleanly. The .exe was not linked: " + sReason + "."))
        Send SetResultColor (RGB(224, 166, 77))
    End_Procedure

    // Fires from oCompilePoll_tm once the compiler process has exited
    // (either normally OR because KillCompile killed it). Checks the
    // .err file, shows success / failure / cancelled in the result form,
    // and surfaces the .err contents on failure.
    /* AUTO-ANALYZE, but ONLY when a guided Check drove this compile (Nils 2026-08-07: "after the
       compile has finished, in this special situation, automatically start the Analyze action").

       The Check button already compiles for you; stopping there and waiting for a second click was
       the flow losing its own thread - the trail asked the question, the answer needs no further
       instruction. A dock-driven compile is untouched: Analyze stays a deliberate click there,
       because the user is working the bar directly and may want the output first.

       CALLED FROM THE THREE ENDINGS WHERE THE PROGRAM COMPILE ACTUALLY RAN AND REACHED A VERDICT
       ABOUT THE SOURCE: the non-zero-exit branch (a compiler reporting errors exits non-zero, so
       this is the ordinary "it failed" route), the success return, and the failure tail.

       Deliberately NOT from the other four places FinishCompile stops the marquee:
         - CANCELLED - the user stopped it; analyzing what it got to is not what Stop means.
         - PRECOMPILE FAILED - the program compile never started, so there is no verdict on it.
         - "still reports 4522" / "needs a precompile of X" - the compile was refused pending a
           Studio action. The headline already names the single next step, and there is no ranked
           source verdict to add to it.
       Nor from the relaunch paths (retry, manifest fix, precompile-then-recompile), which are not
       endings at all - the next compile calls FinishCompile again when it really is done.

       Getting this list wrong is invisible in one direction: the first cut wired only the success
       and tail endings, so a clean build auto-analyzed and a compile WITH ERRORS - the commonest
       case, and the one the guided Check most exists for - silently did nothing.

       BOTH endings, deliberately. A FAILED compile is not a reason to skip it - the errors are what
       the guided Check most wants to show (the "Breaks the build" band - second since 08-16, behind Auto-fixable), and analyzing the
       .err is how they get there. Clean, warnings, or broken: all three are answers.

       Deferred, never called inline. FinishCompile runs on the compile-poll tick; StartAnalyze pops
       dialogs, docks panels and can run for half a minute on a large listing. CI_TCDeferAnalyze is
       the same code the Analyze button arms, so the automatic path and the manual one are one path. */
    Procedure MaybeAutoAnalyzeAfterGuidedCheck
        Boolean bGuided
        Get pbGuidedCheckRun of ghoApplication to bGuided
        If (bGuided = False) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Send ArmAction of oButtonDeferTimer CI_TCDeferAnalyze
    End_Procedure

    Procedure FinishCompile
        String sProgram sErrorText sErrPath sCmdLine sCompilerLog sLogFile sPrnPath sPkgName sDeclined sExePath sPkgBare sLastPre sBail sHeadline sCrash
        String[] asLogLines
        Boolean bOK bExists bCancelled bWasPrecompile bTakenOver
        Integer iExitCode iRetries iWarnCount iSec
        Handle  hoSB
        tSwsFile TargetWS
        tCompileError[] aPrnMsgs

        // The compile process has exited: restore (delete) any temporarily-injected .cfg suffix keys NOW,
        // the single choke point every FinishCompile path passes. The compiler read the cfg at launch and
        // the live-poll paths were cached at StartCompile, so nothing still reads the injected keys; a
        // retry/precompile relaunch goes back through StartCompile, which re-injects. (Nils 2026-07-08.)
        Send RestoreInjectedCfgSuffixes of ghoApplication
        // ... and the temporarily-injected BuildManifestFile=1 (the generated <output>.exe.manifest
        // itself STAYS - a build artifact; with it present the injection never fires again).
        Send RestoreInjectedCfgManifestBuild of ghoApplication
        Send RestoreInjectedCfgCompilerWarnings of ghoApplication

        // A fresh compile clears any prior clean-build .prn warning target.
        Set psPrnFilePath to ""

        Get psCompilingProgram to sProgram
        Get pCompilingWS       to TargetWS
        Get pbCancelled        to bCancelled
        Get psCompiledCmdLine  to sCmdLine
        Get psCompileLogFile   to sLogFile

        // Was the child that just exited a PRECOMPILE (launched by the
        // Error-4522 handler below) rather than the program compile?
        // Read-and-clear, so no stale True can leak into a later compile's
        // post-mortem (e.g. after a Stop mid-precompile).
        Get pbPrecompiling to bWasPrecompile
        Set pbPrecompiling to False

        // Capture the compiler's exit code BEFORE CleanUp releases the
        // process handle (ProcessExitCode returns -1 once phProcess = 0).
        Get ProcessExitCode of oCompileProcess to iExitCode

        /* DID IT EXIT, OR DID IT DIE? (2026-10-05.) A crashed compiler returns its exception code, and its
           listing and .err stop at whatever buffer was written last - in the middle of a line. Known HERE,
           before the listing's tail is drained below, so that half line is not read as a diagnostic - and
           told to the Check. A precompile and a stopped compile are not judged. */
        Get CompilerCrashWords iExitCode to sCrash
        If (bCancelled = True or bWasPrecompile = True) ;
            Move "" to sCrash
        Set pbCompileCrashed to (sCrash <> "")
        If (bWasPrecompile = False) ;
            Set psChkCompileCrash of ghoApplication to sCrash

        // Final read of the compiler stdout log (everything cmd.exe's
        // redirection captured). df-cli is mostly silent during a build,
        // so this is often empty — but on failure it can carry the one
        // line that explains what went wrong without making it into
        // the .err file. Read it once here and reuse across all branches.
        Get LoadCompileLogTail sLogFile to sCompilerLog

        // Release process handles and restore the Compile button's label (flipped to "&Stop"
        // by StartCompile). The marquee is NOT stopped here: it keeps running through the
        // post-compile work below (reading the .err/.prn, populating the output dock) and is
        // stopped at each terminal branch instead - so it runs until Analyze is actually
        // ready, rather than leaving a dead bar while the dock still loads.
        Send CleanUp of oCompileProcess
        // Live .prn tail: a normal program compile is complete on process exit - drain the remainder,
        // flush the final line, and stamp the cache so the .prn reads below (and a following Analyze)
        // can skip the full listing re-read. A cancelled compile leaves a truncated listing and a
        // precompile tracked a package build - both only invalidate.
        If (bCancelled = False and bWasPrecompile = False) ;
            Send DrainPrnTail
        Else ;
            Send InvalidatePrnCache "compile cancelled / precompile exit"
        Send RefreshActionButtons False   // the "Stop Compile" button back to "Compile"
        // Compile process finished - clear the "Compiling..." progress from the status bar (the verdict shows on
        // the result line / the dock's "Compile Finished in N seconds" banner; a following Analyze sets its own note).
        Get Statusbar_Id of (phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)) to hoSB
        // Plain "Ready." only - the tail-trace (psPrnTailNote) stays INTERNAL (Nils 2026-07-03:
        // no diagnostic statistics in the status bar).
        Set StatusText of hoSB to "Ready."

        // Final elapsed compile time for the dock's "----Compile Finished ... in N seconds ----" banner.
        // WALL CLOCK (piCompileStartTick), NOT ticks/10 - the re-entrancy latch skips ticks under load,
        // so the tick count undercounts (a 24 s compile read "8 s"). Clamped to a minimum of 1 so a
        // sub-second compile reads "1 seconds" (like Studio) rather than "0 seconds".
        Move ((GetTickCount() - piCompileStartTick(Self)) / 1000) to iSec
        If (iSec < 1) ;
            Move 1 to iSec
        Set piCompileSeconds of ghoCompilerOutputDock to iSec

        // No .err to offer unless the failure path below finds one.
        Send SetErrFile ""

        // Cancelled path: the verdict says stopped, but DON'T yank the partial output away - the
        // user may have stopped BECAUSE the streamed errors caught their eye (Nils 2026-07-04).
        // df-cli: keep the dock on the output reached so far (one refresh for the final captured
        // lines). Legacy: the live .err tail streamed parsed errors into the grid - reload it ONCE
        // so the rows written between the last live refresh and the kill land too (a no-op via the
        // grid's COM guard when no error ever popped the dock; the old CloseCompileErrorDialog here
        // predated the live tail, when a legacy cancel had nothing showing). The on-disk .err is
        // TRUNCATED (killed mid-write) - fine for browsing; the next compile deletes + rewrites it.
        If (bCancelled = True) Begin
            /* THE GUIDED CHECK IS OVER, so say so (2026-09-16). This branch returns without
               reaching the tail where pbGuidedCheckRun is cleared, so a stopped Check used to
               leave it True until the NEXT Check completed - the same shape as the note above
               at "pbGuidedCheckRun stayed True to hijack whatever the user analyzed NEXT".
               It matters more now that the board holds its remedies while the flag is set:
               without this, stopping a Check would disable them with no way back. */
            Set pbGuidedCheckRun of ghoApplication to False
            Send SetResultText ("Cancelled: " * sProgram * "compile was stopped.")
            Send SetResultColor (FTThemeInk())
            If (pbStreamOutput(Self) = True) Begin
                Send RefreshCompileOutputLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
            End
            Else Begin
                Send RefreshErrorOutput of ghoCompilerOutputDock
            End
            Send SetBusyMarquee False
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // The exit we are post-morteming is the PRECOMPILE launched by the
        // Error-4522 handler below, not the program compile (sProgram holds
        // the PACKAGE name here - TryStartPrecompile pointed the ticker at
        // it). On a VERIFIED green result, CHAIN straight back into the
        // program compile the user originally clicked - the bail merely
        // interrupted it (auto-chain, 2026-07-19; psLastPrecompiledPkg
        // guards the chained compile against a same-package re-bail). A
        // failed precompile is terminal - never chained - and surfaces its
        // own output. Must run BEFORE the program-centric branches:
        // CheckCompileSucceeded would mis-read the PROGRAM's .err for a
        // PACKAGE build.
        If (bWasPrecompile = True) Begin
            // The exit code alone is NOT the verdict: df-cli has been seen exiting 0 while the
            // package build ERRORED - it writes the PACKAGE's own .err (a PRECOMPILE.err full
            // of 4345/4328) and a green verdict would then loop the user straight back into
            // the same 4522 question with no explanation (VDF18-era workspace, 2026-07-05).
            // TryStartPrecompile deleted stale candidates before the launch, so an .err found
            // here is THIS run's - and it IS the verdict. Chaining makes this check load-
            // bearing twice over: a mis-read green would now auto-START a build.
            Get ResolveCompiledFile of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "err" to sErrPath
            If (iExitCode = 0 and sErrPath = "") Begin
                // Verified green - resume the interrupted program compile. The dock gets the
                // phase note first (the chained StartCompile KEEPS it - see psChainedFromPkg -
                // so the rebuilt output still shows the precompile succeeded), and the same-
                // package guard arms so a chained 4522 on THIS package goes terminal instead
                // of re-asking. StartCompile re-reads the user's picked program from settings
                // (the one whose compile bailed) and re-arms the marquee; dropping it here
                // also covers StartCompile's own early-bail returns.
                Set psLastPrecompiledPkg to sProgram
                Set psChainedFromPkg     to sProgram
                Set psCompileChainNote of ghoCompilerOutputDock to ;
                    ("----Pre-compile of " + sProgram + " succeeded in " + String(iSec) + " seconds ----")
                Send SetBusyMarquee False
                Send StartCompile
                Procedure_Return
            End
            If (sErrPath <> "") Begin
                Send SetResultText ("Failed: " * sProgram * "cannot be precompiled - the package build reported compile errors (shown below). Fix them, then compile again.")
                Send SetResultColor clRed
                Send SetErrFile sErrPath
                Send ActivateCombinedCompileOutput of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sLogFile sErrPath
            End
            Else Begin
                Send SetResultText ("Failed: precompile of " * sProgram * ("exited with code " + String(iExitCode) + "."))
                Send SetResultColor clRed
                Send RefreshCompileOutputLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
            End
            Send SetBusyMarquee False
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // Program compile actually finished (not cancelled, not a precompile). Mirror the analyzer's status-bar
        // note with the elapsed compile time (iSec, from the progress ticker above) - same "Ready. ... Ns." style
        // as "Ready. Analyzed in 24s.". Overrides the plain "Ready." set at the top of FinishCompile.
        Set StatusText of hoSB to ("Ready. Compiled in " + String(iSec) + "s.")

        // The TWO precompile messages, checked BEFORE the exit-code and
        // .err branches below (the bail's shape varies: df-cli can write the
        // 4522 into an .err with --write-errors, and has been seen exiting 0
        // on it - either routes past a check buried in the no-.err failure
        // branch; the bug Nils caught 2026-06-10).
        //
        // 1) The BAIL: "Error 4522 ... please precompile X" - nothing was
        //    built; offer the precompile, and on No surface the manual hint.
        //    NOT gated on pbStreamOutput: the legacy DfCompConsole raises the
        //    SAME 4522 ("WINSERV.SRC(2,1) Error 4522 ... please precompile
        //    <full path>") but writes it into the .err with an EMPTY stdout
        //    log - the old gate skipped the scan entirely, so neither the
        //    offer nor the manual hint ever appeared (Nils's WinServ #HEADER
        //    program, 2026-07-17). The stdout scan is a no-op on the empty
        //    legacy log; the .err scan serves both compilers.
        // 2) The SELF-HEALING warning: "Warning: Precompiled package X is
        //    not compatible, recompiling." - df-cli rebuilt it IN MEMORY and
        //    the compile ran to completion, but the on-disk .pkd is never
        //    updated, so the inline recompile taxes EVERY compile. Offer the
        //    targeted precompile so the disk copy gets rebuilt once; on No,
        //    remember the package (don't nag) and FALL THROUGH to the normal
        //    success/failure verdict - unlike the bail, there IS a verdict.
        //    df-cli-only wording, so this one stays behind pbStreamOutput.
        // A GREEN precompile chains straight back into the program compile
        // (the bWasPrecompile branch above); only a declined or failed one
        // leaves the manual "Precompile it in the Studio" hint.
        Get ResolveCompiledFile of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "err" to sErrPath
        Get ExtractPrecompilePackage sCompilerLog to sPkgName
        If (sPkgName = "" and sErrPath <> "") Begin
            // LoadCompileLogTail is just the byte-safe whole-file reader.
            Get LoadCompileLogTail sErrPath to sErrorText
            Get ExtractPrecompilePackage sErrorText to sPkgName
        End
        If (sPkgName <> "") Begin
            // Same-package re-bail after a chained green precompile: the targeted precompile
            // COMPLETED, yet the compile it chained into still 4522s on that very package - a
            // re-ask would loop the user through Yes for nothing, so go terminal-red instead.
            // The next Compile press clears the guard (DoCompileToggle), so a genuinely new
            // staleness of the same package can be offered again. Compare BARE names: the
            // classic DfCompConsole message carries the full path, the guard stores the bare
            // name TryStartPrecompile derived.
            Move sPkgName to sPkgBare
            If ((sPkgBare contains ":") or (sPkgBare contains "\")) ;
                Get ParseFileName sPkgBare to sPkgBare
            Get psLastPrecompiledPkg to sLastPre
            If (sLastPre <> "" and Lowercase(sPkgBare) = Lowercase(sLastPre)) Begin
                Send SetResultText ("Failed: " * sProgram * ("still reports Error 4522 for " + sPkgBare + " although its precompile just succeeded. Precompile it in the Studio and check that the compiler versions/platforms match, then compile again."))
                Send SetResultColor clRed
                If (sErrPath <> "") Begin
                    Send SetErrFile sErrPath
                    Send ActivateCombinedCompileOutput of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sLogFile sErrPath
                End
                Else Begin
                    Send RefreshCompileOutputLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
                End
                Send SetBusyMarquee False
                Procedure_Return
            End
            Get TryStartPrecompile sPkgName TargetWS "is out of date (Error 4522)" to bOK
            If (bOK = True) ;
                Procedure_Return
            // The precompile didn't launch: the user answered No, the
            // package source is missing, or the command could not be
            // built. Tell the user what the compiler wants, and show
            // whatever the compiler left behind.
            Send SetResultText ("Failed: " * sProgram * ("needs a precompile of " + sPkgName + ". Precompile it in the Studio, then compile again."))
            Send SetResultColor clRed
            If (sErrPath <> "") Begin
                Send SetErrFile sErrPath
                Send FeedDockCompileWarnings sProgram TargetWS
                Send ActivateCombinedCompileOutput of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sLogFile sErrPath
            End
            Else Begin
                Send RefreshCompileOutputLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
            End
            Send SetBusyMarquee False
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Else If (pbStreamOutput(Self) = True) Begin
            Get ExtractIncompatiblePrecompilePackage sCompilerLog to sPkgName
            Get psDeclinedPrecompilePkg to sDeclined
            // The psLastPrecompiledPkg clause: a chained compile that STILL carries the
            // variant-2 warning for the package just precompiled means the rebuild didn't
            // take - re-offering would nag; fall through to the real verdict instead (the
            // compile itself completed, unlike the 4522 bail).
            Get psLastPrecompiledPkg to sLastPre
            If (sPkgName <> "" and Lowercase(sPkgName) <> Lowercase(sDeclined) and Lowercase(sPkgName) <> Lowercase(sLastPre)) Begin
                Get TryStartPrecompile sPkgName TargetWS ;
                    "on disk is not compatible with this compiler, so it is silently recompiled in memory on EVERY compile" to bOK
                If (bOK = True) ;
                    Procedure_Return
                // Declined (or couldn't launch): don't ask again for this
                // package this session, and continue to the normal verdict
                // below - the compile itself ran to completion.
                Set psDeclinedPrecompilePkg to sPkgName
            End
            Move "" to sPkgName
        End

        // Non-zero compiler exit code is authoritative — the compiler
        // bailed early and the .err file may not even exist. Most common
        // cause: a version-mismatched compiler invoked on a workspace it
        // can't load (e.g. DF25's dfcomp.exe on a DF26 JSON .sws). Without
        // this check we'd mistake "compiler refused to run" for "clean
        // compile" because no .err landed in AppSrc.
        If (iExitCode <> 0) Begin
            // Resolve the .err FIRST: a non-zero exit usually means the compiler bailed early with NO .err,
            // but a LINK-ONLY failure (manifest/exe) exits non-zero yet DID compile - it leaves a .err of
            // only LINK ERROR lines + an intact .prn, so headline it amber (ready to Analyze) rather than a
            // red "compiler exited with code N". When there's no .err the headline carries the genuine bail.
            Get ResolveCompiledFile of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "err" to sErrPath
            Move (sErrPath <> "") to bExists
            // In words: "compiler exited with code -1073741571" told nobody that it had crashed.
            If (sCrash <> "") ;
                Move ("Failed: the DataFlex compiler crashed while compiling" * sProgram * ("(" + sCrash + "). Its listing stops where it crashed, so the findings are incomplete.")) to sHeadline
            Else ;
                Move ("Failed: compiler exited with code " * String(iExitCode) + ". " * sProgram * "was not compiled.") to sHeadline
            Get _ShowCompileFailure sProgram TargetWS sLogFile sErrPath sHeadline to bTakenOver
            If (bTakenOver = True) ;
                Procedure_Return
            If (bExists = False) Begin
                // No .err produced - the compiler bailed before the compile
                // proper. (The Error-4522 precompile bail was already handled
                // upstream, before the exit-code branch.) This is the (rarer)
                // transient bail: external state; a genuine compile error
                // would have produced an .err, so a real error never reaches
                // here. Retry ONCE immediately - capped at 1 so a hard
                // failure can't loop.
                Get piCompileRetries to iRetries
                If (pbStreamOutput(Self) = True and iRetries < 1) Begin
                    Set piCompileRetries to (iRetries + 1)
                    Send StartCompile
                    Procedure_Return
                End
                // Already retried (or a legacy compiler): leave the streamed output showing.
                If (pbStreamOutput(Self) = True) ;
                    Send RefreshCompileOutputLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
                /* TELL THE CHECK (Nils 2026-09-23). The compiler exited without compiling, so there
                   is no listing for the analyze - and the Check-done dialog, judging the grid as it
                   stood, called the program clean over this pane's "Failed". Its exit code and its
                   last words go to psChkCompileBail for that dialog to show instead. */
                Move (Replaces(Character(13), sCompilerLog, "")) to sCompilerLog
                Move (StrSplitToArray(sCompilerLog, Character(10))) to asLogLines
                Get DfCliOutputNote (&asLogLines) 6 to sBail
                Set psChkCompileBail of ghoApplication to ("Exit code " + String(iExitCode) + "." + (If((sBail = ""), " It wrote nothing.", sBail)))
            End
            Send SetBusyMarquee False
            /* THE ENDING A COMPILE WITH ERRORS ACTUALLY TAKES - and the one this was missing when
               it first shipped (Nils 2026-08-07: "the compiler generated errors, and no Analyzer
               action was started"). A compiler that reports errors exits NON-ZERO, so it lands in
               this branch and returns here, never reaching the tail at the foot of the procedure
               where the first two calls were placed. The retry above returns earlier, so by this
               point the compile really is over. */
            Send MaybeAutoAnalyzeAfterGuidedCheck
            Procedure_Return
        End

        Get CheckCompileSucceeded of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS (&sErrorText) to bOK
        If (bOK = True) Begin
            Send SetResultColor clGreen
            // Clean build: no .err, but the listing may still carry warnings to modernize.
            // Resolve the fresh .prn, count its warnings, arm Analyze (psPrnFilePath) when any
            // exist, and headline the count so a warnings-only build prompts a click on Analyze.
            Move 0 to iWarnCount
            Get ResolvePrnFile sProgram TargetWS to sPrnPath
            If (sPrnPath <> "") Begin
                // Fast path: the live tail already harvested + counted the diagnostics (-1 = no cache).
                Get CachedPrnMessageCount sPrnPath to iWarnCount
                If (iWarnCount < 0) Begin
                    Get ReadCompilerMessages sPrnPath to aPrnMsgs
                    Move (SizeOfArray(aPrnMsgs)) to iWarnCount
                End
                /* A CLEAN BUILD IS ANALYZED TOO (Nils 2026-09-11). This armed Analyze only when the
                   listing held warnings, so a truly clean build left no path at all - and StartAnalyze
                   then took its "nothing to analyze" exit, which re-renders whatever the grid already
                   holds. After a plan run that is the PREVIOUS analysis: a workspace whose run had just
                   converted 89 grids showed every one of them still waiting on the board, because its
                   first clean build never replaced them. The analyze is also where the Deep Scan lane
                   merges in, so the scan's fresh findings were dropped with them. A clean listing has
                   no diagnostics to read, so it analyzes at once - and gives the true board. */
                Set psPrnFilePath to sPrnPath
            End
            // Resolve the exe the compiler just wrote (Programs\<base><suffix>.exe, cfg-suffix by existence) -
            // it feeds the Studio-style success footer's "----Executable written: ..." line in both success
            // shapes. "" (unexpectedly not found) renders the "Finished" line alone. (Nils 2026-07-08.)
            Get ResolveProgramExe of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS to sExePath
            If (iWarnCount > 0) Begin
                // A SUCCESSFUL build that produced warnings: feed the compiler chip and KEEP the output
                // visible showing them. The old code closed the dock + returned, so the chips stayed at
                // their compile-start 0 and the warnings only reappeared when the user reopened the Output
                // tab (they live in the .prn cache). Set the chip directly (the .prn tail may still be
                // finalizing a big listing, so ActivateCompileWarningsOutput's own LoadCombined count can
                // lag by one refresh - it re-sets the chip to the same N once the cache lands). Nils 2026-07-07.
                Send SetResultText ("Success: " * sProgram * ("compiled - " + String(iWarnCount) + " warnings."))
                Set piCompileWarnings of ghoCompilerOutputDock to iWarnCount
                Send ActivateCompileWarningsOutput of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sLogFile sPrnPath sExePath
            End
            Else Begin
                // Truly clean - KEEP the output dock open showing the compile log capped by the Studio
                // success footer ("Compile Finished" + "Executable written") instead of an empty grid
                // (Nils 2026-07-08; before that the dock CLOSED here). Chips stay hidden - the green
                // verdict text carries the message.
                Send SetResultText ("Success: " * sProgram * "compiled cleanly.")
                Send ActivateCompileSuccessOutput of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) sLogFile sExePath
            End
            Send SetBusyMarquee False
            // The compile really finished (clean, or clean-with-warnings) - see the note there.
            Send MaybeAutoAnalyzeAfterGuidedCheck
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // Failure path: show CompilerOutput.dg's error dock for the .err (if one exists).
        // Resolve the .err the compiler actually wrote (cfg suffix, either bitness) FIRST, so we can tell a
        // genuine compile failure from a LINK-ONLY failure (manifest/exe) before headlining the result.
        Get ResolveCompiledFile of ghoApplication sProgram TargetWS "err" to sErrPath
        Get _ShowCompileFailure sProgram TargetWS sLogFile sErrPath ("Failed: " * sProgram * "did not compile.") to bTakenOver
        If (bTakenOver = True) ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (sErrPath = "") Begin
            If (pbStreamOutput(Self) = True) ;
                Send RefreshCompileOutputLive of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
        End
        Send SetBusyMarquee False
        // The compile really finished, badly - and the errors are exactly what a guided Check wants
        // in its "Breaks the build" band, so this ending auto-analyzes too. See the note there.
        Send MaybeAutoAnalyzeAfterGuidedCheck
    End_Procedure
    
    // Prime the panel before it is shown: refill the program list and pre-select the
    // Prime the panel before it is shown. This only ever seeded the program combo; with the combo gone
    // (program selection lives in the Check Workspace view) there is nothing to prime, so it is now a
    // no-op - kept because ShowTestCompilePanel / OnUpdate / the deferred-show timer still call it.
    Procedure PrepareForShow
    End_Procedure

    // The cRDCCJToolPanel base sends OnUpdate during its show/activate cycle, AFTER the panel
    // and its OCX children are realized - the right moment to prime it (CompilerOutput loads
    // its grid here). A cRDCCJToolPanel subclass MUST define OnUpdate; without it the base's
    // "Send OnUpdate" has no handler and faults (that was the runtime error).
    Procedure OnUpdate
        Send PrepareForShow
        // Re-render the web action buttons whenever the dock (re)activates. The post-compile
        // SyncWebButtonStates (oCompilePoll_tm) runs while the live-output dock has focus, so its
        // WebSets set server values that never paint on this then-inactive host - they need re-pushing
        // once THIS dock is active again (e.g. the user returns to it to click Analyze). Skip the FIRST
        // OnUpdate (initial realization: the design-time pbEnabled is already correct, and a WebSet that
        // early faults 4402 - see PrepareForShow); on every later activation defer one tick (host now
        // active + web-ready) then sync.
        If (pbFirstUpdateDone(Self) = False) Begin
            Set pbFirstUpdateDone to True
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Send Arm of oSyncDeferTimer
    End_Procedure

    // Hide the dock (the Close button + Esc). Forward to the cRDCCJToolPanel base, and take the
    // piggybacked CompilerOutput error dock down with it - closing Test Compile hides its errors.
    Procedure Close_Panel
        Forward Send Close_Panel
        // CloseCompileErrorDialog now closes the whole merged bottom dock (both the Compiler Output and
        // Modernization Analyzer tabs), so a separate analyzer-hide is no longer needed.
        Send CloseCompileErrorDialog of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
    End_Procedure

    // A genuine USER close (the Close button defer, Esc, Alt+L): hide the dock AND clear the user-wants flag, so
    // the editor view's Activate restore-on-return does NOT bring it back. The view-switch auto-hide path
    // (CloseAllTestCompileDocks -> CloseTestCompileDialog -> Close_Panel) deliberately does NOT route through
    // here, so it leaves the flag set and the dock returns when you switch back to the editor.
    Procedure ModernizeCloseByUser
        Set pbModernizePanelWanted of ghoApplication to False
        Send Close_Panel
    End_Procedure

    On_Key Key_F5        Send DoCompileToggle
    On_Key Key_Alt+Key_L Send ModernizeCloseByUser
    On_Key Key_Escape    Send ModernizeCloseByUser
End_Object

// Show + dock the Modernize panel WITHOUT switching views (the editor view is already, or is being made,
// active). Used by the editor's toggle (Ctrl+M / button), by EditorViewFT's Activate restore-on-return, and by
// ShowTestCompilePanel below. IDEMPOTENT: a no-op when the dock is already visible - which also guards the
// re-entrancy where "Send Activate of ho" re-fires the editor view's Activate (-> this proc again). Sets the
// user-wants flag and re-parks phoTestCompileFT (re-arming the non-editor views' one-shot view-switch hide).
//     Send ShowTestCompileDockOnly of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
/* THE TEST COMPILE BAR DOCKS FIRST - BEFORE THE OUTPUT TABS EVER DO (Nils 2026-09-20: "the Output,
   Analyzer and Manual Changes tabs are positioned on top of the TestCompile dialog with all the
   chips/buttons"; the same inversion as 2026-07-29). CodeJock stacks bottom bars in the order they
   FIRST DOCK, and a bar claims its slot when it docks, not when it shows - so whichever path showed
   the merged output dock before this bar had ever docked put the tabs above the bar for the rest of
   the session, and hiding the dock again cannot undo it. This is the other half of that rule, used
   FOR us: the output dock calls this before its own first dock, and the bar docks here - shown, then
   hidden in the same message, so nothing paints in between - and holds the top slot whatever order
   the clicks arrive in. A no-op once the bar has docked (hidden or shown): phoCJDialogBar is set by
   Create_ToolPanel and by nothing else. Activate is the only way a cRDCCJToolPanel docks (Add_Focus ->
   Update -> Create_ToolPanel); it also runs the panel's first OnUpdate, which primes it and no more.
   ShowTestCompileDockOnly below still works after this: its early-out is on pbVisible, which is
   False again, and its Set pbVisible + Activate then simply show the already-docked bar. */
Procedure EnsureTestCompileBarDocked
    Handle ho
    Boolean bExiting

    Get pbAppExiting of ghoApplication to bExiting
    If (bExiting = True) ;
        Procedure_Return
    Move (oTestCompile_dg(Self)) to ho
    If (phoCJDialogBar(ho) <> 0) ;
        Procedure_Return
    Send Activate of ho
    Set pbVisible of ho to False
End_Procedure

Procedure ShowTestCompileDockOnly
    Handle ho
    Boolean bHeld bExiting

    // NEVER (re)show the dock once program exit is committed: during teardown the CJ MDI activates
    // the next view (the editor), whose Activate restore-on-return lands here while the CodeJock COM
    // objects are being destroyed -> error 4399 "pvComObject is NULL" in ComEnableGroups (exit with
    // the Modernize pane open, Nils 2026-07-03). Single choke point - the toggle, the restore, the
    // dashboard's deferred timer and ShowTestCompilePanel all route through this procedure.
    Get pbAppExiting of ghoApplication to bExiting
    If (bExiting = True) ;
        Procedure_Return
    Move (oTestCompile_dg(Self)) to ho
    // Record intent FIRST (so "dock visible" always implies "wanted"), THEN bail if it is already up.
    Set pbModernizePanelWanted of ghoApplication to True
    If (pbVisible(ho) = True) ;
        Procedure_Return
    // Show + dock the (non-modal) panel. The base then fires OnUpdate (after the OCX children are realized),
    // which primes the panel via PrepareForShow - so we do NOT prime here (priming before the panel was
    // realized was the runtime error).
    Set phoTestCompileFT of ghoApplication to ho
    Set pbVisible of ho to True
    Send Activate of ho
    // Always bring the merged output/analyzer dock up WITH the bar so the grids are on screen (Nils 2026-07-08).
    // Hiding kept its content (Nils 2026-09-30: Close and Hide/Show Compile pane "should just hide/unhide it -
    // not clear the data. That is what the Clear button is for"), so it comes back as it was, on the tab it was
    // on. It comes up EMPTY on the Compiler Output tab only when it holds nothing - the first showing, or after
    // Clear - or holds another workspace's results.
    Get CompileOutputHeld of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) to bHeld
    If (bHeld = True) ;
        Send ReshowCompileOutput of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
    Else ;
        Send ShowEmptyCompileOutput of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
End_Procedure

// *** Public entry message - replaces the old modal "Send Popup of (oTestCompile_dg(...))" ***
// Bring the non-modal, dockable Test Compile panel up: switch to the Scintilla editor view FIRST (the dock
// coexists with it, and an error->source jump must land in a paged-in editor OCX; doing it here, not at the
// call site, keeps the trigger view-independent), then show/dock it. Send to the command-bar client area:
//     Send ShowTestCompilePanel of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
Procedure ShowTestCompilePanel
    Send Activate_oEditorView_vw of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
    Send ShowTestCompileDockOnly
End_Procedure

// Public access method close the Test compile panel.
Procedure CloseTestCompileDialog
    Handle ho
    Move (oTestCompile_dg(Self)) to ho
    Set pbVisible    of ho to False
    Send Close_Panel of ho
End_Procedure 

Function IsTestCompileDialogVisible Returns Boolean
    Handle ho
    Move (oTestCompile_dg(Self)) to ho
    Function_Return (pbVisible(ho))
End_Function

// Toggle which TAB of the merged bottom dock is shown: Compiler Output <-> Modernization
// Analyzer. Wired to the shared dock context menu (cCompilerOutputContextMenu's
// cToggleDockMenuItem) AND mirrored by the dock's own bottom tab strip, so the user can flip
// back to the last compile's output WITHOUT recompiling, and to the analysis WITHOUT re-analyzing.
//
// ShowTab does the instant switch (one area shown, the other hidden) - both areas keep their
// content by design (the .err/log persist; the grids are reused), so no re-parse. A tab whose
// area has never held content this session is guarded below (explain, don't flash an empty pane).
Procedure ToggleCompileDocks
    Boolean bAnalyzerActive
    String sErrFile sRawLog
    Integer eTab

    If (ghoOutputDock = 0) ;
        Procedure_Return
    Get peActiveTab of ghoOutputDock to eTab
    Move (eTab = eTabAnalyzer) to bAnalyzerActive
    If (bAnalyzerActive = True) Begin
        // -> Compiler Output, IF it holds anything (both its source properties
        // blank = no compile has shown it this session).
        Get psErrorFile  of ghoCompilerOutputDock to sErrFile
        Get psRawLogFile of ghoCompilerOutputDock to sRawLog
        If (sErrFile = "" and sRawLog = "") Begin
            Send FTInfoBox "There is no compiler output to show yet - compile first."
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Send ShowTab of ghoOutputDock eTabCompiler
    End
    Else Begin
        // -> Modernization Analyzer, IF an analysis has been loaded (psErrorFile is
        // set by ActivateModAnalyzer and cleared by CloseModAnalyzer).
        Get psErrorFile of ghoModAnalyzerDock to sErrFile
        If (sErrFile = "") Begin
            Send FTInfoBox "No analysis is loaded yet - compile, then press Analyze first."
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Send ShowTab of ghoOutputDock eTabAnalyzer
    End
End_Procedure

// Close ALL THREE bottom docks (Test Compile controller + Compiler Output + ModAnalyzer) in one call.
// Every NON-editor view's Activate_View routes here, so switching to the Dashboard / Select Functions /
// Select Folders never leaves a dock hanging with a previous workspace's result. Each step is guarded -
// a safe no-op when that dock isn't open. The two output docks are closed explicitly (they can be shown
// via the dock toggle), and CloseTestCompileDialog then cascades (its Close_Panel re-closes them) so the
// controller's own teardown is covered too. The editor is the ONE view that must NOT call this -
// ShowTestCompilePanel activates the editor to OPEN the docks.
Procedure CloseAllTestCompileDocks
    Boolean bVisible
    If (ghoCompilerOutputDock <> 0) ;
        Send CloseCompileErrorDialog of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
    // CloseCompileErrorDialog above already closed the merged bottom dock (both tabs). The analyzer's loaded
    // analysis is KEPT regardless: psErrorFile (the "analysis loaded" marker ToggleCompileDocks gates on) AND
    // the parsed grid rows live on the persistent analyzer-area object, so returning to the editor and
    // selecting the Analyzer tab shows it again instead of "No analysis is loaded yet". Only Clear / a fresh
    // Analyze forgets it (Clear still calls CloseModAnalyzer, which wipes the marker).
    Get IsTestCompileDialogVisible of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars)) to bVisible
    If (bVisible = True) ;
        Send CloseTestCompileDialog of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
End_Procedure

// Dashboard "Run Modernization Plan": open ONLY the Refactoring Plan dialog - NO ModAnalyzer triage grid and
// NO Test Compile dock. Force the saved-plan branch (set the analyzer grid's pbSavedView), then reuse
// StartAutoSelect on the dock OBJECT directly (oTestCompile_dg, resolved without showing/parking it - so
// phoTestCompileFT is not required). StartAutoSelect's saved branch builds the plan from the saved-plan JSON
// (ReadSavedPlan + PlanFromSavedRows - it does NOT read the grid, only checks pbSavedView), opens ShowPlan for
// review, and on Proceed runs the engine (eRunPlan). Send to the command-bar client area:
//     Send StartSavedPlanReview of (Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))
Procedure StartSavedPlanReview
    If (ghoModAnalyzerDock <> 0) ;
        Set pbSavedView of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to True
    // The "Loading Refactoring Plan..." status-bar note + PumpMsgQueue flush now live inside StartAutoSelect,
    // so both this dashboard entry and the dock's own "Refactor Plan" action show the same wait message.
    Send StartAutoSelect of (oTestCompile_dg(Self))
End_Procedure
